window.PULSE_RECHERCHE_EN = [{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"What ships with it Catalogue The styles, scenes, effects, characters and formats that come with Pulse Motion. You don’t need to know them by heart: Pulse Motion suggests the ones that suit your video. This page helps you know what to ask for. Entrances, transitions and camera moves have their own gallery: the moves ."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles","t":"Catalogue","s":"The twenty styles","x":"A style is the look of the video: its shapes, its fonts, the way it moves. Your brand kit (logo, colours, fonts) is applied on top. To switch style along the way: « passe en style éditorial » (“switch to the editorial style”). To create your own: /pulse-motion:style . calm · readable · reassuring clair-pedagogique Light background, titles in the primary colour, highlights in the accent colour. For explaining: training, presentations. Figtree and Inter fonts. Light or dark tone. welcoming · soft · human chaleureux Warm tones (terracotta, sage, peach), round shapes, pebbles that breathe. For a public service, a charity, healthcare. Fredoka and Nunito Sans fonts. Light tone. elegant · demanding · calm editorial Typography carries the video, like a magazine: fine rules that draw themselves, very large titles, slow movements. For storytelling. Fraunces and Instrument Sans fonts. Light (cream) or dark tone. bold · fast · punchy energique Colour everywhere, flat blocks, giant capital-letter titles that drop and bounce. For social media, a launch. Anton and Archivo fonts. Light (yellow) or dark tone. clean · confident · essential minimal Swiss grid, lots of space, oversized typography, a single accent detail per scene. For a product, results, a decision. Schibsted Grotesk font. Light or dark tone. precise · modern · technical tech-sombre Dark background, a sliding grid, text typed out on the keyboard with its cursor. For a software demo, data, a technical tutorial. Space Grotesk and JetBrains Mono fonts. Dark or light tone. direct · readable · factual info-sans-son News you can read with the sound off: a giant caption that snaps in two or three pieces, a single keyword on an accent block, figures that roll up with their source. For social media, internal communication, a key figure. Barlow Condensed and Barlow fonts. Dark tone. precise · methodical · technical plan-technique A technical drawing that traces itself on cyanotype paper: white lines on blue, title block, dimensions, hatching, revision cloud, a single part in the accent colour. For industry, maintenance, technical training. B612 and B612 Mono fonts. Dark or light tone. premium · refined · bright lancement The launch film where the interface is the star: near-black background, soft light, cards that snap together, a giant number that freezes. For launching a product, showcasing software. Sora and Manrope fonts. Dark tone. The styles in motion Each style plays the same little story, “the cold chain”: two still frames (A, the title; B, the key figure), then its nine ready-made scenes in motion. The styles are grouped by family; open one to see it move, with its personality, its uses, what it is not, and where it comes from. On your computer, « montre-moi les autres styles » (“show me the other styles”) opens the same story, played live, so you can choose by watching."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-essentiels","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"The essentials The chaleureux style · welcoming, gentle, human For: Welcoming newcomers · Talking about health or prevention · Presenting a charity or association What it is not: Not a childish or cartoon style: soft shapes, with no character in the spotlight. No sharp corners, no garish colours. Signature: Organic shapes that breathe, cards with generous rounded corners, gentle motion that stops cleanly Do: A direct, caring tone · Airy cards Avoid: Sharp corners · Garish colours Origin: an original Pulse Motion style The clair-pedagogique style · composed, legible, reassuring For: Training a team · Explaining a procedure · Presenting a product What it is not: Not a terminal or magazine style: no grid, no monospace, no serifs. No pebbles or soft cards like chaleureux. Signature: Large colour circles drifting in the background, text that slides in Do: A single message per scene · Leave at least 1.5 s of reading time after the last entrance Avoid: More than 4 points in a list · Text beyond the maximum lengths Origin: an original Pulse Motion style The editorial style · elegant, demanding, calm For: Telling a story, a testimonial · Carrying an institutional message · Training at an unhurried pace What it is not: Not a plain slideshow: typography leads, very large. No flat colour, no bounce, nothing rushed. Signature: Thin rules that draw themselves, very large serif headings, text rising from behind a mask Do: Let it breathe: wide margins, few elements · A single bright colour per scene Avoid: Bounces and fast motion · Solid blocks of flat colour Origin: an original Pulse Motion style The energique style · bold, fast, punchy For: Announcing a launch · Posting on social media · Launching a promotion What it is not: Not a composed style: it shouts. No long text, no subtle gradients, no slowness. Signature: Solid flat colours, giant capital headings that drop and bounce, moving bands Do: Very short headings · One idea per screen Avoid: Long paragraphs · More than three colours Origin: an original Pulse Motion style The minimal style · crisp, confident, essential For: Presenting a product · Showing results · Announcing a decision What it is not: Not an empty or cold style: the grid and oversized typography carry everything. No busy backdrop, no gradient, no more than one accent. Signature: Swiss grid, lots of space, oversized typography, a single accent, short crisp motion Do: Very short headings · A single accent colour per scene Avoid: Decoration · Several colours Origin: lemo-opuscar, “swiss-motion” style (MIT) The tech-sombre style · precise, modern, technical For: Demonstrating software · Presenting data · Making a technical tutorial What it is not: Not a science-fiction screen: no garish neon, no glow everywhere. Not a warm style like chaleureux: monospace, sharp corners, a single accent. Signature: Sliding background grid, text that types itself out with a cursor, monospace figures, glowing accent Do: Short, factual sentences · A single glowing accent per scene Avoid: Bounces · Bright warm colours Origin: an original Pulse Motion style"},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-dessine","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"Hand-drawn The carnet style · spontaneous, warm, curious For: Telling an idea or an approach · Summing up a workshop · Explaining with sketches What it is not: Not a whiteboard: paper, no floating markers. Not a wax-crayon drawing: ink, with no wax or wash. Not chaleureux: drawn lines, not rounded cards. Signature: Felt-tip sketches on cream paper: lines that wobble slightly and never quite close, hand hatching, a single line circling what matters, notes in the margin Do: Let the video draw itself, one stroke after another · A single line for what matters Avoid: Making text meant to be read wobble · Roughness that makes 9:16 illegible Origin: explainroo, “paper” style (MIT) · take-one, “sketchbook” style (MIT) · lemo-opuscar, “one-line” style (MIT) The crayon style · tender, handcrafted, patient For: Raising public awareness · Telling a simple story · Explaining to children or families What it is not: Not a sketchbook: wax and wash, not ink or hatching. Not a board: a picture-book page, on grainy paper. Signature: Picture-book page in wax crayon on grainy paper: the wax only catches on the paper's raised grain, a translucent wash passes beneath and reveals, the lines quiver gently Do: Let the page draw itself, one wax stroke after another · A wash to reveal what matters, not everywhere Avoid: Making text meant to be read wobble · More than three crayons in the same scene Origin: lemo-opuscar, “crayon-book” style (MIT) · take-one, “crayon-storybook” style (MIT) The tableau-blanc style · clear, composed, educational For: Explaining a concept step by step · Training in the room or remotely · Drawing a process on the board What it is not: Not a technical drawing: drawn by hand, not with a ruler. Not a crayon sketchbook: marker on a glossy board. Not kinetic typography: real marker, written in order. Signature: Marker on a glossy whiteboard: text writes itself in order, the ink darkens where two strokes cross, a marker floats with its shadow then parks off-screen, magnets hold the points Do: Write every text in marker (data-lettres=\"ecrit\") · Keep each marker's meaning for the whole video Avoid: A cut on the board (prefer a whip pan or a slide) · More than three markers, or the accent everywhere Origin: lemo-opuscar, “whiteboard” style (MIT) The tableau-noir style · calm, schoolroom, attentive For: Giving a lesson · Revising a concept · Telling a method step by step What it is not: Not a whiteboard: no gloss, no markers, no magnets, no ghosting. The camera stays face on, in front of the board. Signature: Chalk on a matt green slate: text writes itself with pressure, the board's grain bites into the chalk, a little dust as each stroke lifts, window light gliding across Do: Few words, large letters, written in chalk · Keep the board alive (smudging, window light) Avoid: Accent chalk everywhere · More than one erasing per video Origin: lemo-opuscar, “chalkboard” style (MIT) · explainroo, “chalk” style (MIT)"},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-schemas-donnees","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"Diagrams and data The iso-album style · calm, caring, precise For: Explaining a safety instruction · Presenting a welcome procedure · Showing the right order to go through What it is not: Not a gag cartoon: small faceless characters, seen from behind. Not iso-infographie: an inhabited place and an everyday rule, not a system to take apart. Signature: An everyday place in true flat-colour isometric view, small characters seen from behind doing the thing in order, and the rule written on the ground, letter by letter Do: Act out the rule in order, one step at a time · Place the text on the ground, near what it describes Avoid: Faces, expressions, gags · Several rules in the same place Origin: lemo-opuscar, “isometric-picture-book” style (MIT) The iso-infographie style · clear, structured, ingenious For: Explaining a system or a circuit · Showing a site, a warehouse, a factory · Showing the inside of a machine What it is not: Not low-poly 3D: flat three-tone blocks, with no lighting or perspective. Not a flat diagram nor plan-technique: volumes, not lines. Signature: An isometric diorama of flat three-tone blocks that builds itself, thin labels along the way, numbers in Isotype; things open up to show their insides Do: One colour per type of object, the accent for the sole subject · Build the system block by block, in the order it works Avoid: Cast shadows, reflections, perspective · Too many objects: when the camera pulls back, they become illegible Origin: lemo-opuscar, “iso-infographic” style (MIT) The pictogrammes style · clear, rhythmic, universal For: Giving an instruction or a user guide · Presenting a public or internal service · Showing a quantity in silhouettes What it is not: Not an isometric infographic: everything is flat, face on. Not interface icons: signs, a grid and beats. Signature: Signage that comes alive on the beat: geometric pictograms placed one per beat, a pattern grid of circles, half-discs and cells whose rows slide one cell per beat, signage typography Do: Place each thing on a beat · One pictogram per idea, never an illustration Avoid: Detailed or multicoloured pictograms · A move that falls between two beats Origin: lemo-opuscar, “pictogram-motion” style (MIT) The plan-technique style · precise, methodical, technical For: Explaining a machine or a part · Training for maintenance · Presenting an industrial process What it is not: Not tech-sombre: no interface, no keyboard, no typed text. Not a whiteboard: ruled lines, no hand. Not an isometric infographic: lines, not colour blocks. Signature: White line on Prussian blue, drawn in the draughtsman's order, fine grid, title block, arrowed dimensions, section hatching, a single part in the accent Do: Draw in order: frame, outlines, details, dimensions · A single part in the accent per scene Avoid: Bounces and lively entrances · Too many lines drawn at the same time Origin: lemo-opuscar, “blueprint” style (MIT) · explainroo, “blueprint” style (MIT) The recit-donnees style · rigorous, clear, narrative For: Telling a story with figures · Comparing results · Tracking a trend What it is not: Not a dashboard: one idea at a time. Not a presentation with charts: the chart is the story itself. Signature: The chart is the film: cream dot-grid paper, axes that grow with the story, one value in the accent, handwritten notes pinned up, the tip of a red-and-blue pencil, an empty dotted box for what we don't know Do: One idea per chart, real data with its source · Make every move an operation on the chart Avoid: Extrapolating or inventing a value · An axis that doesn't start at zero without saying so Origin: lemo-opuscar, “dataviz” style (MIT)"},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-papier-imprime","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"Paper and print The papier-decoupe style · handcrafted, cheerful, layered For: Presenting an offer or a service · Welcoming and introducing a team · Telling a project in stages What it is not: Not a flat style: depth comes from the shadows between layers. Not traditional red-and-gold paper cutting: papers from the brand kit, set out like a mock-up. Signature: Cut papers laid in layers, with slightly irregular edges: real cast shadows between the layers, a sheet that lifts, a flap that rises to reveal the layer beneath Do: Place each text on a sheet, and let the shadow convey the depth · Only one sheet lifted at a time: the one that matters Avoid: Hard black shadows · Stacking more than four layers Origin: take-one, “paper-cutout” style (MIT) · lemo-opuscar, “papercut-red” style (MIT) The riso style · vivid, independent, graphic For: Announcing an event · Posting on social media · Giving a brand character What it is not: Not a flat style: the inks overlap and blend. Not an energetic poster: little motion, inks that settle, no dropping headings. Signature: Two or three translucent inks overprinted on warm paper: a halftone dot screen, ink grain, plates never quite in register; text in the darkest ink Do: Add one ink at each step, never more than three · Keep the text in the darkest ink Avoid: Text on solid accent ink · A plate misregistration that changes on every frame Origin: lemo-opuscar, “risograph” style (MIT) · take-one, “riso-pop” style (MIT)"},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-ecrans-annonces","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"Screens and announcements The info-sans-son style · direct, legible, factual For: Posting news on social media · Making an internal announcement · Making a key figure speak What it is not: Not energique: the captions snap into place, without dropping or bouncing. Not a TV news bulletin: no permanent ticker, no presenter. Signature: Giant caption that snaps in two or three pieces, a single keyword on an accent block, rolling figures with their source, dark ruled sheet with a double red margin Do: One idea per caption, readable with the sound off · A source under every figure Avoid: More than one keyword per caption · A loss shown by colour alone Origin: lemo-opuscar, “caption-news” style (MIT) The lancement style · premium, clean, luminous For: Launching a product or an offer · Presenting software · Announcing something new What it is not: Not tech-sombre: no terminal, no command prompt. Not minimal: dark, with light and depth. Signature: Near-black background with a fine grid, soft moving light, interface cards that snap into place, a reflection sweeping across the heading, a giant thin-line number, a single accent Do: One action per beat, the interface in the spotlight · A single coloured element per scene Avoid: Grey too dark to be read · Too many elements moving at once Origin: lemo-opuscar, “dark-keynote” style (MIT) · take-one, “monochrome-launch, dark-premium” style (MIT)"},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"styles-anime","t":"Catalogue","s":"","x":"Animated The mode-d-emploi style · calm, precise, vintage For: Getting-started guide for a product · Step-by-step procedure for a workstation · Online help and how-to tutorial What it is not: Not a warm style with breathing shapes like chaleureux: period flat colours, limited animation. Not a gag cartoon: a calm narrator who shows the gesture. Signature: 1950s educational film: printed flat colours a few pixels off their broken outline, limited frame-by-frame animation, a guide with a still body whose hands alone do the work, numbered steps Do: One step per scene, its number first, then what the hand does · Colour a part in at the moment it is named Avoid: Moving the guide's whole body while it explains · Smooth, bouncy motion: everything moves frame by frame Origin: lemo-opuscar, “midcentury-toon” style (MIT) Which style for which genre? Genre Suggested first The contrast suggested third Training clair-pedagogique, chaleureux minimal Product presentation minimal, tech-sombre energique Storytelling editorial, chaleureux tech-sombre Social media post energique, minimal editorial Your personal styles are added to this list: a personal style made for that genre is suggested right after the first two, instead of the contrasting style. Only styles checked since their last change are suggested."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"scenes","t":"Catalogue","s":"The ready-made scenes","x":"Every style ships the same nine scenes, each drawn in its own way. Each one has texts to fill in, with a maximum length : beyond it, the text overflows or the scene becomes too short to be read. Scene Role Texts (maximum length, in characters) titre Open the video or a chapter label (25), title (40), subtitle (50) liste 2 to 4 points revealed one by one title (40), 2 to 4 points (45 each) chiffre A key figure that counts up to its value title (40), unit (3), caption (60) comparaison Two columns: before / after, do / avoid title (40), two column headings (20), two texts (60) etape A numbered step in a procedure number (2), title (40), text (80) alerte A point to watch, a mistake to avoid title (40), text (80) schema Three steps linked by arrows title (40), three labels (25) moyeu One idea in the centre, 3 to 5 parts around it, linked by arrows title (40), centre (24), 3 to 5 parts (22 each) conclusion The key message and the logo title (40), subtitle (50) The energique style asks for shorter texts: a 28-character title, 32-character points. In vertical format, columns, steps and diagrams stack up; the hub’s parts move into a row beneath the centre."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"effets","t":"Catalogue","s":"The particle effects","x":"An effect serves a purpose : highlighting a figure, celebrating a conclusion, setting a mood. Never for decoration. Pulse Motion decides by itself which scenes get one: at most two effects on screen at the same time, and one or two in a whole short training video. 34 built-in effects, each shown exactly as the engine draws it. air The scene's air: voluminous smoke rising from the bottom, curling and flowing around the declared obstacles; acts as wind for coupled effects (data-milieu). assembler Particles from all over the screen assemble into the target element (data-ancre), from left to right; it appears when they arrive (at the end of data-dur, or earlier with \"arrivee\"). banc A two-tone school of fish swimming together around the anchor (linked swarms); back layer, for the sea, the team, the collective. banniere A banner made from the anchor (heading, logo, image): the element becomes a cloth held by its top edge, flapping in the wind, then breaks loose and falls onto the declared obstacles (data-obstacle); the real element is hidden meanwhile. braises Glowing embers rising slowly as they swirl; derived from the sparks (same settings). braises-portees Embers carried by the scene's air (data-milieu): they follow the plume and swirl around the obstacles; derived from the embers. brasier Blaze: fire coloured by its temperature (from dark red to white, black body), licking the obstacles and glowing (halo), topped with smoke. briser The target element (data-ancre) shatters into shards flying from the point of impact, spinning and falling; they bounce off the scene's declared obstacles. confettis A burst of confetti in the brand kit colours that flutters down; ideal for a conclusion. corde A rope hanging from the middle of the zone's top edge (the anchor, or the marker), dangling and swaying; its length is the width of the zone. desintegrer The target element (data-ancre) crumbles into dust in its own colours, from left to right during data-dur, carried off by the wind with a glow before fading out. drapeau A flag in the brand kit colours (three vertical stripes) flapping in the wind, held by its left edge at the anchor's left edge (the pole); the anchor is the flag's resting place; lit folds. eau Water pouring from the anchor, flowing over the declared obstacles and forming a smooth pool with reflections (fluid); declare a floor or a tank (data-obstacle). eau-encre Ink in water: two inks poured at the top of the zone, one slightly lower than the other, flowing in swirls, meeting and blending by absorption; on a light background. energie A stream of energy swirling around the zone like plasma, in glowing trails. etincelles A spray of hot sparks bursting from the zone then falling back, with small embers as they die. explosion Explosion: a blinding flash, a spray of glowing debris and a cloud of smoke that clears. feu Bright flames licking the zone as they ripple, with a few escaping sparks. feu-artifice Rockets rising with a trail, then bursting into colourful sprays that twinkle as they fall. filet A net stretched between the anchor's left and right edges, catching whatever falls into it (an animated element declared data-obstacle, particles linked with \"avec\") and giving way beyond its strength; only the mesh is drawn. fumee Wisps of soft smoke that rise, widen and clear; to place on a back layer. fusee-panache Rockets rising and leaving a plume of hot smoke in the scene's air (data-milieu): it widens, curls and rises. guirlande A string of light bulbs on a wire hanging from one side of the zone to the other (the anchor, or the marker placed from one element to another), swayed by a breeze; bulb halo. lucioles Fireflies floating in small loose groups around the anchor, lighting up then going out (slow swarm, halo); back layer, night or magic. metamorphose The target element (data-ancre) comes apart into particles that reassemble into another element (data-cible), from the centre outwards; the colours shift from one to the other on the way. miel A thick, slow liquid (honey, syrup, lava) flowing from the anchor and spreading over the declared obstacles; derived from the water, more viscous and denser to the eye. neige Snowflakes falling gently and swaying from the top of the frame (full-frame zone); they settle and build up on the scene's obstacles. neige-lourde Heavy, sticky snow falling from the top of the frame and forming real heaps on the obstacles (cohesive grains); back layer, more demanding than the snow. nuee A flock of birds wheeling around the anchor while keeping their distance (swarm); back layer, over a clear area of the scene. paillettes Magic glitter that twinkles and rises gently around the zone (highlighting, reveal). pluie Fine, fast rain, slightly slanted by the wind, from the top of the frame (full-frame zone); splashes on contact with the scene's obstacles. poussiere Fine dust in suspension, floating slowly in the light; background atmosphere, on a back layer. rideau A curtain covering the anchor, split down the middle and held by its top edge in two halves that draw apart: it opens all the way down, its panels hang (or tear, and their pieces fall); place the anchor on what it hides, data-start at the moment of the reveal. sable Sand pouring from the anchor and forming a real heap, which collapses if its support moves (grains); declare a floor or an obstacle beneath (data-obstacle). Adjusting an effect with a sentence You say What changes « plus d'étincelles » (“more sparkles”), « moins de confettis » (“less confetti”) The number of particles, doubled or halved. « plus lent » (“slower”), « plus vif » (“livelier”) The speed. « en bleu » (“in blue”), « aux couleurs de la marque » (“in the brand colours”) The colour: a specific shade, or those of your brand kit. « fais-les partir autrement » (“make them go off differently”) Another random draw, same effect. « derrière le texte » (“behind the text”) The effect moves behind the content. « plus tard » (“later”), « plus longtemps » (“for longer”) When the effect happens and how long it lasts. « enlève les confettis » (“remove the confetti”), « ajoute de la neige sur l'intro » (“add snow on the intro”) The effect is removed or added. Tip: on a light background, snow and dust, white by default, become invisible. Pulse Motion switches them to your brand kit colour or to a blue-grey; if you can’t see them, ask « neige plus visible » (“make the snow more visible”). No effect fits? Pulse Motion can create one for your video, preferably starting from an existing effect (« des étincelles bleues et plus lentes, pour un sujet sur le froid » (“blue, slower sparkles, for a topic about the cold”))."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"personnages","t":"Catalogue","s":"The characters","x":"A character is animated on a shared skeleton: the stick figures, a drawing made on request ( /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage ) or your own cut-out illustration ( /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage ) all take the same poses and make the same moves. The details are on the characters page (in French). What ships with it The details One drawing touffu : the little figure with white sticks, big eyes and a green tuft, who serves as the model for new drawings. Six poses standing, pointing, arms raised, hands on hips, sitting, leaning. Eight moves walk, run, jump, crouch, stand up, wave (a hello with the hand), fear, rest. Breathing and blinking are added by themselves. Six expressions neutral, joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise. Mouths Those of the expressions (smile, laugh, sad, surprise, anger) and those for speech, to follow the words of the voice. Two templates The standard proportions, and the same with a swinging tail, for an animal. The moves, played by the Touffu accroupi crouches, arms forward for balance coucou waves hello, arm raised to the side course runs, arms bent, small bounce marche walks calmly, arms swinging peur trembles with fear, round eyes, tuft standing on end repos stands, breathing gently saut jumps on the spot: wind-up, take-off, cushioned landing se-relever gets up from the crouching position What it does in a scene Action You say Walk or run to a point, without sliding « qu'il entre en marchant » (“have him walk in”), « qu'il reparte en courant » (“have him run off”) Take a pose and hold it « qu'il montre le chiffre » (“have him point at the figure”) Make a gesture « qu'il salue » (“have him wave”), « qu'il saute de joie » (“have him jump for joy”) Turn around « qu'il se tourne vers nous » (“have him turn towards us”) Change expression, open the mouth « qu'il sourie » (“have him smile”), « qu'il ait l'air surpris » (“have him look surprised”) Speak with the scene’s voice « fais-le parler pendant la voix de la scène 2 » (“make him talk during the voice in scene 2”) Each action is timed to another element of the scene, to a word of the voice or to a beat of the music. To create a pose or a move: /pulse-motion:animer-personnage ; to adjust them with the mouse: /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages ."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"formats","t":"Catalogue","s":"The picture formats","x":"Format Shape For 16:9 Landscape Training, presentations, YouTube, a training platform. The default format. 9:16 Vertical Stories, Reels, TikTok, Shorts. Chosen for the “social” genre. 4:5 Almost square, vertical The LinkedIn or Instagram feed. 1:1 Square Social media feeds, when you want the same video everywhere. All the shipped styles work in all four formats: sizes adapt to the height of the picture. In 9:16 , the check also makes sure no text falls under the social media buttons (top, bottom, and right-hand side)."},{"u":"en/catalogue.html","a":"exemple","t":"Catalogue","s":"The shipped example","x":"The plugin contains an example video, “The cold chain” : a one-minute training video, in six scenes, in the clair-pedagogique style, with composed music, a voice, subtitles and sound effects on the transitions. It is the one followed in tutorial 1 ."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:animer-personnage Create or change a character’s pose or move, and see it from several angles (« fais une pose où il se gratte la tête » (“make a pose where he scratches his head”), « il marche trop vite » (“he walks too fast”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Description","x":"A Pulse Motion character moves with poses (a held attitude: pointing, hands on hips, sitting) and moves (walking, running, a jump, a gesture). It also changes expression : joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise. This command creates new ones or changes existing ones, for a drawn character as well as for the stick figure. After each change, Pulse Motion checks the result and looks at it on a sheet , an image made of thumbnails that shows the move from several angles: limbs must not bend the wrong way, feet must stay on the ground, the gesture must look like what you asked for. It fixes things, in three rounds at most, then shows you the sheet. It is also used to put a character in a scene and have it act there: walk in, point at a figure, wave, leave, or talk over the scene’s voice."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Why use it","x":"A new gesture in one sentence. « Fais une pose où il se gratte la tête » (“Make a pose where he scratches his head”), « fais-le sauter plus haut » (“make him jump higher”). You see it from every angle : in profile, three-quarter, from the front. The built-in moves stay intact. To change the walk, Pulse Motion makes a copy stored with your video, which replaces the walk for that video only. The character acts with the scene. Its actions are timed to the other elements or to a word in the voice; between two actions it breathes, and every change happens smoothly."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage or /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage , to make your character move. The stick figure is always there anyway. To find out what already exists : « quels mouvements existent ? » (“what moves are there?”). This works even without a video in the folder. Before /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages , if you then want to fine-tune the gesture with the mouse. Everything characters can do is gathered on the Personnages (in French) page."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage [la pose ou le mouvement voulu] “[the pose or move you want]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. You can also run the command on its own and describe the gesture afterwards, in your own words. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage fais une pose où il se gratte la tête “make a pose where he scratches his head” /pulse-motion:animer-personnage montre-moi la course de face et de profil “show me the run from the front and in profile” /pulse-motion:animer-personnage mets le cuisinier dans la scène 3, qu'il montre le chiffre “put the chef in scene 3, pointing at the figure” What you can ask for What you want Example sentences Find out what exists « quels mouvements existent ? » (“what moves are there?”) See a move « montre-moi la course de face et de profil » (“show me the run from the front and in profile”) A new pose « fais une pose où il se gratte la tête » (“make a pose where he scratches his head”) A changed move « il marche trop vite », « fais-le sauter plus haut » (“he walks too fast”, “make him jump higher”) An expression at a given moment « fais-lui une tête surprise quand il salue » (“give him a surprised face when he waves”) A new gesture « un geste où il applaudit » (“a gesture where he claps”) The character in a scene « mets le cuisinier dans la scène 3, qu'il montre le chiffre », « fais-le entrer puis repartir » (“put the chef in scene 3, pointing at the figure”, “have him come in and then leave”) Make it talk « fais-le parler », « qu'il dise le texte de la scène » (“make him talk”, “have him say the scene’s text”) What already exists Family Built into Pulse Motion Poses standing, pointing, arms raised, hands on hips, sitting, leaning Moves idle, walk, run, jump, crouch, stand up, wave, fright Expressions neutral, joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise Drawn character touffu What a character does in a scene Action What it does Walk, run Go from one point to another, without sliding, turning to face the direction of travel. Strike a pose Hold an attitude for a while. Play a move A jump, a wave… Turn In profile, from the front, three-quarter. Change expression Joy, fear… until the next expression. Change mouth A specific mouth, for as long as needed. Talk The mouth follows the words of the scene’s voice."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"What it produces","x":"The new or changed poses and moves , stored with the video: personnages/poses/<nom>.json and personnages/clips/<nom>.json . A sheet showing the move from several angles, with a sentence about what has changed. For a new pose or move , a question: « Je le garde pour vos prochaines vidéos ? » (Shall I keep it for your next videos?). Once kept, it joins your personal library . For a character in a scene : the updated scene, the video checked, and a sheet of the scene to check its position."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Creating needs a video in the folder. Without one, Pulse Motion can only show you the moves; it offers to create a video with /pulse-motion:creer . A change only applies to this video. The built-in moves are never changed: your slower walk stays specific to the video in the folder. To talk, the scene must have a voice. If two characters share the voice, each one talks during its part, which starts on a word. A gesture during a walk would make the character slide. Pulse Motion places poses and gestures after the end of the journey. Text stays readable. Pulse Motion places it out of the character’s path, or has the character cross the scene before the text arrives."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion says it needs a video’s folder You can look at the moves anywhere, but a new pose or move is stored with a video. Open your video’s folder, or create one with /pulse-motion:creer . The move doesn’t look like what I wanted Say what is wrong, in one sentence: « il lève le bras trop haut », « plus lentement » (“he raises his arm too high”, “more slowly”). To set the position of the arms and legs yourself, open /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages . The character doesn’t talk The scene has no voice. Add one with /pulse-motion:retoucher (« ajoute une voix à la vidéo » (“add a voice to the video”)), or record it with /pulse-motion:voix . Its mouth moves badly when it talks A drawn character needs speaking mouths. Ask for them with /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage (« dessine-lui les bouches pour parler » (“draw him the mouths for talking”)). My changed walk doesn’t appear in another video That is intended: a change is stored with the video where you made it. Make the request again in the other video. “node” not found Node.js is not installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage il marche trop vite pulse-motion personnages afficher marche pulse-motion personnages verifier --dossier . pulse-motion personnages planche marche --vues 0,45,90 --dessin cuisinier --dossier . Le cuisinier marche plus posément : chaque pas dure un peu plus longtemps. Ce changement vaut pour cette vidéo seulement. Pour régler la marche vous-même, à la souris : /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages. In English You: /pulse-motion:animer-personnage he walks too fast Claude: The chef now walks more calmly: each step lasts a little longer. This change applies to this video only. To adjust the walk yourself, with the mouse: /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage mets le cuisinier dans la scène 3, qu'il entre et montre le chiffre pulse-motion assembler . pulse-motion controler . pulse-motion planche . chiffre Dans la scène 3, le cuisinier entre en marchant par la gauche, s'arrête, puis montre le chiffre quand il apparaît. Le texte reste dégagé. L'aperçu s'est rechargé. In English You: /pulse-motion:animer-personnage put the chef in scene 3, have him come in and point at the figure Claude: In scene 3, the chef walks in from the left, stops, then points at the figure when it appears. The text stays clear. The preview has reloaded. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage fais-le parler dans l'intro pulse-motion assembler . pulse-motion planche . intro Dans l'intro, la bouche du cuisinier suit maintenant les mots de la voix, jusqu'à la fin de la scène. In English You: /pulse-motion:animer-personnage make him talk in the intro Claude: In the intro, the chef’s mouth now follows the words of the voice, until the end of the scene."},{"u":"en/commandes/animer-personnage.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage : draw the character, or edit its drawing. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages : adjust a pose or a move with the mouse. /pulse-motion:voix : record the voice it talks over."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:atelier-particules Open the particle effects workshop to adjust an effect while watching it (« laisse-moi régler les étincelles » (“let me adjust the sparks”), « je veux voir ce que donne la neige » (“I want to see what the snow looks like”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Description","x":"Particle effects (sparks, confetti, snow, smoke…) are usually edited in one sentence: « plus d'étincelles » (“more sparks”), « en bleu » (“in blue”). This command lets you adjust them by eye , with sliders, while watching the result. Depending on your request, Pulse Motion opens one of these two places: The preview’s « Effets » (Effects) button , for an effect already placed in a scene. You choose the effect from the list, or click on it in the picture; the sliders change the picture straight away, and playback loops on the effect. « Enregistrer » (Save) stores your settings in the scene. « Recette complète » (Full recipe) shows all the effect’s settings, and « Enregistrer comme nouvel effet » (Save as new effect) turns it into an effect of your own, which the scene uses at once. The workshop page , to work on an effect on its own (« laisse-moi régler les étincelles » (“let me adjust the sparks”)). The effect loops, on a light background (your brand kit’s) or a dark one; you move and resize with the mouse the area where the particles appear. « Enregistrer » stores the effect with your video: your settings become its starting settings. When you say you have finished, Pulse Motion reads the chosen setting and describes it to you in one sentence."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Why use it","x":"An effect is judged by eye. The right amount of snow or the right speed for a burst is found faster by watching it. The result is immediate , without reloading the page. You can create your own effect from a built-in one, and reuse it in the video. Nothing is lost. Every save goes through the history : « annule » (“undo”) reverses it."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:creer , when a scene has an effect you want to fine-tune. To try out an effect before putting it in a scene: « je veux voir ce que donne la neige » (“I want to see what the snow looks like”). Instead of /pulse-motion:retoucher when a sentence is no longer enough. For a simple change (« plus d'étincelles » (“more sparks”), « enlève les confettis » (“remove the confetti”)), an edit is quicker."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules [l'effet à régler] “[the effect to adjust]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. You can also run the command on its own and then say which effect to adjust. /pulse-motion:atelier-particules laisse-moi régler les étincelles “let me adjust the sparks” /pulse-motion:atelier-particules je veux voir ce que donne la neige “I want to see what the snow looks like” The built-in effects: étincelles (sparks), braises (embers), confettis (confetti), feu d’artifice (fireworks), fumée (smoke), feu (fire), poussière (dust), neige (snow), pluie (rain), paillettes (glitter), énergie (energy), explosion (explosion). They are described in the catalogue . You only say « ouvre l'éditeur » (“open the editor”), and your video also has characters? Pulse Motion asks you which: « Les effets de particules » (The particle effects) or « Les personnages » (The characters). For characters, use /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages ."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"What it produces","x":"The effect’s settings, stored in the scene , when you save from the preview’s « Effets » button. An effect of your own , stored with the video in effets/<nom>.json , when you save a new effect or an effect adjusted on the workshop page. A sentence summing up the chosen setting, once you have finished."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Outside a video’s folder, the workshop does not save. It opens anyway so you can try things out; Pulse Motion warns you. A scene changed in the meantime is not overwritten. If the scene changed while you were adjusting (an edit, for example), the workshop refuses to save and offers to reload the page. The name of a new effect is written in lower case, with numbers and hyphens, and must differ from those of the built-in effects. Tip: an effect serves a purpose: highlighting a figure, celebrating a conclusion, setting a mood. One well-placed subtle effect is worth more than three spectacular ones."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"I saved, but nothing changed in my video The open folder probably has no video in it: the workshop adjusts, but does not save. Open your video’s folder in VS Code and run the command again. The workshop refuses to save and offers to reload the page The scene was changed while you were adjusting the effect. Reload the page, redo your adjustment, then save: nothing from the edit has been overwritten. The snow can’t be seen on a light background Snow and dust are white to start with. On a light background, choose your brand kit’s primary colour, or a blue-grey. Pulse Motion asks me which editor to open Your video has effects and characters, and “the editor” could mean either. Choose « Les effets de particules » (The particle effects); for characters, use /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages . “node” not found Node.js is not installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules laisse-moi régler les étincelles de la scène 3 pulse-motion apercu . --ouvrir L'aperçu est ouvert. Cliquez sur « Effets », puis choisissez les étincelles : les curseurs changent l'image tout de suite, et la lecture boucle sur l'effet. Cliquez sur « Enregistrer » quand elles vous plaisent. C'est fait. Les étincelles de la scène 3 sont enregistrées : plus nombreuses et un peu plus lentes, dans la couleur d'accent de votre charte. In English You: /pulse-motion:atelier-particules let me adjust the sparks in scene 3 Claude: The preview is open. Click “Effets” (Effects), then choose the sparks: the sliders change the picture straight away, and playback loops on the effect. Click “Enregistrer” (Save) when you like them. You: Done. Claude: The sparks in scene 3 are saved: more of them and a little slower, in your brand kit’s accent colour. /pulse-motion:atelier-particules je veux voir ce que donne la neige pulse-motion atelier neige L'atelier est ouvert sur la neige, qui tombe en boucle. Passez du fond clair au fond sombre, déplacez à la souris la zone d'où tombent les flocons, réglez les curseurs. « Enregistrer » range l'effet avec votre vidéo. In English You: /pulse-motion:atelier-particules I want to see what the snow looks like Claude: The workshop is open on the snow, which falls in a loop. Switch between the light and dark backgrounds, move the area the flakes fall from with the mouse, adjust the sliders. “Enregistrer” (Save) stores the effect with your video."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-particules.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-particules","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher : edit an effect in one sentence, add it or remove it. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages : the character editor."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages Open the character workshop to pose and animate a character with the mouse (« je veux régler son salut moi-même » (“I want to adjust his wave myself”), « laisse-moi placer ses bras » (“let me place his arms”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Description","x":"The character workshop is an editor that opens in your browser. Pulse Motion opens it on the pose or move you ask for (“his wave” is the coucou move), with your character: the video’s character if there is only one, otherwise it asks you which. Without a drawn character, it is the stick figure. In the workshop: Dragging a joint rotates the limb. Shift + dragging a hand or a foot brings it under the mouse: the arm or leg follows. The strip at the bottom holds the moments of the move: you adjust the character at each moment. « Enregistrer » (Save) stores the result with your video; « annule » (“undo”) reverses it. The workshop is also used to fix a cut-out , for a character taken from your illustration with /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage : click a shape then a bone of the skeleton to assign it, drag the dots to move the joints, « Découper » (Cut out) to see the walking result, and « Enregistrer ». When you say you have finished, Pulse Motion reads what you saved, looks at the move’s sheet and describes it to you in one sentence."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Why use it","x":"Some gestures are easier to show than to describe. Placing a hand in an exact spot, setting the angle of an elbow: it is simpler with the mouse. You see the result as you work , from every angle. You risk nothing. Until you have saved, nothing changes; afterwards, « annule » (“undo”) goes back. A cut-out can be fixed without starting over. A misplaced elbow moves with one gesture."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:animer-personnage , when a sentence is no longer enough to get the exact gesture. After /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage , to see your character from every angle. After /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage , if a limb turns around the wrong point or a shape is in the wrong limb. Everything characters can do is gathered on the Personnages (in French) page."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages [le mouvement ou la pose à ouvrir, et le personnage] “[the move or pose to open, and the character]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. You can also run the command on its own and then say what you want to adjust. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages je veux régler son salut moi-même “I want to adjust his wave myself” /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages je veux voir le cuisinier sous tous les angles “I want to see the chef from every angle” /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages laisse-moi corriger la découpe “let me fix the cut-out” You only say « ouvre l'éditeur » (“open the editor”), and your video also has particle effects? Pulse Motion asks you which: « Les personnages (Recommandé) » (The characters, Recommended) or « Les effets de particules » (The particle effects). For effects, use /pulse-motion:atelier-particules ."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"What it produces","x":"The workshop open in your browser , on the requested move or pose. What you save , stored with the video: the adjusted pose or move, or the fixed cut-out. If you saved it under a new name, Pulse Motion tells you. A sheet of the result, once you have finished, to see it in motion again."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Without a video in the folder, nothing is saved. The workshop opens anyway, to look and try things out; Pulse Motion warns you. Tell Claude when you have finished. That is when Pulse Motion reads what you saved and shows you the result. A save can be undone. « annule » (“undo”) goes back to the previous state, as for any edit. Tip: start with the most telling moment of the gesture (the arm at the very top of a wave), save, look, then refine the other moments on the strip."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"I clicked “Enregistrer” (Save) and nothing changed in my video The open folder probably has no video in it: the workshop adjusts, but does not save. Open your video’s folder in VS Code and run the command again. Pulse Motion asks me which editor to open Your video has characters and particle effects, and “the editor” could mean either. Choose « Les personnages » (The characters); for effects, use /pulse-motion:atelier-particules . A limb turns around the wrong point The joint is in the wrong place in the cut-out. Ask « laisse-moi corriger la découpe » (“let me fix the cut-out”), then drag the joint’s dot (the elbow, the knee) to its centre, and « Enregistrer ». I want to go back to the previous version Say « annule » (“undo”), for example with /pulse-motion:retoucher annule : your save is undone. “node” not found Node.js is not installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages je veux régler son salut moi-même pulse-motion personnages liste --dossier . pulse-motion personnages atelier coucou --dessin cuisinier --dossier . L'atelier des personnages est ouvert sur le salut du cuisinier. Glissez une articulation pour tourner le membre ; Maj + glisser une main ou un pied l'amène sous la souris. La frise du bas porte les moments du salut : « Enregistrer » range le résultat avec la vidéo, et « annule » le défait. J'ai fini. pulse-motion personnages planche coucou --dessin cuisinier --dossier . Votre salut est enregistré : le bras monte plus haut et la main reste ouverte. Voici la planche. In English You: /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages I want to adjust his wave myself Claude: The character workshop is open on the chef’s wave. Drag a joint to rotate the limb; Shift + drag a hand or a foot brings it under the mouse. The strip at the bottom holds the moments of the wave: “Enregistrer” (Save) stores the result with the video, and “annule” (undo) reverses it. You: I’ve finished. Claude: Your wave is saved: the arm goes up higher and the hand stays open. Here is the sheet. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages le coude de la mascotte est mal placé pulse-motion personnages atelier --decoupe mascotte --dossier . L'atelier montre votre illustration découpée. Cliquez une forme puis un os pour l'y ranger, glissez les ronds pour déplacer les articulations. « Découper » montre le résultat qui marche ; « Enregistrer » le range. C'est bon. pulse-motion personnages planche marche --dessin mascotte --dossier . Le bras de la mascotte plie maintenant au bon endroit. Voici sa marche. In English You: /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages the mascot’s elbow is in the wrong place Claude: The workshop shows your cut-out illustration. Click a shape then a bone to assign it, drag the dots to move the joints. “Découper” (Cut out) shows the walking result; “Enregistrer” (Save) stores it. You: That’s fine. Claude: The mascot’s arm now bends in the right place. Here is its walk."},{"u":"en/commandes/atelier-personnages.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage : create a pose or a move in one sentence. /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage : cut out your illustration before fixing it here. /pulse-motion:atelier-particules : the particle effects editor."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"","x":"Step 1 · Create /pulse-motion:creer Create a motion design video in a few questions, approve its timeline, then watch it in your browser."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Description","x":"This is the command you start with. You give the subject in one sentence; Pulse Motion asks you two short rounds of questions, writes the timeline (the list of scenes, their text, their length) and shows it to you as a table. Nothing is animated until you approve it; as soon as you do, a first animated draft opens in your browser, in under a minute. Once the timeline is approved, Pulse Motion makes the video: a specialist assistant, the animator , writes each scene, all at the same time. Each finished scene replaces its draft in the preview, right before your eyes. Automatic checks then test readability and fix the safe cases on their own: a video that still contains a draft cannot be exported. One video = one folder. Run the command in an empty folder, open in VS Code. If the folder already holds a video, Pulse Motion offers to pick it up , or to create the new one somewhere else."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Why use it","x":"You talk about your subject, not about technique. Pulse Motion works out the genre (training, product, storytelling, social media) and the format, then picks the structure that works for that genre. One approval is enough. On the quick path, you only approve the timeline, along with the style. Everything else is done for you. The pace is right from the start. Each scene lasts long enough to be read (1.5 s + 0.3 s per word), nothing stays still for more than 2.5 s, and the video ends on a steady frame. You see the video move straight away. An animated draft opens as soon as you approve the timeline, with the music; it is replaced scene by scene. You compare before you choose. Three styles appear side by side, on your first two scenes, in your browser."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise For every new video , in an empty folder. After /pulse-motion:style , if you have created your own style: it will be offered alongside the built-in styles. Before /pulse-motion:retoucher and /pulse-motion:exporter , which work on the video created here."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:creer [le sujet de la vidéo en une phrase] “[the subject of the video in one sentence]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. With no subject, there is a single question: « Que voulez-vous faire ? » (“What would you like to do?”): a video from your idea (recommended), start from a video template, see an example first, or a video in one of your styles (« Créer mon propre style » (“Create my own style”) if you don’t have one yet). /pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid en une minute pour mes stagiaires “the cold chain in one minute, for my trainees” /pulse-motion:creer présenter notre application de réservation en 30 secondes “present our booking app in 30 seconds” A plain sentence works too: « fais-moi une vidéo d'une minute sur la chaîne du froid » (“make me a one-minute video about the cold chain”), « que sait faire Pulse Motion ? » (“what can Pulse Motion do?”). Pulse Motion only creates a video if you ask for one, first reminds you that this is a new video in this folder, and writes nothing before your first answer; in a folder that already has a video, it offers to pick it up. The questions asked A first round, four questions at most: Question Suggested answers Length 30 s · 1 min (recommended for a short training video) · 3 min · 5 min Audience General public · professionals in the field · pupils or trainees (adapted to your subject). Not asked if your subject already says so (« pour mes stagiaires » (“for my trainees”)). Path Quick (recommended): you only approve the timeline · Guided : you also approve the style and the final video · Do it for me : no more questions, you just watch the result To add My logo and my colours · my company’s website (colours, logo and fonts suggested, after your consent) · « Ma charte… » (“My brand kit…”) if you have saved one · nothing for now Then a round on sound (in French): Question Suggested answers Music Music composed for the video (recommended) · I have a file · No music Voice and subtitles My voice, with subtitles (recommended) · My voice, without subtitles · Synthetic voice · No voice (so no subtitles: they repeat the spoken text). A file of your voice: just say so. The computer Only if a useful tool is missing (voice transcription, ffmpeg): Get the computer ready while I work (recommended) · Later In total, without a brand kit: 7 answers at most on the quick path, 9 on the guided path. The “Do it for me” path You answer the first round, and that’s it: 4 answers . Pulse Motion chooses the rest for you: music composed to suit the genre, no voice and no subtitles (you can add them later), the recommended style, the words of your trade noted in the glossary, nothing installed. Only exception: it asks you for your logo file if you chose “My logo and my colours”. At the end, the summary lists « Mes choix » (“My choices”) : each one can be changed in one sentence with /pulse-motion:retoucher (« passe en style éditorial » (“switch to the editorial style”)). The format follows from the genre: 16:9 (landscape) in general, 9:16 (vertical) for social media, 4:5 if you are aiming at the LinkedIn or Instagram feed."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"modeles","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Starting from a template","x":"Pulse Motion recognises nine kinds of video from your subject. When it recognises one, it starts from its template: a proven scene structure, a length, a format, a musical mood and three styles that suit it. It tells you so in one sentence (« Je pars du modèle « Pitch » : 6 scènes, environ 1 min. » (“I’m starting from the ‘Pitch’ template: 6 scenes, about 1 min.”)) and opens the finished example of that template while you answer the questions, without writing anything in your folder; you can change everything afterwards. For a subject that matches no template, it carries on as usual. Template For Length, format Short training video getting a rule or a gesture across 1 min, landscape Software tutorial showing how to do something in an app, step by step, filmed on screen (see /pulse-motion:filmer ) 2 min, landscape; or “30-second tip”, vertical Explainer video making a mechanism, a circuit or a calculation clear: arrows, chart, formula, a camera that finds its own close-ups (see The explainer video ) 1 min, landscape Product presentation introducing an offer 45 s, landscape Story telling a journey, an origin 1 min, landscape Social media post hooking people in 2 seconds 30 s, vertical Welcoming a newcomer saying welcome, guiding the first steps 1 min, landscape Pitch convincing a jury or investors 1 min, landscape Announcement a launch, an opening, an event 30 s, vertical /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de notre projet de recyclerie, à partir du modèle pitch “the pitch for our reuse-centre project, from the pitch template” To see the result before you start, run the command below in an empty folder: the template’s finished example is copied there and opens in the preview. /pulse-motion:creer montre-moi un exemple de pitch “show me an example of a pitch” All the ideas, with their video examples and sentences to copy, are on the Video ideas page."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"What it produces","x":"Everything is stored in the video’s folder. You never have to open these files, but you can read them. File What it is for deroule.json The timeline: the title, the genre, the format, the style, the music, and for each scene its text, its spoken text, its length and its transition. scenes/01-intro.html … One scene per file, numbered in order. The text isn’t written in them: it comes from the timeline. charte/ Your logo, your colours ( charte.json ), your fonts and the glossary of your trade. assets/ Your files: music, images, voice takes ( assets/voix/ ). sortie/<titre>.html The animated video , as a single page that opens with no internet connection. sortie/<titre>.srt and .vtt The subtitles, if you asked for them, for platforms that show them separately. sortie/controle.json The report of the last check: what was fixed automatically, what remains. sortie/comparaison/ The images from the style comparison. At the end, if you added your logo and your colours, Pulse Motion offers to save them as a brand kit (“client-dupont”, for example): it will be offered for your next videos."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Nothing is animated before you approve. If the timeline doesn’t suit you, say what needs changing: Pulse Motion corrects it and shows it to you again. Your files are copied, never moved. The logo, music or voice you provide is copied into the video’s folder; the original stays where it was. Installations need your consent. Transcription (Python, about 800 MB) is only installed if you accept. Everything works without it. The placeholder voice isn’t yours. If you record yourself, the computer’s voice first reads the text to time the animation. It is never delivered: export refuses it until you have recorded. Warning: a JPEG or WebP logo isn’t enough to pick out your colours cleanly. Provide a PNG or an SVG."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"“node” not found, or Node.js too old Install the LTS version of Node.js from nodejs.org , then close VS Code completely and reopen it. See the requirements . Pulse Motion says a video already exists here The folder already holds a timeline. To carry on with that video: /pulse-motion:reprendre . To make a new one: create another empty folder, open it, and run the command again. The style comparison doesn’t open It opens in Chrome or Edge. Without them, Pulse Motion describes each style by its personality, in three words: choose on that basis. See Chrome or Edge . Pulse Motion tells me it made a scene longer That’s the reading check: the scene was too short to read its text. Pulse Motion lengthened it (by 50% at most) and tells you in one sentence. If you prefer a precise length, ask for it with /pulse-motion:retoucher . « Le rendu n'a pas pu être vérifié » (“The render could not be checked”) Neither Chrome nor Edge was found, or the page ran into an error. The video is made, but overflow and contrast haven’t been checked on screen. Install one of the two browsers, then ask for an edit, even a tiny one: the full check runs again. My font wasn’t found Pulse Motion fetches free fonts from Bunny Fonts. If yours isn’t there, it keeps the closest of the style’s fonts and tells you. You can give it the font file ( .ttf , .otf , .woff or .woff2 )."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires Durée ? → 1 min (Recommandé pour une formation courte) Public ? → Élèves ou stagiaires Parcours ? → Rapide (Recommandé) : je valide seulement le déroulé À ajouter ? → Rien pour l'instant Musique ? → Une musique composée pour la vidéo (Recommandé), lumineuse, posée, qui avance Voix et sous-titres ? → Ma voix, avec sous-titres (Recommandé) pulse-motion comparer . clair-pedagogique,chaleureux,minimal --ouvrir Voici le déroulé : 6 scènes, 1 min 03. La comparaison des styles s'est ouverte dans votre navigateur. → Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible, rassurant (Recommandé) / … accueillant, doux, humain / … net, sûr, essentiel / Je veux changer quelque chose Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible, rassurant Voici une première ébauche animée ; je la remplace scène par scène. J'ai allongé la scène 3 d'une seconde pour laisser le temps de lire. ✅ Fait : la vidéo « La chaîne du froid » (6 scènes, 1 min 04) est prête et s'affiche dans votre navigateur. Pour enregistrer votre voix, cliquez sur « Voix » dans la barre de l'aperçu. In English You: /pulse-motion:creer the cold chain, for my trainees Claude: Length? → 1 min (Recommended for a short training video). Audience? → Pupils or trainees. Path? → Quick (Recommended): I only approve the timeline. To add? → Nothing for now Claude: Music? → Music composed for the video (Recommended), bright, calm, moving forward. Voice and subtitles? → My voice, with subtitles (Recommended) Claude: Here is the timeline: 6 scenes, 1 min 03 s. The style comparison has opened in your browser. → I approve, with the calm, readable, reassuring style (Recommended) / … welcoming, gentle, human / … crisp, confident, essential / I want to change something You: I approve, with the calm, readable, reassuring style Claude: Here is a first animated draft; I’m replacing it scene by scene. Claude: I made scene 3 one second longer to leave time to read. ✅ Done: the video “La chaîne du froid” (6 scenes, 1 min 04 s) is ready and showing in your browser. To record your voice, click “Voix” (Voice) in the preview bar. /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo sur le lavage des mains Ce dossier contient déjà la vidéo « La chaîne du froid ». Je vous propose de la reprendre avec /pulse-motion:reprendre, ou de créer la nouvelle vidéo dans un autre dossier. In English You: /pulse-motion:creer a video about handwashing Claude: This folder already holds the video “La chaîne du froid”. I suggest picking it up with /pulse-motion:reprendre, or creating the new video in another folder. /pulse-motion:creer le lancement de notre application, avec un pop-in sur chaque titre et un whip pan entre les scènes C'est noté : les titres entreront en pop-in (mouvement n° 001), et les scènes s'enchaîneront en panoramique filé (n° 042). Voici le déroulé… In English You: /pulse-motion:creer the launch of our app, with a pop-in on every title and a whip pan between scenes Claude: Noted: the titles will come in with a pop-in (move no. 001), and the scenes will flow into each other with a whip pan (no. 042). Here is the timeline… /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue, avec un truc qui claque pour le titre Une page s'ouvre : cochez les mouvements qui vous plaisent, puis cliquez sur « Envoyer à Claude ». In English You: /pulse-motion:creer a welcome video, with something punchy for the title Claude: A page opens: tick the moves you like, then click “Envoyer à Claude” (Send to Claude). Tip: don’t know the name of a move? Ask « quels mouvements existent ? » (“which moves are there?”), say « montre-moi les mouvements » (“show me the moves”) to choose them by watching, or browse the move gallery : each one has a name and a number."},{"u":"en/commandes/creer.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:creer","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:reprendre : carry on with a video you have started. /pulse-motion:retoucher : change the video in one sentence. /pulse-motion:exporter : make the MP4. /pulse-motion:style : create your own style before creating the video."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage Animate an illustration of a character you provide (Illustrator, Inkscape, Figma…) by cutting it out onto the Pulse Motion skeleton (« voici notre mascotte, fais-la marcher » (“here is our mascot, make it walk”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Description","x":"You already have a drawing of your character, a mascot for example, exported from your drawing software as a .svg vector file. Pulse Motion cuts it out so it can be animated: It looks at the illustration. It copies it into your video, then makes an annotated image of it: each shape numbered, on a grid. It assigns each shape to a limb of the skeleton (the head, the torso, each arm, each leg…) and places the joints at the centre of the drawing’s own: hip, knee, ankle; shoulder, elbow, wrist; the pelvis and the neck; the centre of the head. The background and the shadow on the ground are set aside. It names the main colours (the skin, the outfit…), so that « change sa tenue en rouge » (“make its outfit red”) works afterwards. It makes it walk on a sheet, from several angles, and fixes what is wrong, in three rounds at most. Your illustration becomes a drawn character, which can be animated with all of Pulse Motion’s poses and moves. If the eyes and the mouth are separate shapes in your drawing, the face comes alive: the eyes look around and blink."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Why use it","x":"Your character, just as you drew it. It keeps its look and its proportions. It moves straight away : walking, running, jumping, waving, and all the poses. Its colours can be edited in one sentence , once they have names. The cut-out can be fixed with the mouse , in the character workshop, if a joint is in the wrong place."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:creer : the cut-out is stored in a video’s folder. When you already have an illustration. For a character to invent, use /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage . Before /pulse-motion:animer-personnage , to give it gestures or have it act in a scene. Everything characters can do is gathered on the Personnages (in French) page."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage [le fichier SVG du personnage] “[the character’s SVG file]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. You can also run the command on its own and give the file afterwards: its path, or its name if it is already in the video’s assets/ folder. /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage assets/mascotte.svg /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage j'ai un personnage dans assets, anime-le “I have a character in assets, animate it” Preparing the illustration One character per file, preferably in profile (facing right), otherwise from the front. Some elements prevent the cut-out: Pulse Motion tells you which, and what to change in your drawing software. Your illustration contains In the drawing software Text Convert the text to shapes (“Create Outlines”, “Object to Path”). An image (a photo, a texture) Redraw it as shapes, or leave it out. A linked copy (a clone) Unlink the clone (“Object > Expand”). A filter, a mask Remove them, or apply them. “Entity” styles (Illustrator) Export again with “CSS Properties: Style Elements”. A clipping mask, on the other hand, does not get in the way: the shape is drawn whole, and Pulse Motion lets you know."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"What it produces","x":"File What it is for assets/<nom>.svg The copy of your illustration, stored with the video. The original stays where it was. personnages/decoupes/<nom>.json The cut-out: each shape assigned to a limb, and the position of the joints. personnages/dessins/<nom>.json The resulting drawn character, ready to move. Pulse Motion finishes with the walk sheet and what you can do next: fix the cut-out with the mouse, make the character move, draw its front if the illustration is only in profile."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"There must be a video in the folder. Without one, Pulse Motion tells you and offers to create one with /pulse-motion:creer . A rejected element stops the cut-out until it is fixed in your software. If it is only decorative, Pulse Motion may offer to leave it out. A front-view illustration also serves as the profile , until something better comes along. For a real profile, or a missing front, ask /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage . The face stays frozen if the eyes and the mouth are not separate shapes. With a living face, the other mouths (smile, surprise…) are drawn afterwards with /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage . Redoing the cut-out does not undo the rest. The other drawn views and the added mouths are kept; the colours keep their names. Tip: before exporting your illustration, keep the eyes and the mouth as separate shapes: your character will have a living face that looks around and blinks."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion rejects my illustration It contains text, an image, a clone, a filter or a mask. Pulse Motion tells you which: fix it in your drawing software (see the Preparing the illustration table), export again, then give it the file again. A limb comes loose or turns around the wrong point A joint is in the wrong place. Pulse Motion sees it on the sheet and fixes it; if the problem remains, fix it yourself with the mouse using /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages (« laisse-moi corriger la découpe » (“let me fix the cut-out”)). Pulse Motion reports shapes that were left out These shapes were not assigned to any limb. Pulse Motion assigns them, or sets them aside if they are not part of the character (a shadow, a background). My character’s eyes don’t move In your drawing, the eyes are part of the same shape as the head. Separate them in your drawing software (the white of the eye and the pupil), export again, and run the cut-out again. “node” not found Node.js is not installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage voici notre mascotte, fais-la marcher : C:\\Users\\Marie\\Bureau\\mascotte.svg pulse-motion personnages analyser assets/mascotte.svg --dossier . pulse-motion personnages decouper mascotte --dossier . pulse-motion personnages renommer-couleur mascotte c1 tenue --dossier . pulse-motion personnages planche marche --dessin mascotte --vues 0,45,180 --dossier . Votre mascotte marche ! Voici la planche, sous trois angles. Ensuite, vous pouvez corriger la découpe à la souris (/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages), la faire bouger (/pulse-motion:animer-personnage), ou dessiner sa face (/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage) : votre illustration n'est que de profil. In English You: /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage here is our mascot, make it walk: C:\\Users\\Marie\\Bureau\\mascotte.svg Claude: Your mascot is walking! Here is the sheet, from three angles. Next, you can fix the cut-out with the mouse (/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages), make it move (/pulse-motion:animer-personnage), or draw its front (/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage): your illustration is only in profile. /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage assets/chef.svg pulse-motion personnages analyser assets/chef.svg --dossier . Votre illustration contient du texte : le nom brodé sur le tablier. Dans votre logiciel de dessin, convertissez-le en formes (« Vectoriser » ou « Objet en chemin »), réexportez le fichier, puis redonnez-le-moi. In English You: /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage assets/chef.svg Claude: Your illustration contains text: the name embroidered on the apron. In your drawing software, convert it to shapes (“Create Outlines” or “Object to Path”), export the file again, then give it back to me."},{"u":"en/commandes/decouper-personnage.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:decouper-personnage","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages : fix the cut-out with the mouse. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage : its poses, its moves, its place in a scene. /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage : draw a missing view, or the mouths for its face."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage Draw a character for the video, or edit its drawing, and see it move (« dessine-moi un cuisinier rondouillard avec une toque » (“draw me a chubby chef with a chef’s hat”), « change sa tenue en rouge » (“make his outfit red”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Description","x":"Describe your character in one sentence: Pulse Motion draws it piece by piece (the head, the torso, the arms, the legs, the hands, the feet), placed on the same skeleton as every Pulse Motion character. The result: it can be animated straight away with all the poses and moves already available, from walking to waving. Pulse Motion starts from the character that comes with the plugin, “touffu”, and transforms it. The drawing is done in this order: The profile first , always: the character facing right (facing left is the same drawing, mirrored). Then the front , and if needed the back and the three-quarter view, if the character has to turn towards you. The face : the eyes, the eyebrows and the mouths for each expression (neutral, smile, laughter, sad, surprise, anger). The accessories : a chef’s hat, glasses, a cape, a backpack, placed in front of or behind the piece that carries them. Before showing it to you, Pulse Motion checks the drawing and looks at it on two sheets , images made of thumbnails: its walk from several angles, then its expressions and its mouths. It compares the views with each other and fixes what is wrong, in three rounds at most. The drawing is stored with your video, so you need to run the command in a video’s folder."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Why use it","x":"A character of your own, in one sentence. A job, a figure, an outfit: you describe, Pulse Motion draws. It moves straight away. Walking, running, jumping, waving, fright: Pulse Motion’s moves fit it without redoing anything. Edits are simple. The colours have names (the skin, the outfit…): « change sa tenue en rouge » (“make his outfit red”) changes one colour, without redrawing. It is checked before it reaches you. Pulse Motion looks for a head detached from the body, a gap at a joint, a floating accessory, an element present in one view and missing from another."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:creer : the character is stored in a video’s folder. Before /pulse-motion:animer-personnage , to give it new gestures or have it act in a scene. Instead of /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage if you don’t have an illustration yet. If you do, that is what you should cut out. Everything characters can do is gathered on the Personnages (in French) page."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage [le personnage à dessiner, ou la retouche de son dessin] “[the character to draw, or the edit to its drawing]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. You can also run the command on its own and describe the character afterwards, in your own words. /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage dessine-moi un cuisinier rondouillard avec une toque “draw me a chubby chef with a chef’s hat” /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage change sa tenue en rouge “make his outfit red” /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage ajoute-lui des lunettes “give him glasses” If your description leaves open a point that changes the whole drawing (a human or an animal, the figure), Pulse Motion asks you a single question, with the recommended answer first. Otherwise, it decides. What you can ask for What you want Example sentences A new character « dessine-moi un cuisinier rondouillard avec une toque », « une docteure avec des lunettes » (“draw me a chubby chef with a chef’s hat”, “a woman doctor with glasses”) A colour « change sa tenue en rouge », « des cheveux plus foncés » (“make his outfit red”, “darker hair”) Part of the drawing « refais-lui la tête » (“redo his head”) An accessory « ajoute-lui des lunettes », « donne-lui un sac à dos » (“give him glasses”, “give him a backpack”) One more view « il doit pouvoir se tourner vers nous » (“he needs to be able to turn towards us”): Pulse Motion draws his front"},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"What it produces","x":"The character’s drawing , stored with the video: personnages/dessins/<nom>.json . The name comes from your request, in lower case with hyphens: cuisinier , docteur-lunettes . Two sheets : the walk from several angles, and the expressions with the mouths. Pulse Motion shows you the walk sheet, and opens it if you want to see it. What you can do next : make it move, adjust it yourself with the mouse, or change a colour. For a new character , a question: « Je le garde pour vos prochaines vidéos ? » (Shall I keep it for your next videos?). Once kept, it joins your personal library , and Pulse Motion offers it to you when you ask for a character in another video."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"There must be a video in the folder. Without one, Pulse Motion tells you and offers to create one with /pulse-motion:creer . A character that turns needs more drawing. The profile is enough as long as it stays side-on. If it has to face you, say so: its front is drawn, with the same elements in the same proportions. A character that talks needs speaking mouths. If you plan to make it talk over the scene’s voice, say so: Pulse Motion draws at least the open mouth, the rounded mouth and the closed lips; the others are worked out from them. The style stays simple. Clean shapes, few details, an even line, and your brand kit’s colours when possible: the character has to read well in motion, in a video. Tip: to change the colour of an outfit, ask with those very words (« change sa tenue en rouge » (“make his outfit red”)): only the colour changes, the drawing stays the same."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion says there is no video in this folder The drawing is stored with a video. Open your video’s folder in VS Code, or create one with /pulse-motion:creer , then run the command again. Something looks wrong on the sheet The head seems detached, an accessory is floating, a hand is in the wrong place? Say it in one sentence (« de face, la tête flotte au-dessus du cou » (“from the front, the head floats above the neck”)): Pulse Motion fixes the drawing and shows you the sheet again. I want it to make a different gesture Poses and moves are not drawn: they are created with /pulse-motion:animer-personnage (« fais une pose où il se gratte la tête » (“make a pose where he scratches his head”)). I want to place its arms myself Open the character workshop with /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages : you pose it with the mouse. I already have a drawing of my character No need to have it redrawn: /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage cuts out your illustration so it can be animated. “node” not found Node.js is not installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage dessine-moi un cuisinier rondouillard avec une toque pulse-motion personnages afficher touffu --type dessin pulse-motion personnages verifier --dossier . pulse-motion personnages planche marche --dessin cuisinier --vues 0,45,90,-90 --dossier . pulse-motion personnages planche --dessin cuisinier --visage --dossier . Voici votre cuisinier : tout rond, en tablier blanc, avec une grande toque, dessiné de profil et de face. La planche le montre en train de marcher, sous quatre angles. Ensuite, vous pouvez le faire bouger (/pulse-motion:animer-personnage), le régler vous-même (/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages) ou changer une couleur. In English You: /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage draw me a chubby chef with a chef’s hat Claude: Here is your chef: nice and round, in a white apron, with a tall chef’s hat, drawn in profile and from the front. The sheet shows him walking, from four angles. Next, you can make him move (/pulse-motion:animer-personnage), adjust him yourself (/pulse-motion:atelier-personnages) or change a colour. /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage change sa tenue en rouge pulse-motion personnages afficher cuisinier --type dessin --dossier . pulse-motion personnages verifier --dossier . Le cuisinier porte maintenant une tenue rouge. Seule la couleur a changé : le dessin et ses mouvements restent les mêmes. In English You: /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage make his outfit red Claude: The chef now wears a red outfit. Only the colour has changed: the drawing and its moves stay the same."},{"u":"en/commandes/dessiner-personnage.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:animer-personnage : its poses, its moves, its expressions, its place in a scene. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages : pose it and animate it with the mouse. /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage : animate an illustration you already have."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"","x":"Step 3 · Export /pulse-motion:exporter Export the video as an MP4 set up for its use (the web, social media, a meeting, a video editor, an email), made on your computer, ready to publish or send."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Description","x":"The animated page is watched in a browser; the MP4 file can be published anywhere: social media, a training platform, email, YouTube. This command makes it on your computer, without sending anything online. It takes the video as it is : it changes nothing. You say what the video is for (“for LinkedIn”, “to send by email”) and Pulse Motion picks the settings: the picture size, its sharpness, the file size. It checks the video first, then chooses the best way to export: Mode When What you get High-fidelity export ffmpeg is installed (Pulse Motion offers it to you once) The browser’s real frames, one by one, and the sound at the right level, checked. The time depends on the use (see the table below). In-browser export Without ffmpeg, or if you decline to install it An MP4 made directly in Chrome or Edge, with nothing to install. A few advanced effects are rendered less well. Both exports have exactly the same sound as the preview: the music, the voice and the sound effects are mixed only once, in the page, and brought to the level the streaming platforms expect. The video’s colours are the page’s, in common players (Chrome, Safari, VLC, PowerPoint)."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"usages","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Uses","x":"Each use gives its own file: exporting for email does not overwrite the web version. Sizes and times measured on the two reference videos (one minute and three minutes) with a recent desktop computer; allow more on a laptop. Use What for What changes in the picture Size Export time Files included Needs ffmpeg Pour le web (For the web) website, YouTube, intranet, training platform the reference quality, the same as the preview about 5,1 Mo per minute about 1 min 19 s per minute of video cover, chapters, credits, subtitles no Pour les réseaux sociaux (For social media) LinkedIn, Instagram, TikTok, YouTube Shorts a little sharper than for the web, to stand up to the platforms’ recompression about 7,2 Mo per minute about 1 min 17 s per minute of video cover, credits, subtitles no Pour la projeter en réunion (To project in a meeting) projector, PowerPoint, Keynote, trade show very sharp, plays in presentation software, 30 frames per second about 7,5 Mo per minute about 1 min 17 s per minute of video cover, credits no Pour archiver ou confier à un monteur (To archive or hand to a video editor) the best quality, to keep or rework as faithful as possible, to rework with no visible loss; motion blur on fast passages about 6,8 Mo per minute about 2 min 31 s per minute of video cover, chapters, credits, subtitles no Pour l'envoyer par courriel (To send by email) attachment, messaging (WhatsApp, Teams) smaller (720p or 540p depending on length), grain refreshed less often; stays under the stated size under 10 Mo in total (video of 2 min 52 s at most) about 1 min 23 s per minute of video credits no Version de travail (Working version) quickly check a long video, never for publishing small (540p), 30 frames per second at most, grain refreshed less often about 2,5 Mo per minute about 1 min 7 s per minute of video credits no After a first export, another use redoes no frames: it starts again from the frames already captured. The email version aims for under 10 MB (or the size you say: « moins de 25 Mo pour Teams » (“under 25 MB for Teams”)); if the video is too long to fit, Pulse Motion tells you before starting, with the size it would need. The working version is made quickly, by the graphics card when it can: it is for checking, never for publishing. In the preview, the MP4 button in the playback bar offers four uses: Web, Réseaux (Social media), Présentation (Presentation), Courriel (Email). Chapters are only included if you asked for them (« ajoute des chapitres » (“add chapters”), an edit); they are then also built into the MP4 when ffmpeg is installed, and YouTube shows them from three upwards. Credits come with every export: fonts, music, voice, images and sounds, with their licence. A file you imported without saying where it came from is marked « à préciser » (to be specified): just say it (« la musique vient de Pixabay » (“the music comes from Pixabay”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"sorties","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Other outputs","x":"As well as the video, you can ask for: You say You get Size Time Needs ffmpeg « juste la scène 3 » (“just scene 3”) The scene on its own, without its transitions, with its sound about 1 Mo for 10 s 5 to 8 s after an export (nothing is redone) no « de 12 à 18 secondes » (“from 12 to 18 seconds”) That passage on its own, to quickly review an edit about 1 Mo for 10 s 5 to 8 s after an export no « en GIF » (“as a GIF”) A looping GIF, without sound, of a scene or a passage of 20 s at most about 0,6 Mo for 6 s 2 to 3 s after an export yes « dans un téléphone » (“in a phone”) A vertical video shown inside a phone, on your brand kit’s background about 3,5 Mo per minute about 1 min 18 s per minute of video no « à incruster dans mon montage » (“to overlay in my edit”) A video with no background, to place over another picture: for editing software (Premiere, DaVinci Resolve, Final Cut), or « pour le web » (“for the web”) about 850 Mo per minute for editing; 6 Mo per minute for the web about 1 min 25 s per minute of video yes « les images une par une pour mon monteur » (“the frames one by one for my editor”) Every frame of the video (or of a passage) in its own file, PNG or JPEG, with the sound apart: Premiere, DaVinci Resolve or After Effects import them as a sequence. If interrupted, the sequence picks up where it stopped about 2 Mo per frame as PNG (3,6 Go per minute at 30 frames per second), 0,4 Mo as JPEG; above 2 Go, Pulse Motion asks you first about that of a faithful export no « un PDF des scènes pour le client » (“a PDF of the scenes for the client”) An A4 landscape PDF, two scenes per page, three frames and the spoken text about 0,3 Mo per page 4 to 8 s no « un texte pour LinkedIn » (“a text for LinkedIn”) A post, an email or a YouTube description, with no figure that is not in the video; nothing is published — — no The master adds a light motion blur to fast passages, like a camera (never on text to be read): the export takes longer where things move fast (30% longer for the one-minute video, more than twice as long for the very lively three-minute one). The no-background video refuses a scene with a solid background, and names it: Pulse Motion offers to remove that background for this version. For a video that loops (« fais-la tourner en boucle » (“make it loop”), an edit), the music no longer fades in or out, and the check makes sure the end joins the beginning without a jolt. A scene, a passage or a GIF start from the frames already made: after an export, they capture nothing again. They never touch the full video. For a vertical video made for TikTok, Shorts or Reels, say so (an edit): the check then makes sure nothing sits under the app’s buttons, and the subtitles move above them. In the preview, the Z key shows this area. A page that can make its own MP4. At the end of the export, Claude also offers you a page that makes its MP4 by itself, even without Claude (about 250 KB more); the recommended answer is « Non merci » (No thanks). With this page, open it later in Chrome or Edge and click MP4 in the playback bar: the video is made in the browser, then you choose where to save it. In another browser, the page tells you to open it in Chrome or Edge. For the best quality, exporting through Claude is still recommended. « Une page plus légère, sans le bouton MP4 » (“A lighter page, without the MP4 button”) puts it back as before."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Why use it","x":"Everything stays with you. No online service, no queue, no watermark. What you see is what you get. The high-fidelity export films the browser frame by frame: no stutter, even on a modest computer. Clean sound. The sound level is checked at export; the music lowers itself under the voice. An interrupted export is not lost. The parts already done are picked up at the next export."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise When you like the video in the preview, and your voice is recorded. After each round of edits : the MP4 does not update by itself, unlike the animated page. Tip: you only want to put the video on your website? No need to export: the animated page sortie/<titre>.html is already ready and opens offline, on the cover image with a ▶ button."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:exporter It takes nothing after the name, but you can say what you want: You say Pulse Motion exports (nothing) It asks you what the video is for: the web (recommended), social media, an email or a meeting. A vertical video, or one made for social media, suggests social media first. « pour YouTube », « pour mon site », « pour notre plateforme de formation » (“for YouTube”, “for my website”, “for our training platform”) For the web: the preview’s quality. « pour LinkedIn », « pour Instagram » (“for LinkedIn”, “for Instagram”) For social media: a little sharper, to stand up to the platforms’ recompression. « pour la projeter », « pour PowerPoint » (“to project it”, “for PowerPoint”) For presentation: very sharp, plays in PowerPoint and Keynote. « pour mon monteur », « la meilleure qualité » (“for my video editor”, “the best quality”) The master: the most faithful file, to rework, with a light motion blur on fast passages (like a camera); « sans flou » (“no blur”) removes it. « à incruster dans mon montage », « fond transparent » (“to overlay in my edit”, “transparent background”) A video with no background, to place over another picture in editing software (see the table above). « par courriel », « moins de 20 Mo » (“by email”, “under 20 MB”) A light version that stays under the stated size (10 MB if you don’t say). « vite fait, juste pour vérifier » (“quickly, just to check”) A working version, small and fast, not for publishing. « plus fluide » (“smoother”) At 60 frames per second. The export takes twice as long. « une version de travail avec la voix de l'ordinateur » (“a working version with the computer’s voice”) With the placeholder voice, only if you ask for it. « juste la scène 3 », « en GIF », « dans un téléphone », « un PDF des scènes », « en boucle » (“just scene 3”, “as a GIF”, “in a phone”, “a PDF of the scenes”, “looping”) The other outputs (see the table above). « un texte pour accompagner la vidéo » (“a text to go with the video”) Pulse Motion writes sortie/<titre>-textes.md ; it offers it to you by itself after an export for social media or email. Before starting, Pulse Motion tells you how long it will take; during the export, it tracks the time remaining."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"What it produces","x":"File What it is for sortie/<titre>.mp4 The video for the web, to publish or send. The title is written in lower case, without accents or spaces: “La chaîne du froid” gives la-chaine-du-froid.mp4 . sortie/<titre>-<usage>.mp4 The other uses, each with its own file: -reseaux , -presentation , -master , -courriel , -brouillon . sortie/<titre>.html The animated page, already there: it opens offline, ready to put on your website. sortie/<titre>.srt and .vtt With subtitles: to upload to YouTube, LinkedIn or a training platform. sortie/<titre>-couverture.jpg The thumbnail image, without subtitles. It is also built into the MP4: players and file explorers show it. sortie/<titre>-chapitres.txt and .vtt If you have chapters: the text to paste into the YouTube description, and the file for a training platform. sortie/<titre>-credits.txt The credits: to read through before publishing. sortie/<titre>-<scène>.mp4 , .gif , -telephone.mp4 , -transparent.mov or .webm , -planches.pdf , sortie/images/<titre>/ The other outputs, when you ask for them. Pulse Motion finishes with a summary: the use, the length, the file size, the sound (or « muette », silent), the export mode, and each file with its role."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Errors come first. If errors remain, Pulse Motion tells you plainly and offers to fix them first with /pulse-motion:retoucher . Simple warnings do not block the export: it mentions them in one sentence. The placeholder voice is never published by mistake. If a scene still speaks with the computer’s voice, Pulse Motion offers to record your voice first. If you choose « une version de travail » (a working version), it does not ask again as long as that voice doesn’t change, and the file is called <titre>-version-de-travail.mp4 : the final video is not touched. ffmpeg is only installed with your agreement , and only with your system’s official command (winget on Windows, Homebrew on macOS). No incomplete files. If the export fails, no half-written MP4 is left in sortie/ . An email version that would exceed the stated size is never written. A scene with an error stops the export. If a scene’s script fails during the export, Pulse Motion stops and tells you which one, and at roughly what moment. Warning: a flash more than three times per second over a large part of the picture is an error , not a warning: it can trigger an epileptic seizure. Pulse Motion offers to soften it before exporting."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"“Chrome ou Edge introuvable” (Chrome or Edge not found) The export needs one of the two. Install Chrome or Edge (see the requirements ). Another option: open the preview in one of them and click the MP4 button in the playback bar. The video is exported silent, or without the music A sound file could not be read. Pulse Motion tells you in a warning: the video is exported without the damaged source. Give the file again (music or voice), then run the export again. The export was done “in the browser” when I wanted the high-fidelity export ffmpeg was not installed, or its version is too old. Pulse Motion offers to install it for next time. See ffmpeg . The export stopped halfway Just run /pulse-motion:exporter again: the parts already done are picked up. The file is too big for an email Ask for « une version pour l'envoyer par courriel » (“a version to send by email”): it stays under 10 MB, or under the size you say. If the video is too long, Pulse Motion suggests a larger size or a single scene. “Un export de cette vidéo est déjà en cours” (An export of this video is already running) Another export of the same video is still running (the message says since what time): wait for it to finish, then run it again."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:exporter pulse-motion controler . La vidéo est prête à exporter. Une alerte : un texte de la scène 4 est un peu près du bord, sans gravité. ffmpeg est un petit logiciel libre qui fabrique la vidéo en haute qualité, avec un son propre ; il s'installe une fois. → Installer ffmpeg (Recommandé) / Exporter sans, dans le navigateur Installer ffmpeg winget install --id Gyan.FFmpeg -e --accept-source-agreements --accept-package-agreements À quoi va servir la vidéo ? → Pour le web (Recommandé) / Pour les réseaux sociaux / Pour l'envoyer par courriel / Pour la projeter en réunion Pour le web pulse-motion exporter . --pour web ✅ Fait : la vidéo « La chaîne du froid » est exportée en MP4 pour le web (1 min 04, avec le son, export fidèle). 📄 Fichiers : sortie/la-chaine-du-froid.mp4 (à publier), la page animée et les sous-titres .srt et .vtt. In English You: /pulse-motion:exporter Claude: The video is ready to export. One warning: a text in scene 4 is a little close to the edge, nothing serious. ffmpeg is a small free program that makes the video in high quality, with clean sound; it is installed once. → Install ffmpeg (Recommended) / Export without it, in the browser You: Install ffmpeg Claude: What will the video be used for? → For the web (Recommended) / For social media / To send by email / To project in a meeting You: For the web Claude: ✅ Done: the video “La chaîne du froid” is exported as an MP4 for the web (1 min 04 s, with sound, high-fidelity export). 📄 Files: sortie/la-chaine-du-froid.mp4 (to publish), the animated page and the .srt and .vtt subtitles. /pulse-motion:exporter La vidéo parle encore avec la voix de l'ordinateur, qui sert seulement à caler l'animation. → Enregistrer ma voix d'abord (Recommandé) / Exporter une version de travail avec la voix de l'ordinateur Enregistrer ma voix d'abord Dans l'aperçu, cliquez sur « Voix » : le texte défile, vous parlez, vous réécoutez, vous gardez ou refaites. Relancez ensuite /pulse-motion:exporter. In English You: /pulse-motion:exporter Claude: The video still speaks with the computer’s voice, which is only there to time the animation. → Record my voice first (Recommended) / Export a working version with the computer’s voice You: Record my voice first Claude: In the preview, click “Voix” (Voice): the text scrolls, you speak, you listen back, you keep it or do it again. Then run /pulse-motion:exporter again."},{"u":"en/commandes/exporter.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:exporter","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher : fix things before exporting, then export again. /pulse-motion:creer : the next video."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"","x":"Step 1 · Create /pulse-motion:filmer Film a web app or a website for a tutorial, or start from a video or screenshots of your screen (« filme comment on crée une facture sur app.exemple.fr » (“film how to create an invoice on app.exemple.fr”), « j'ai enregistré mon écran » (“I’ve recorded my screen”), « floute les noms des clients » (“blur the customers’ names”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Description","x":"This is the command for software tutorials : showing how to do something in an app (“we go to Invoices, click New invoice, type the customer, save”). You describe the journey in your own words; Pulse Motion does it itself in the app, step by step, and films it. You describe the journey , as you would to a colleague, and give the starting address. Pulse Motion reads it back to you as numbered sentences: you approve or correct it. For a live website, it asks for your consent , naming the site. If the app asks for a login, you sign in yourself , in a window that opens on your screen. It films : an image of each state of the app, the short reactions after each click, and the position of each click. A few seconds per step. It looks at the result with you : a contact sheet, one image per step. It points out anything that looks personal and offers to blur it. You choose the take you keep, or you film again. The footage goes into the video : one scene for one to three steps. The pointer, clicks, zooms and spotlight are added by Pulse Motion, after filming. It can also start from a video of your screen (an installed program, filmed with your computer’s own tool), an animation (a GIF) or screenshots : the same pointer, zooms and spotlight go on top. You can run it on its own, to add footage to a video that already exists. /pulse-motion:creer also calls it by itself for a tutorial subject."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Why use it","x":"Everything can be edited without filming again. The pointer, zooms, spotlight and the time between two clicks are added afterwards: « le pointeur va trop vite » (“the pointer is going too fast”), « zoome sur le bouton » (“zoom in on the button”) take one sentence. The footage is kept with the video. Once filmed, the app is never visited again: neither the preview nor the export needs the internet, and two exports give exactly the same images. Personal data is blurred in the images themselves , at filming time: email addresses, phone numbers, IBANs, card numbers. No sharp image is kept. Your password stays yours. Pulse Motion never types it, never sees it and never writes it anywhere, even if you give it in the conversation. A failed take can be redone. Each filming is a new take; you choose the one that goes into the video."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise For a new tutorial video : run /pulse-motion:creer instead, with your subject (« un tutoriel : comment créer une facture dans notre logiciel » (“a tutorial: how to create an invoice in our software”)). It starts from the “Software tutorial” template, asks you what to show, then films with you before writing the timeline. To add footage to an existing video : run /pulse-motion:filmer in the video’s folder, then /pulse-motion:retoucher places the scene. To try it out safely : « montre-moi sur l'appli d'essai » (“show me on the test app”). Facturo, a small invoicing app that comes with Pulse Motion, runs on your computer; everything in it is fake."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:filmer Say what you want to film right after the name, or in the conversation. /pulse-motion:filmer comment on crée une facture sur app.exemple.fr “how to create an invoice on app.exemple.fr” /pulse-motion:filmer montre-moi sur l'appli d'essai “show me on the test app” /pulse-motion:filmer j'ai enregistré mon écran “I’ve recorded my screen” What you can ask You say What happens « filme comment on crée une facture sur app.exemple.fr » (“film how to create an invoice on app.exemple.fr”) Pulse Motion has you describe the journey, reads it back as numbered sentences, then asks for your consent for this site before filming. « montre-moi sur l'appli d'essai » (“show me on the test app”) A sample journey on Facturo (creating an invoice), to review, then to film. No consent asked: nothing leaves your computer. « je dois d'abord me connecter » (“I need to sign in first”) A window opens on the site. You sign in as usual, go to the starting page, then say « c'est fait » (“done”): filming carries on in the same window. « floute les noms des clients » (“blur the customers’ names”), « floute la colonne Client » (“blur the Customer column”) Pulse Motion advises filming again with this blur, applied from the start of filming. It can also blur an area of the take already made, permanently. « ne floute pas les adresses, elles sont fausses » (“don’t blur the addresses, they’re fake”) Addresses are no longer blurred automatically in the next take. Passwords always stay blurred. « refilme » (“film it again”), « reprends la première prise » (“go back to the first take”) A new take, or the previous take put back into the video. « j'ai enregistré mon écran » (“I’ve recorded my screen”), « voici un GIF de la manipulation » (“here’s a GIF of the steps”) Pulse Motion turns the video or animation into footage, with no internet, and shows you one image per second so you can place the clicks and zooms. « je veux montrer Excel » (“I want to show Excel”), « c'est un logiciel installé » (“it’s an installed program”) It explains how to record your screen with your computer’s own tool, then imports the video. « oublie ma connexion à app.exemple.fr » (“forget my sign-in to app.exemple.fr”) What the Pulse Motion browser was keeping for this site (its sign-in) is erased from your computer. Once the footage is in the video, edits go through /pulse-motion:retoucher : « le pointeur va trop vite » (“the pointer is going too fast”), « clique quand je dis enregistrez » (“click when I say save”), « suis les clics » (“follow the clicks”), « mets un projecteur sur le total » (“put a spotlight on the total”), « mets-le dans un téléphone » (“put it in a phone”), « accélère la saisie » (“speed up the typing”)."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"What it produces","x":"The footage , stored in the video’s folder, under films/ : the images of each step, already blurred, and the position of each click. Each filming adds a take there. A contact sheet per take : one image per step, with the click target and the number of blurred areas. Pulse Motion looks at it with you. Filmed scenes in the video, one for one to three steps, shown in a browser frame (or a phone, tablet or window frame). What is kept on your computer, and what isn’t, is explained in the Privacy section of the concepts."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"The clicks are real. Pulse Motion really carries out the actions in the app: a saved invoice is saved, a sent message is sent, and nothing is undone afterwards. Use a demo account if you have one. The automatic blur doesn’t recognise everything. It spots email addresses, phone numbers, IBANs, card and social security numbers, address and date-of-birth fields. Names, faces or amounts are pointed out by Pulse Motion when it looks at the contact sheet, and you decide. A blur added afterwards is permanent in that take: to remove it, you have to film again. A video already exported before that blur keeps the sharp image: export it again. One site per filming. Your consent applies to that site; if the page tries to go elsewhere, navigation is blocked and Pulse Motion tells you. Don’t touch the window while filming , when it is visible (after signing in): a few seconds per step. Tip: start with the test app (« montre-moi sur l'appli d'essai » (“show me on the test app”)): you see the whole journey, signing in and blurring included, without touching a real app."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion can’t find the button or the field The take stops at that step. Pulse Motion tells you what it sees instead, and shows you the image. Describe the target differently, using the text actually displayed (« le bouton Créer » (“the Create button”)), its position (« le deuxième Modifier » (“the second Edit”)) or where it is (« dans la fenêtre » (“in the window”)), then film again. Actions already carried out are not undone. The page asks me to sign in again The sign-in has expired, or the site asks for a login that Pulse Motion doesn’t have. Reply « je dois d'abord me connecter » (“I need to sign in first”): the window opens, you sign in, then you say « c'est fait » (“done”). You can’t see the page loading By default, Pulse Motion moves from one page to the next with a fade. Say « on ne voit pas le chargement » (“you can’t see it loading”): the next take keeps the loading images. My screen recording is refused Its format can’t be read by your computer’s browser. Install ffmpeg with /pulse-motion:outils , then give the file again. My screen recording is longer than 3 minutes Pulse Motion asks you which part to keep: the start and end, in minutes and seconds (« de 0:12 à 1:05 » (“from 0:12 to 1:05”)). Pulse Motion warns about a heavy video Above 25 MB of images in the page, it tells you before the export. Delete the takes you don’t need, shorten the reactions, or show fewer steps per scene. « Chrome ou Edge est introuvable » (“Chrome or Edge can’t be found”) Filming goes through Chrome or Edge. Install one of the two, then check with /pulse-motion:outils . See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:filmer montre-moi sur l'appli d'essai Voici le parcours, sur Facturo, l'application d'essai : 1. Cliquer sur « Nouvelle facture » 2. Dans « Client », taper « Dupont SARL » 3. Choisir « Virement » dans « Mode de paiement » 4. Cliquer sur « Enregistrer » 5. Confirmer dans la fenêtre 6. Attendre que « Facture enregistrée » s'affiche → C'est bien ça (Recommandé) / Je corrige C'est bien ça Je filme. Ne touchez à rien, c'est l'affaire de quelques secondes par étape… Voici la planche : une image par étape. L'adresse électronique du client est floutée. → Je garde cette prise (Recommandé) / Je refilme Je garde cette prise ✅ Fait : la prise est gardée, 6 étapes, 14 secondes de film. ➡️ Ensuite : je l'ajoute à la vidéo avec /pulse-motion:retoucher, ou nous créons un tutoriel avec /pulse-motion:creer. In English You: /pulse-motion:filmer show me on the test app Claude: Here is the journey, on Facturo, the test app: 1. Click “Nouvelle facture” (New invoice). 2. In “Client” (Customer), type “Dupont SARL”. 3. Choose “Virement” (Bank transfer) in “Mode de paiement” (Payment method). 4. Click “Enregistrer” (Save). 5. Confirm in the window. 6. Wait for “Facture enregistrée” (Invoice saved) to appear. → That’s right (Recommended) / I’ll correct it You: That’s right Claude: I’m filming. Don’t touch anything, it only takes a few seconds per step… Here is the contact sheet: one image per step. The customer’s email address is blurred. → I’ll keep this take (Recommended) / I’ll film again You: I’ll keep this take Claude: ✅ Done: the take is kept, 6 steps, 14 seconds of footage. ➡️ Next: I add it to the video with /pulse-motion:retoucher, or we create a tutorial with /pulse-motion:creer. /pulse-motion:filmer comment on ajoute un client sur app.exemple.fr Pour filmer, Pulse Motion va ouvrir app.exemple.fr sur cet ordinateur et y faire les clics pour de vrai. Utilisez un compte de démonstration si vous en avez un. Si vous vous connectez, ce navigateur garde la connexion du site sur cet ordinateur, jamais votre mot de passe. → Oui, filmer (Recommandé) / Je dois d'abord me connecter / Non Je dois d'abord me connecter Une fenêtre s'est ouverte sur app.exemple.fr. Connectez-vous comme d'habitude, allez sur la page de départ, puis dites-moi « c'est fait ». Je ne vois pas ce que vous tapez. C'est fait Ne touchez pas à la fenêtre pendant le tournage, environ 40 secondes… À l'étape 4, je vois des noms de clients dans le tableau. Je les floute ? → Flouter et refilmer (Recommandé) / Flouter cette prise / C'est bon In English You: /pulse-motion:filmer how to add a customer on app.exemple.fr Claude: To film, Pulse Motion will open app.exemple.fr on this computer and really click through it. Use a demo account if you have one. If you sign in, this browser keeps the site’s sign-in on this computer, never your password. → Yes, film (Recommended) / I need to sign in first / No You: I need to sign in first Claude: A window has opened on app.exemple.fr. Sign in as usual, go to the starting page, then tell me “done”. I can’t see what you type. You: Done Claude: Don’t touch the window while filming, about 40 seconds… At step 4, I can see customer names in the table. Shall I blur them? → Blur and film again (Recommended) / Blur this take / It’s fine /pulse-motion:filmer j'ai enregistré mon écran pour montrer un tableau Excel Où est votre fichier ? Donnez son chemin, ou glissez-le ici. C:\\Users\\Marie\\Videos\\tableau-ventes.mp4 C'est fait : 48 secondes, une image par seconde sur la planche. Votre voix est dans la vidéo. → Garder votre voix de la vidéo (Recommandé) / Couper le son Où voulez-vous un clic, un zoom ou le projecteur ? Dites-le avec la seconde (« à 12 s, zoome sur le total »). In English You: /pulse-motion:filmer I’ve recorded my screen to show an Excel spreadsheet Claude: Where is your file? Give its path, or drag it here. You: C:\\Users\\Marie\\Videos\\tableau-ventes.mp4 Claude: Done: 48 seconds, one image per second on the contact sheet. Your voice is in the video. → Keep your voice from the video (Recommended) / Mute the sound. Where would you like a click, a zoom or the spotlight? Give the second (“at 12 s, zoom in on the total”)."},{"u":"en/commandes/filmer.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:filmer","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:creer : a complete tutorial, from the “Software tutorial” template. /pulse-motion:retoucher : place the footage in the video, adjust the pointer, zooms, spotlight and frame. /pulse-motion:voix : record your voice, or keep the one from your screen recording. /pulse-motion:outils : install ffmpeg to import every video format. /pulse-motion:exporter : a filmed video exports like any other. Step by step, with the test app: the Filmer un logiciel tutorial (in French)."},{"u":"en/commandes/index.html","a":"","t":"The commands","s":"","x":"13 commands, 4 steps The commands You type each command in Claude Code, starting with /pulse-motion: . Then you answer the questions in your own words: Pulse Motion runs everything else itself and never shows you any code. Not sure which one? Quelle commande pour… ? (in French) guides you in two or three questions. Search for a command"},{"u":"en/commandes/index.html","a":"etape-creer","t":"The commands","s":"Step 1 Create","x":"/pulse-motion:creer Create a motion design video in a few questions, approve its timeline, then watch it in your browser. Music, voice and subtitles optional. /pulse-motion:filmer Film a web app for a tutorial, step by step, or start from a recording of your screen: pointer, clicks, zooms and spotlight added afterwards, personal data blurred. /pulse-motion:reprendre Pick up the current video where it stopped: a timeline to approve, scenes to write, a preview to reopen. /pulse-motion:outils Check that the computer is ready and install what is missing, with your consent: ffmpeg for export, transcription, Python. Never touches a video."},{"u":"en/commandes/index.html","a":"etape-retoucher","t":"The commands","s":"Step 2 Edit","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher Edit the video in one sentence (« ralentis la scène 3 » (“slow down scene 3”), « change le titre » (“change the title”), « baisse la musique » (“turn the music down”), « annule » (“undo”)) and see it straight away in the preview. /pulse-motion:voix Record your voice in the preview’s studio, import a recording, add a placeholder voice, realign the words, or play the music on its own."},{"u":"en/commandes/index.html","a":"etape-exporter","t":"The commands","s":"Step 3 Export","x":"/pulse-motion:exporter Export the video as an MP4 tuned for its use (web, social media, presentation, master, email, draft), made on your computer, ready to publish or send."},{"u":"en/commandes/index.html","a":"etape-personnaliser","t":"The commands","s":"Step 4 Customise","x":"/pulse-motion:style Create your own video style (« un style papercraft aux couleurs pop-art » (“a papercraft style in pop-art colours”)): look, colours, fonts and moves, checked before use. /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage Draw a character for the video (« un cuisinier rondouillard avec une toque » (“a chubby chef in a toque”)), or edit its drawing, and see it move. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage Create or change a pose, a move or an expression, and see it from several angles. Put a character in a scene and make it talk. /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage Animate a character illustration you provide (Illustrator, Inkscape, Figma…) by cutting it up onto the Pulse Motion skeleton. /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages Open the character workshop to pose and animate a character with the mouse, or fix how an illustration was cut up. /pulse-motion:atelier-particules Open the particle effects workshop to tune an effect while you watch it: sparks, snow, confetti… No command matches. Try another word, or Quelle commande pour… ? (in French)"},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"","x":"Step 1 · Create /pulse-motion:outils Check that the computer is ready for videos and install what is missing (ffmpeg, transcription, Python), with your consent. Never creates or changes a video."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Description","x":"Pulse Motion relies on a few programs on your computer. This command simply tells you what is ready and what is missing, then installs what is missing, with your consent . It works in any folder, with or without a video. It starts with the installed version of Pulse Motion (« Pulse Motion 0.16.0 est installé » (“Pulse Motion 0.16.0 is installed”)), then shows a table: Tool What it is for Chrome or Edge Watching the video, checking it, exporting it. ffmpeg A high-quality MP4 with clean sound. Without it, the export is done in the browser. Python Needed for transcription. Transcription Aligning the subtitles and animations with the actual words of your voice. Optional. The computer’s voice The placeholder voice, before you record your own. The synthetic voice A French voice generated on the computer, which can be delivered as it is. Optional, installed only if you ask for it. Each missing tool is followed by what you can do, for example: say « installe ffmpeg » (“install ffmpeg”)."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Why use it","x":"You can see at a glance whether everything is ready , before a first export or a first recording. Nothing is installed without your consent. Pulse Motion asks you « Installer (Recommandé) » (Install (Recommended)) or « Pas maintenant » (Not now), one installation at a time. You have nothing to type on Windows and Mac: Pulse Motion runs the installation itself. On Linux, it gives you the line to type. Your videos aren’t touched. The tools are installed on the computer; no video is created or changed."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise Before your first video , once the plugin is installed (see the requirements ). Before /pulse-motion:exporter , to install ffmpeg and get a high-fidelity export. Before /pulse-motion:voix , to install transcription if you want to import a recording of the whole video or realign the words. When a message mentions a missing tool. When something isn’t working : « signale un problème » (“report a problem”). Claude prepares a summary (version, system, tools, latest errors), with nothing from your video, and saves it in a file. With your consent, it opens the reporting page, already filled in: you read it over and send it yourself (a GitHub account is needed; otherwise, send the file by email). Nothing is ever sent on your behalf. When you change computer : « emporte mes chartes sur mon autre ordinateur » (“take my brand kits to my other computer”). Your personal library (brand kits, styles, effects, characters, preferences) goes into a single file, which you copy yourself."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:outils On its own, it checks the computer. To install a tool, say so right after the name or in the conversation. /pulse-motion:outils installe ffmpeg “install ffmpeg” /pulse-motion:outils installe la transcription “install transcription” What you can ask You say What happens « vérifie mon ordinateur » (“check my computer”), « est-ce que tout est prêt pour l'export ? » (“is everything ready for export?”) The Pulse Motion version and the table of tools, ready or missing. « installe ffmpeg » (“install ffmpeg”) Pulse Motion explains in one sentence what it is for, asks for your consent, installs it, then checks that it is really there. « installe la transcription » (“install transcription”) Three choices: « Installer (Recommandé) » (Install (Recommended)), about 800 MB to download once; « Version légère » (Light version), 250 MB, slightly less accurate; « Pas maintenant » (Not now). If Python is missing, it is installed first, with your consent. « installe la voix de synthèse » (“install the synthetic voice”) Pulse Motion says in one sentence what it is (a single French voice, female, less natural than a real voice) and its size (about 300 MB, or 550 MB for the more faithful version), then asks for your consent. Nothing is downloaded without it. It needs Python, version 3.10 to 3.13. « emporte mes chartes sur mon autre ordinateur » (“take my brand kits to my other computer”), « emporte ma bibliothèque » (“take my library with me”) Pulse Motion tells you what your library contains, then offers « Tout emporter (Recommandé) » (Take everything (Recommended)) or « Choisir » (Choose). It turns it into a ZIP file, saved in your Documents, which you copy like any other (USB stick, email). Sign-ins to the sites you have filmed never leave. « importe ma bibliothèque » (“import my library”) (with the ZIP file) Pulse Motion first tells you what is new, what you already have, and what has a name that is already taken. Nothing already on the computer is replaced without your consent: for each taken name, « Garder le mien (Recommandé) » (Keep mine (Recommended)) or « Prendre celui de l'archive » (Take the one from the archive). The preferences it remembered for you are added to yours. « installe Python » (“install Python”) Only if it is missing or too old: Pulse Motion asks for your consent, installs it, then checks. The same tools, installed by hand, are described in the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"What it produces","x":"An overview in plain words: what is ready, what is missing, and how to get it. The tools installed on the computer , if you accept: ffmpeg, Python, transcription. Nothing in your video changes. What to do next : the action that was waiting for the tool (the export, recording a voice), otherwise creating a video with /pulse-motion:creer ."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"Transcription is a big download. About 800 MB (250 MB for the light version), just once. Installation takes a few minutes: Pulse Motion warns you and waits until it is finished. A tool you have just installed may stay invisible. The computer only sees a new program when Claude Code restarts. Restart it, then ask « vérifie mon ordinateur » (“check my computer”) again. On Linux, you type the installation line yourself , in a terminal, because it asks for your password. Pulse Motion waits for you to tell it you’re done, then checks. The browser and the microphone can’t be installed from here. Chrome or Edge are installed from their website. The microphone is tested in the voice studio: ask « j'enregistre la scène 1 » (“I’m recording scene 1”) when a video is open. Tip: ffmpeg and transcription are optional. Without ffmpeg, the export is done in the browser; without transcription, the words of the voice are aligned as well as possible. Everything else works."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"ffmpeg is still missing after installation The computer only sees it when Claude Code restarts. Close Claude Code, start it again, then ask « vérifie mon ordinateur » (“check my computer”) again. Python still can’t be found Same cause: restart Claude Code to finish the installation, then ask « installe la transcription » (“install transcription”) again. « Python est trop ancien » (“Python is too old”) Transcription needs a more recent version. Ask « installe Python » (“install Python”): Pulse Motion installs the version required, with your consent. Installing transcription failed Pulse Motion tells you why, in plain words. Everything works without it: the words of the voice are aligned as well as possible. You can try again later. Chrome or Edge are reported missing Install one of the two from its website, then ask « vérifie mon ordinateur » (“check my computer”) again. See Chrome or Edge . « Ce nom est déjà pris » (“This name is already taken”) when importing the library On this computer, you already have a brand kit, a style, an effect or a character with the same name, but different. Nothing has been replaced: choose whether to keep yours or take the one from the archive. The rest of the library is imported. “node” not found Node.js isn’t installed: Pulse Motion can’t check anything without it. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org (installer), then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:outils pulse-motion outils Pulse Motion 0.16.0 est installé. ✅ Chrome ou Edge : regarder la vidéo, la contrôler, l'exporter ❌ ffmpeg : un MP4 de qualité avec un son propre (sans lui, l'export se fait dans le navigateur). Dites « installe ffmpeg ». ✅ Python : nécessaire à la transcription ❌ Transcription : caler les sous-titres et les animations sur les vrais mots de la voix (facultative). Dites « installe la transcription ». ✅ Voix de l'ordinateur : la voix provisoire, avant d'enregistrer la vôtre In English You: /pulse-motion:outils Claude: Pulse Motion 0.16.0 is installed. ✅ Chrome or Edge: watching the video, checking it, exporting it. ❌ ffmpeg: a high-quality MP4 with clean sound (without it, the export is done in the browser). Say “install ffmpeg”. ✅ Python: needed for transcription. ❌ Transcription: aligning the subtitles and animations with the actual words of the voice (optional). Say “install transcription”. ✅ Computer’s voice: the placeholder voice, before you record your own /pulse-motion:outils installe la transcription pulse-motion whisper La transcription cale les sous-titres et les animations sur les vrais mots de votre voix ; environ 800 Mo à télécharger une fois. → Installer (Recommandé) / Version légère (250 Mo, un peu moins précise) / Pas maintenant Installer pulse-motion whisper installer L'installation prend quelques minutes, je vous préviens dès qu'elle est finie. ✅ Fait : la transcription est installée. Votre vidéo a déjà des prises de voix : voulez-vous que je recale leurs mots ? 📄 Fichiers : rien dans votre vidéo n'a changé ; les outils sont installés sur l'ordinateur. In English You: /pulse-motion:outils install transcription Claude: Transcription aligns the subtitles and animations with the actual words of your voice; about 800 MB to download once. → Install (Recommended) / Light version (250 MB, slightly less accurate) / Not now You: Install Claude: Installation takes a few minutes, I’ll let you know as soon as it’s finished. Claude: ✅ Done: transcription is installed. Your video already has voice takes: would you like me to realign their words? 📄 Files: nothing in your video has changed; the tools are installed on the computer."},{"u":"en/commandes/outils.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:outils","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:exporter : the high-fidelity export, with ffmpeg. /pulse-motion:voix : import a recording of the whole video, realign the words. /pulse-motion:creer : create a video once the computer is ready."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"","x":"Step 1 · Create /pulse-motion:reprendre Pick up the current video where it stopped."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Description","x":"Closed VS Code halfway through a video? A conversation got interrupted? This command reads the state of the video in the folder again, tells you in one sentence where it stands, then carries on at exactly the right step. It also reads your personal glossary (in French): tool words that have already been explained to you aren’t explained again. Where the video was What Pulse Motion does No video in the folder Suggests /pulse-motion:creer . The timeline has errors Fixes them, then shows you the timeline. The timeline is waiting for your approval Shows it to you and asks for your approval. Scenes are missing, or are still only drafts Opens the preview on the animated draft if it is closed, has the animators write the scenes, then checks the video. The style is waiting for your approval (guided path) Shows you the style comparison again. The final video is waiting for your approval (guided path) Opens the preview and asks for your approval. The video is ready Reopens the preview if it is closed, then suggests an edit."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Why use it","x":"Nothing is lost. The whole state of the video is in its folder: you can shut the computer down at any step. You don’t have to remember. Pulse Motion knows which step comes next and offers it to you. Your brand kit stays up to date. If the saved brand kit used by this video has changed since (another colour, a new logo), Pulse Motion points it out and offers to apply it again. Nothing changes without your approval."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After a break , when you reopen a video’s folder. When Pulse Motion reminds you. At the start of each session, it checks whether the folder holds a video in progress and offers to pick it up. After restarting Claude Code , for example to finish installing transcription. To find your videos : « quelles sont mes vidéos ? » (“what are my videos?”), « retrouve ma vidéo sur la chaîne du froid » (“find my video about the cold chain”). Claude shows you the list of your videos, wherever they are on the computer: where each one stands, its length, its style and its MP4. To edit one, open Claude Code in its folder."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:reprendre It takes nothing after the name. Run it in the video’s folder: in VS Code, File menu → Open Folder… , then open the Claude Code panel."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"What it produces","x":"Nothing new on its own: it picks up the work of /pulse-motion:creer where it stopped, and so produces the same files (timeline, scenes, animated page in sortie/ )."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"It changes nothing before talking to you. It first tells you where the video stands. One folder, one video. Make sure you open the video’s folder, not the folder that contains it: otherwise Pulse Motion finds no video."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion says there is no video here The folder you opened isn’t the video’s folder. It must contain a deroule.json file. Open the right folder with File → Open Folder… , then run the command again. My saved brand kit no longer exists It has been deleted from your computer. The video keeps its own copy of the brand kit, in its charte/ folder: nothing is broken. Pulse Motion simply lets you know."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:reprendre pulse-motion etat . La vidéo « La chaîne du froid » attend encore votre accord sur son déroulé. Le voici : 6 scènes, 1 min 03. → Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible, rassurant (Recommandé) / Je veux changer quelque chose In English You: /pulse-motion:reprendre Claude: The video “La chaîne du froid” is still waiting for your approval of its timeline. Here it is: 6 scenes, 1 min 03 s. → I approve, with the calm, readable, reassuring style (Recommended) / I want to change something /pulse-motion:reprendre La vidéo « La chaîne du froid » est prête. J'ai rouvert l'aperçu dans votre navigateur. Une retouche ? Décrivez-la en une phrase, par exemple « ralentis la scène 3 ». In English You: /pulse-motion:reprendre Claude: The video “La chaîne du froid” is ready. I’ve reopened the preview in your browser. An edit? Describe it in one sentence, for example “slow down scene 3”."},{"u":"en/commandes/reprendre.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:reprendre","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:creer : the steps that reprendre carries on. /pulse-motion:retoucher : change a finished video."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"","x":"Step 2 · Edit /pulse-motion:retoucher Edit the video in one sentence (« ralentis la scène 3 » (“slow down scene 3”), « change le titre » (“change the title”), « annule » (“undo”)) and see the result straight away."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Description","x":"You’re watching the video in the preview and something isn’t right: say so in one sentence. Pulse Motion makes the smallest change that answers your sentence, without “improving” anything else along the way. It checks the video, then the preview reloads by itself, at the same position. Every edit is saved before it is made . If you don’t like the result, « annule » (“undo”) goes back to the previous state."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Why use it","x":"You talk as you would to a video editor. No software to learn: « le chiffre doit rebondir » (“the number should bounce”), « mets la liste à droite » (“put the list on the right”). You see the result within a second. The preview stays open and reloads at the same position. You can try anything. « annule » (“undo”) goes back, « rétablis » (“redo”) reverses the undo; the last 20 edits are kept, and a version can be saved under a name (« envoyée au client » (“sent to the client”)) so you can get it back whenever you like. You show instead of describing. In the preview, click « Remarque » (Note), then on the image where you want: the note is kept with the exact moment. Claude deals with them one by one and only marks a note as done after looking at the new image. Your impressions become choices. « ça fait cheap » (“it looks cheap”), « plus premium » (“more premium”): Pulse Motion suggests two to four options, in plain words, and applies nothing without your answer. An ambiguous sentence leads to a question, not a mistake. « Ralentis » (“Slow down”)? Pulse Motion asks you which scene, with the most likely answer first."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:creer , while watching the video in the preview. Before /pulse-motion:exporter , to fix an error reported by the check. After an export , for a new version: edit, then export again. For your lasting preferences : « jamais de rebond » (“never any bounce”), « toujours mon logo à la fin » (“always my logo at the end”). Pulse Motion asks whether it should remember this for your next videos; « quelles sont mes préférences ? » (“what are my preferences?”) shows them, « oublie ma préférence sur les rebonds » (“forget my preference about bounces”) removes one. What you ask for right now always comes first. To keep an effect or a character : « garde cet effet de neige pour mes prochaines vidéos » (“keep this snow effect for my next videos”), « garde ce personnage » (“keep this character”). It joins your personal library ; another video uses it by copying it, without ever changing this one. To use your website’s colours : « prends les couleurs de notre site » (“use our website’s colours”). With your consent, Claude reads the home page and suggests two readable colours, the logo and the site’s fonts; nothing is changed before you answer, and no font file from the site is copied. To imitate an animation you like : « fais la scène 2 comme ce GIF » (“make scene 2 like this GIF”), « même rythme que cette pub » (“same pace as this ad”). Put the GIF or the video in your video’s folder. Pulse Motion looks at it frame by frame and measures each movement (when it starts, how long it lasts, how it slows down), then writes your scene with those figures and shows you both side by side. « Dans l’esprit de » (“in the spirit of”) keeps the overall pace; « fais pareil » (“do the same”) matches each movement to within a tenth of a second. For a map : « une carte de nos pays d’export » (“a map of our export countries”), « nos agences en France sur la carte » (“our branches in France on the map”). The countries of the world and the French regions (overseas ones included, as insets) come with Pulse Motion: nothing is loaded online. Countries take a colour, cities get a dot, and a line can link two cities. The borders are Natural Earth’s, as they are on the ground, without taking sides. To keep a piece of music : « garde cette musique pour mes prochaines vidéos » (“keep this music for my next videos”). Hand over the file (bought or free) and say its licence: Pulse Motion stores it in your personal library , measures its length and tempo, then offers it first when a video has the same mood. It never ships or downloads any music; the credit line to quote (CC BY licence) goes into the credits. To add a Lottie animation : « mets mon animation Lottie de fusée dans la scène 2 » (“put my Lottie rocket animation in scene 2”), « donne-lui nos couleurs » (“give it our colours”). Hand over the file (from LottieFiles or your designer): Pulse Motion copies it into your video’s folder, asks where it comes from and its licence for the credits, then starts it at the right moment. It never downloads an animation. If a part cannot be drawn (a text, a special effect), it tells you."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher [la retouche en une phrase] “[the edit in one sentence]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. With no sentence, Pulse Motion asks you what to change. /pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 “slow down scene 3” /pulse-motion:retoucher annule “undo” What you can ask What you want to change Example sentences A piece of text « change le titre » (“change the title”), « corrige la faute dans la scène 2 » (“fix the typo in scene 2”) The pace, a length « ralentis la scène 3 » (“slow down scene 3”), « accélère » (“speed up”), « laisse plus de temps à la fin » (“leave more time at the end”) The look of a scene « le chiffre doit rebondir » (“the number should bounce”), « mets la liste à droite » (“put the list on the right”) A particle effect « plus d'étincelles » (“more sparks”), « en bleu » (“in blue”), « fais-les partir autrement » (“make them start differently”), « ajoute des confettis à la fin » (“add confetti at the end”), « enlève les confettis » (“remove the confetti”) The style « passe en style éditorial » (“switch to the editorial style”), « quelque chose de plus chaleureux » (“something warmer”), « montre-moi les autres styles » (“show me the other styles”) (a page opens: each style plays the same little story, and you tick the one you like) The brand kit « mets mes couleurs » (“use my colours”), « prends les couleurs de mon logo » (“take the colours from my logo”), « applique la charte Dupont » (“apply the Dupont brand kit”), « change la police des titres » (“change the title font”), « passe en sombre » (“switch to dark”) The transition « change la transition » (“change the transition”), « coupe sèche entre les scènes 4 et 5 » (“hard cut between scenes 4 and 5”), « un whip pan entre la scène 2 et la 3 » (“a whip pan between scene 2 and scene 3”) A move by its name « un pop-in sur le titre » (“a pop-in on the title”), « le titre en machine à écrire » (“the title as a typewriter”), « mets un #001 sur le logo » (“put a #001 on the logo”), « quels mouvements existent ? » (“which moves are there?”); they are all in the move gallery A move chosen by watching it « montre-moi les mouvements » (“show me the moves”), « je veux un truc qui claque pour le titre » (“I want something punchy for the title”): a page opens, you tick, then « Envoyer à Claude » (Send to Claude) The voice « baisse la voix » (“turn the voice down”), « je réenregistre la scène 3 » (“I’m re-recording scene 3”), « j'ai un nouvel enregistrement » (“I have a new recording”), « change ce que je dis dans l'intro » (“change what I say in the intro”) The music « plus calme » (“calmer”), « plus entraînant » (“more upbeat”), « une autre variante » (“another variation”), « baisse la musique » (“turn the music down”), « enlève la musique » (“remove the music”), « coupe la musique pendant la scène 4 » (“cut the music during scene 4”), « la musique à fond sur la démo » (“music full blast on the demo”), « fais-moi écouter » (“let me hear it”) Sound effects « mets un son quand le titre arrive » (“add a sound when the title comes in”), « moins fort » (“quieter”), « enlève les bruitages » (“remove the sound effects”) The language, another version « passe la vidéo en anglais » (“switch the video to English”), « fais-en une version verticale pour TikTok » (“make a vertical version for TikTok”), « la même en anglais en gardant l'originale » (“the same one in English, keeping the original”): the second video is stored in the first one’s folder, which does not change; Pulse Motion keeps talking to you in French Subtitles « sous-titres en haut » (“subtitles at the top”), « mot par mot » (“word by word”), « enlève-les de l'image » (“take them off the picture”), « enlève les sous-titres » (“remove the subtitles”) The structure « ajoute une scène sur le lavage des mains » (“add a scene about handwashing”), « supprime la scène 4 » (“delete scene 4”) A character « dessine-moi un cuisinier » (“draw me a chef”), « il marche trop vite » (“he walks too fast”), « fais-lui une tête surprise » (“give him a surprised face”), « ouvre l'éditeur de personnage » (“open the character editor”): Pulse Motion follows the dedicated command, /pulse-motion:dessiner-personnage , /pulse-motion:decouper-personnage , /pulse-motion:animer-personnage or /pulse-motion:atelier-personnages An impression « ça fait cheap » (“it looks cheap”), « plus premium » (“more premium”), « ça fait diaporama » (“it looks like a slideshow”), « trop chargé » (“too busy”), « ça manque de punch » (“it lacks punch”): you are offered options, the recommended one first Your notes on the image « j'ai laissé des remarques » (“I’ve left some notes”) (or the preview’s « Envoyer à Claude » (Send to Claude) button) A quick setting « un peu plus vif partout » (“a bit livelier throughout”), « accent en bleu » (“blue accent”), « passe en sombre » (“switch to dark”), « baisse la musique » (“turn the music down”), « sous-titres mot par mot » (“word-by-word subtitles”), or the preview’s « Réglages » (Settings) button Going back « annule » (“undo”), « reviens en arrière » (“go back”), « rétablis » (“redo”), « finalement remets-le » (“actually, put it back”) Versions « garde cette version sous le nom envoyée au client » (“keep this version under the name sent to the client”), « reviens à la version envoyée au client » (“go back to the version sent to the client”), « quelles versions ai-je ? » (“which versions do I have?”), or the preview’s « Comparer » (Compare) button The sound settings are detailed on the Le son page (in French); styles and effects are in the catalogue . In the preview « Remarque » (Note) (or the N key): a click on the image pauses it and drops a pin; write your note, then « Garder la remarque » (Keep the note), « Sur cette image, sans épingle » (On this image, without a pin) or « Sur toute la vidéo » (On the whole video). Notes appear in colour on the playback bar (orange: to do, green: done). When you have finished: « Envoyer à Claude » (Send to Claude). A note that has been dealt with shows the before and after images side by side. « Réglages » (Settings) : accent colour, pace (calmer, normal, livelier), tone (light, dark, automatic), music (quieter, normal, louder, none) and subtitles (yes, no, word by word). The highlighted value is the video’s; a click writes it straight away, and « annule » (“undo”) reverses it. « Comparer » (Compare) : choose a saved version; both are shown side by side, driven by a single playback bar. The playback bar shows the shape of the voice (hover over it to see the words, click to jump there) and, when you hover over the start of a scene, a small image of the scene with its name. None of this appears in the delivered video or in the MP4."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"What it produces","x":"The updated video : sortie/<titre>.html , and the preview reloaded. A copy of the previous state , in the video’s hidden .historique/ folder (the last 20, plus your named versions, 20 at most), with the sentence for each edit. After a visual edit , a before / after sheet ( sortie/planche/avant-apres-<scène>.png ): the before image, the after image, and the differences in red. Your notes and their images, in sortie/remarques/ : « annule » (“undo”) doesn’t erase them. An updated check report ( sortie/controle.json ). Whatever the check fixed on its own is told to you in one sentence."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"A precise length or spelling is respected. If you ask for « la scène 3 dure 4 secondes » (“scene 3 lasts 4 seconds”) or a particular spelling, Pulse Motion checks without fixing anything on its own, so as not to undo what you asked for. It only points out what causes a problem. Changing the style rewrites some scenes. Ready-made scenes are copied over into the new style; freely composed scenes are rewritten by an animator. « annule » (“undo”) brings back the old style. The music follows the picture. A changed length or transition doesn’t throw the composed music out of step: it is remade to the new timing, each scene with its own section. Music from a file, on the other hand, doesn’t move: with its tempo, each scene has to start on a beat, and Pulse Motion adjusts the lengths itself. It imitates a style, never a work. The drawings, characters, logo, texts and music of a video you show are never taken, and the video itself never goes into yours: it is only used for measuring. Pulse Motion downloads nothing; for a website’s animation, it films the site first, with your consent. An “identical” copy is refused. Tip: one edit at a time. If you string three together in one sentence, « annule » (“undo”) will reverse all three at once."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"« Rien à rétablir » (“Nothing to redo”) « rétablis » (“redo”) reverses the last undo. A new edit made since then erases what could have been redone: to get back to a precise state, save it under a name before trying something else. « Vous gardez déjà 20 versions nommées » (“You’re already keeping 20 named versions”) Say which one to forget (« oublie la version brouillon » (“forget the draft version”)), then save the new one. « Aucune retouche à annuler » (“No edit to undo”) You have gone back to the very first saved state, or the video has never been edited. There is nothing older to restore. The preview didn’t reload It may have been closed. Pulse Motion reopens it if it is no longer active; otherwise, reload the browser page ( F5 , or Cmd + R on Mac). The text is too long and still overflows The check shrinks overflowing text down to 80% of its size, never below what stays readable. Beyond that, it has to be shortened: « raccourcis le titre de la scène 2 » (“shorten the title of scene 2”). The maximum lengths for each style are in the catalogue . Pulse Motion tells me a word in the voice can’t be found An animation starts on a word of your voice, but that word is no longer said (you changed the text or re-recorded). Pulse Motion suggests another word in the scene as the starting point."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 pulse-motion historique sauvegarder . ✅ Fait : la scène 3, « Les 4 maillons de la chaîne », se déroule plus lentement. L'aperçu s'est rechargé. In English You: /pulse-motion:retoucher slow down scene 3 Claude: ✅ Done: scene 3, “Les 4 maillons de la chaîne” (The 4 links in the chain), now plays more slowly. The preview has reloaded. /pulse-motion:retoucher le titre est trop petit Quel titre ? → Celui de la scène 1, « La chaîne du froid » (Recommandé) / Tous les titres / Un autre Celui de la scène 1 ✅ Fait : le titre de l'intro est plus grand. L'aperçu s'est rechargé. In English You: /pulse-motion:retoucher the title is too small Claude: Which title? → The one in scene 1, “La chaîne du froid” (Recommended) / All the titles / Another one You: The one in scene 1 Claude: ✅ Done: the intro title is bigger. The preview has reloaded. /pulse-motion:retoucher annule pulse-motion historique annuler . ✅ Fait : la vidéo est revenue à l'état d'avant la dernière retouche. In English You: /pulse-motion:retoucher undo Claude: ✅ Done: the video is back to how it was before the last edit."},{"u":"en/commandes/retoucher.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:retoucher","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:creer : create the video to edit. /pulse-motion:exporter : make the MP4 once the video is just how you want it. /pulse-motion:style : create a style of your own, then « passe en style… » (“switch to the … style”)."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"","x":"Step 4 · Customise /pulse-motion:style Create your own video style (“a papercraft style in pop-art colours”): look, colours, fonts and movement, checked before use."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Description","x":"Pulse Motion comes with six styles , and your brand kit (logo, colours, fonts) applies to them. If you want to go further, with a look that is yours alone, this command creates a personal style : its mood, its default colours, its fonts, its way of moving, and its nine ready-made scenes. The style is stored on your computer. It is then offered for all your videos, alongside the built-in styles."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Why use it","x":"Your videos look like no one else’s. Papercraft, retro poster, sketchbook: you describe the world, Pulse Motion turns it into a style. A consistent series. All your videos share the same look, without redoing the work each time. Checked before use. The style goes through the same checks as the built-in styles: readability, contrast, overflow in all four formats."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise At any time , outside a video: the style belongs to no video folder. Before /pulse-motion:creer , so that the style is offered to you from the very first question. Before /pulse-motion:retoucher , to switch an existing video to this style (« passe en style papercraft » (“switch to the papercraft style”)). Tip: you only want your colours and your logo? No need for a style: choose « mon logo et mes couleurs » (my logo and my colours) during /pulse-motion:creer , or ask /pulse-motion:retoucher for « prends les couleurs de mon logo » (“use the colours of my logo”)."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:style [le style voulu en une phrase] “[the style you want, in one sentence]” In square brackets [ ] : optional. Without a description, Pulse Motion asks you for one. /pulse-motion:style un style papercraft aux couleurs pop-art “a papercraft style in pop-art colours” A sentence works too: « crée mon propre style, sombre avec des touches néon » (“create my own style, dark with touches of neon”). To try one of the built-in styles or switch to another, that is an edit ( /pulse-motion:retoucher ). The interview One question at a time, each with suggested answers. Pulse Motion only asks what it cannot work out from your description. Question What you decide Personality Three words that describe the style, and what it must not suggest at any cost. References A work, a brand, a movement (« comme les affiches de Lichtenstein » (“like Lichtenstein’s posters”)). Colour Light background, dark, or both; understated with one accent, a bold colour, several colours, or colour everywhere. Fonts Two free families (Bunny Fonts), one for headings, one for text, checked before they are offered to you. Movement Lively or gentle, with or without bounces. Pulse Motion then starts from the closest built-in style: its layout is taken over, then transformed. You can adapt a style you like, or start from a blank page (Pulse Motion then tells you which style it is starting from). What you can change Group For example What it does Safe the colours, the fonts, the rhythm, the sound, a gesture added or swapped the style stays itself Changes its character the materials and rendering, the colour logic, the camera and the transitions possible; Pulse Motion tells you what the style loses Impossible the essence of the style, what makes it recognisable at a glance it would be a different style: Pulse Motion suggests a closer one Five changes at most per round (a font required by your brand kit does not count). Before writing, Pulse Motion shows you a summary: before, after, what it costs. Then a sheet opens: the starting style on the left, yours on the right, with the same little story (« la chaîne du froid » (“the cold chain”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"What it produces","x":"A style folder, stored in your personal folder: .pulse-motion/styles/<nom>/ , in your user folder. File What it is for identite.json The style’s personality: what it suggests, what it avoids, its genres, its signature. charte.json The default colours and fonts, replaced by your brand kit when you have one. style.css The look shared by all the scenes, in all four formats. scenes/ The nine ready-made scenes: title, list, figure, comparison, step, alert, diagram, hub, conclusion. polices/ The font files and their licences ( LICENCES.md ). guide.md The style’s guide, read by Pulse Motion to write your videos: the starting style’s guide, in 11 sections (what it is and isn’t, colour, movement, sound, gestures, pitfalls…), rewritten for yours. changements.json The log of your changes, round by round: before, after, why. During creation, sheets (still images of the scenes) open in your browser, in 16:9 and vertical, with sample text and then at maximum length."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"The name must be new. It is written in lower case with hyphens ( papercraft , affiche-retro ) and cannot reuse the name of a built-in style. Some effects are left out. Generated textures, layer blending and background blurs render badly at export: a paper texture goes through an image, a cut-out shadow through an offset shadow. Nothing loops. A video must end on a steady picture: the style has no endless animation. A modified style must be checked again. If you change a style file by hand, it is no longer offered until it has been checked again: ask « vérifie mon style papercraft » (“check my papercraft style”). The starting style may change. After a Pulse Motion update, if the style yours started from has been improved, Pulse Motion tells you once and offers to bring in its fixes; nothing changes without your agreement. The style stays on this computer. To use it elsewhere, copy its folder to the same place on the other computer."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"“Le style est prêt, mais son affichage n'a pas pu être contrôlé” (The style is ready, but its display could not be checked) Neither Chrome nor Edge was found: overflow was not checked on screen. The style can still be used. Install one of the two browsers (see the requirements ) and ask « vérifie mon style papercraft » (“check my papercraft style”). I don’t like the sample scenes Answer « Je veux changer quelque chose » (I want to change something) and say what, in your own words: « plus de couleur », « des coins moins ronds », « les titres moins gros » (“more colour”, “less rounded corners”, “smaller headings”). Pulse Motion fixes it and redoes the sheet, until you are happy. The font I wanted is not offered Pulse Motion offers free fonts from Bunny Fonts, which it checks before showing them to you. Give the exact name of yours: if it isn’t there, it suggests the closest ones."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:style un style papercraft aux couleurs pop-art Trois mots pour ce style ? → joyeux, artisanal, audacieux (Recommandé) / ludique, coloré, rétro / tactile, chaleureux, vif joyeux, artisanal, audacieux Une référence ? → les affiches de Lichtenstein (Recommandé) / les livres animés pour enfants / Autre pulse-motion style nouveau papercraft --depuis chaleureux Voici le titre et la liste dans votre style ; la planche s'est ouverte dans votre navigateur. → C'est ce que je veux (Recommandé) / Je veux changer quelque chose C'est ce que je veux pulse-motion style verifier papercraft ✅ Fait : le style « papercraft » est prêt ; il sera proposé pour vos prochaines vidéos. In English You: /pulse-motion:style a papercraft style in pop-art colours Claude: Three words for this style? → cheerful, handmade, bold (Recommended) / playful, colourful, retro / tactile, warm, lively You: cheerful, handmade, bold Claude: A reference? → Lichtenstein’s posters (Recommended) / children’s pop-up books / Other Claude: Here are the title and the list in your style; the sheet has opened in your browser. → That’s what I want (Recommended) / I want to change something You: That’s what I want Claude: ✅ Done: the “papercraft” style is ready; it will be offered for your next videos."},{"u":"en/commandes/style.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:style","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:creer : a video in your new style. /pulse-motion:retoucher : « passe en style papercraft » (“switch to the papercraft style”) on an existing video."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"","x":"Step 2 · Edit /pulse-motion:voix Record, import and align the video’s voice, or play its music (« j'enregistre la scène 2 » (“I’m recording scene 2”), « j'ai un fichier de ma voix » (“I have a file of my voice”), « recale les mots » (“realign the words”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"description","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Description","x":"This is the command for your video’s voice, scene by scene. It can do five things: Record your voice in the preview’s studio . The scene’s text is shown in large type; you click « Enregistrer » (Record), read after the countdown, then « Arrêter » (Stop). You listen back, then « Garder » (Keep) or « Refaire » (Redo). Import a recording made elsewhere, for one scene or for the whole video. A recording of the whole video is split up by scene. Add a placeholder voice : the computer’s voice reads the text, to time the animation while you wait to record. Add a synthetic voice : a French voice generated on your computer, with no account and no connection once installed. It can be delivered as it is, with its credits. Realign the words with what you actually said, thanks to transcription (in French). It can also play the music on its own , in your computer’s media player. The scenes to be spoken are the ones with spoken text in the timeline . A video with no spoken text at all has nothing to record: Pulse Motion then offers to add some with /pulse-motion:retoucher (« ajoute une voix à la vidéo » (“add a voice to the video”)). This command doesn’t change the volumes, the spoken text, the subtitles or the mood of the music: for that, use /pulse-motion:retoucher (« baisse la voix » (“turn the voice down”), « change ce que je dis dans l'intro » (“change what I say in the intro”))."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Why use it","x":"No recording software to learn. The studio is in the preview: the text to read is right in front of you. A recording made elsewhere works too. Pulse Motion reads it where it is and stores the take with the video: the original doesn’t move. The video fits itself to your voice. A scene lasts at least as long as its take, and an animation that starts on a word of the voice follows that word. Re-recording a scene realigns it automatically. You can move forward before recording. The placeholder voice holds the place; your real takes are always kept."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"quand","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"When to use it","x":"1 Create 2 Edit 3 Export 4 Customise After /pulse-motion:creer , if you answered « Je l'enregistre » (I’ll record it) or « J'ai un fichier » (I have a file) to the voice question. While editing , to redo a take or realign the subtitles. Before /pulse-motion:exporter : the export refuses the placeholder voice, unless you ask for a working version."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"arguments","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"How to run it","x":"/pulse-motion:voix It needs nothing after the name: say what you want in your own words, right after the name or in the conversation. /pulse-motion:voix j'enregistre la scène 2 “I’m recording scene 2” /pulse-motion:voix j'ai un fichier de ma voix “I have a file of my voice” What you can ask You say What happens « j'enregistre la scène 2 » (“I’m recording scene 2”), « je veux enregistrer ma voix » (“I want to record my voice”) The preview opens on the studio for the scene you asked for (otherwise, the first one to record). The first time, the browser asks you to allow the microphone. « j'ai un fichier de ma voix » (“I have a file of my voice”), « importe mon enregistrement » (“import my recording”) Pulse Motion asks you for the file (its path, or drag it into the conversation), then: “for one scene or for the whole video?”. It recommends the whole video if the recording is longer than a minute. « mets une voix provisoire » (“add a placeholder voice”), « je veux entendre le texte » (“I want to hear the text”) The computer’s voice reads the text of the whole video, or of a single scene. Takes already recorded are kept. « mets une voix de synthèse » (“add a synthetic voice”) Pulse Motion generates the voice for each scene that doesn’t have your take. The first time, it offers to install it (about 300 MB, a few minutes, with your consent); meanwhile, the computer’s voice holds the place. After that, only the scenes that changed are remade. « fais-la parler plus lentement » (“make it speak more slowly”), « un peu plus vite » (“a bit faster”) The speed of the synthetic voice changes (from 0.7 to 1.3 times), then the scenes are remade. « il prononce mal HACCP » (“it mispronounces HACCP”) Pulse Motion offers to note the correct pronunciation in the glossary, for your next videos too, or only in this video. If transcription is installed, it listens back to the synthetic voice itself and tells you which words were misread. « recale les mots » (“realign the words”), « la voix est décalée » (“the voice is out of sync”), « les sous-titres sont en avance » (“the subtitles are early”) Transcription realigns the words of your takes. If it isn’t installed, Pulse Motion offers to install it first. « fais-moi écouter la musique » (“let me hear the music”) The music on its own opens in your computer’s media player. With no music, Pulse Motion offers to add some with /pulse-motion:retoucher . The voice, subtitles and music are covered in detail on the Le son page (in French)."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"produit","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"What it produces","x":"Your voice takes , one per scene, stored with the video in assets/voix/ . Alongside each take, Pulse Motion keeps the moment each word is said. The updated video : sortie/<titre>.html , and the preview, which reloads by itself. A check after each recording, import or placeholder voice. Pulse Motion tells you in one sentence what remains: scenes still using the placeholder voice, a scene made longer by the voice, words aligned as well as possible. The music on its own , when you ask to hear it: sortie/musique.wav ."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"risques","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Risks and precautions","x":"The voice sets the length of the scenes. A scene lasts at least as long as its take, plus a short silence. A take longer than planned therefore makes the scene longer: Pulse Motion tells you. The synthetic voice is honest, not human. A single French voice, female, decent but less natural than a real voice; acronyms, proper names and English words are corrected through the glossary. It doesn’t imitate anyone. Your take always comes before it. The placeholder voice is never delivered. It is only used to time the animation. The export refuses it, unless you ask for a working version yourself. A recording of the whole video needs transcription. That is what splits it up by scene. It is installed once, with your consent (about 800 MB, see /pulse-motion:outils ). Without it, import one take per scene. The subtitles use the written text , never what transcription thought it heard. If you said something other than the text, change the spoken text with /pulse-motion:retoucher . Tip: to redo a single scene, give its number (« j'enregistre la scène 3 » (“I’m recording scene 3”)): only its take is replaced."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"problemes","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Problems and solutions","x":"Pulse Motion says there is no video here The folder you opened doesn’t contain a video. Open your video’s folder in VS Code, or create one with /pulse-motion:creer . No scene has any text to speak The video was created without a voice. Ask « ajoute une voix à la vidéo » (“add a voice to the video”) with /pulse-motion:retoucher : Pulse Motion writes the spoken text for each scene. You can then record it. Importing the whole video is refused Transcription isn’t installed, and it is what splits the recording up by scene. Pulse Motion offers to install it, then runs the same import again. If you’d rather not install it, import or record one take per scene. Pulse Motion reports a poorly recognised scene After importing the whole video, fewer than half the words in this scene were recognised: its take may have been split badly. Re-record this scene in the studio (« j'enregistre la scène 4 » (“I’m recording scene 4”)), or import a take just for it. The studio doesn’t record my voice The browser asks for permission to use the microphone the first time. If you refused, allow the microphone for the preview page in the browser’s settings, then ask « j'enregistre la scène 1 » (“I’m recording scene 1”) again. The subtitles are ahead of or behind the voice The words were aligned as well as possible, without transcription. Ask « recale les mots » (“realign the words”): Pulse Motion realigns them with your voice, after installing transcription if needed. “node” not found Node.js isn’t installed. Install the LTS version from nodejs.org , then restart Claude Code. See the requirements ."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"exemples","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Examples","x":"/pulse-motion:voix j'enregistre la scène 2 pulse-motion apercu . --ouvrir pulse-motion voix studio maillons . Le texte s'affiche en grand ; cliquez sur Enregistrer, lisez après le compte à rebours, puis Arrêter. Réécoutez, puis Garder ou Refaire. C'est gardé. pulse-motion controler . ✅ Fait : la scène 2 parle maintenant avec votre voix. Les scènes 3 à 6 sont encore en voix provisoire. 📄 Fichiers : la voix est rangée avec la vidéo ; sortie/la-chaine-du-froid.html est à jour. ➡️ Ensuite : enregistrer une autre scène (« j'enregistre la scène 3 »), retoucher avec /pulse-motion:retoucher, ou exporter en MP4 avec /pulse-motion:exporter. In English You: /pulse-motion:voix I’m recording scene 2 Claude: The text is shown in large type; click Enregistrer (Record), read after the countdown, then Arrêter (Stop). Listen back, then Garder (Keep) or Refaire (Redo). You: It’s kept. Claude: ✅ Done: scene 2 now speaks with your voice. Scenes 3 to 6 still use the placeholder voice. 📄 Files: the voice is stored with the video; sortie/la-chaine-du-froid.html is up to date. ➡️ Next: record another scene (“I’m recording scene 3”), edit with /pulse-motion:retoucher, or export as an MP4 with /pulse-motion:exporter. /pulse-motion:voix j'ai un fichier de ma voix Où est votre fichier ? Donnez son chemin, ou glissez-le ici. C:\\Users\\Marie\\Documents\\voix-chaine-du-froid.wav Pour une scène ou pour toute la vidéo ? → Toute la vidéo (Recommandé) / Une scène Toute la vidéo pulse-motion voix importer \"C:\\Users\\Marie\\Documents\\voix-chaine-du-froid.wav\" . pulse-motion controler . ✅ Fait : votre enregistrement est découpé en 6 prises, une par scène. La scène 5 a été allongée d'une seconde pour laisser finir la voix. In English You: /pulse-motion:voix I have a file of my voice Claude: Where is your file? Give its path, or drag it here. You: C:\\Users\\Marie\\Documents\\voix-chaine-du-froid.wav Claude: For one scene or for the whole video? → The whole video (Recommended) / One scene You: The whole video Claude: ✅ Done: your recording has been split into 6 takes, one per scene. Scene 5 was made one second longer to let the voice finish. /pulse-motion:voix fais-moi écouter la musique pulse-motion musique ecouter . --ouvrir ✅ Fait : la musique seule s'est ouverte dans le lecteur de votre ordinateur. Pour la changer (« plus calme », « une autre variante »), c'est /pulse-motion:retoucher. In English You: /pulse-motion:voix let me hear the music Claude: ✅ Done: the music on its own has opened in your computer’s media player. To change it (“calmer”, “another variation”), use /pulse-motion:retoucher."},{"u":"en/commandes/voix.html","a":"liees","t":"/pulse-motion:voix","s":"Related commands","x":"/pulse-motion:retoucher : the voice volume, the spoken text, the subtitles, the mood of the music. /pulse-motion:outils : install transcription. /pulse-motion:exporter : make the MP4 once the voice is recorded. /pulse-motion:animer-personnage : make a character talk along with your voice."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"","t":"Concepts","s":"","x":"Understanding Concepts The ideas Pulse Motion is built on, explained in plain words. You don’t need to know them to make a video: they help you understand what Pulse Motion suggests, and why."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"chaine","t":"Concepts","s":"From idea to video file","x":"A Pulse Motion video isn’t edited the way it would be in editing software. It is described , then drawn by the browser , frame by frame, like a web page that moves. That’s what makes it editable with one sentence: changing a word in the timeline is enough to change the video."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"genre","t":"Concepts","s":"Genre and style: two separate choices","x":"Pulse Motion separates two things that often get mixed up: the genre is what the video does : train, present a product, tell a story, post on social media; the style is what it looks like : its shapes, its fonts, the way it moves, like a presentation theme. Any genre can be combined with any style. The genre is worked out from your topic; it decides the structure of the video and the format, and ranks the styles from most to least suitable. Genre Suggested structure Format Styles suggested first Training Title → why (a figure or a warning) → steps or list → do’s and don’ts → conclusion 16:9 clair-pedagogique, chaleureux Product presentation Problem → product → 3 benefits → key figure → call to action 16:9 minimal, tech-sombre Storytelling Situation → difficulty → turning point → resolution → moral 16:9 editorial, chaleureux Social media post Hook in 2 s → 2 or 3 short scenes → conclusion 9:16 (4:5 for the feed) energique, minimal The third suggested style is deliberately contrasting : it shows you another possible direction. All twenty styles are presented in the catalogue ."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"charte","t":"Concepts","s":"The brand kit and the brand vocabulary","x":"The brand kit is your brand’s colours, fonts and logo. It applies on top of the style : the style gives the look, the brand kit gives your identity. That’s what avoids the “all videos look the same” effect. Two colours: the primary (titles, main elements) and the accent (highlights). Pulse Motion takes them from your logo, or from your words: « bleu marine » (“navy blue”). Colours that are always readable: for each colour, Pulse Motion works out a version just dark or light enough to read against the background, and whether white or black should sit on a solid block of that colour. Two fonts: one for titles, one for body text, embedded in the video. A tone: light or dark, when the style offers both. A brand kit is created during a video, then saved under a name (“atelier-nord”) so it can be offered for your next videos. If you change it later, /pulse-motion:reprendre tells you when a video uses an older version, and offers to update it. The brand vocabulary goes with the brand kit: these are the words of your trade (acronyms, product names), with their exact spelling and the forms to avoid. The check flags any mistake in these words. Not to be confused with the personal glossary , which keeps track of the tool’s words that have already been explained to you."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"deroule","t":"Concepts","s":"The timeline","x":"The timeline is the list of scenes in order, like a running order for a TV show. It’s the heart of the video: a single file, deroule.json , holding everything that one sentence can change. For the whole video For each scene The title, the genre, the path The text shown on screen The format (16:9, 9:16, 1:1, 4:5) and the frames per second The spoken text, if the video has a voice The style and the brand kit settings specific to this video The length, and the pace (slower, livelier) The music, the voice, the subtitles, the sound effects The transition to the next scene Why it matters: the text is never written in the scenes, only in the timeline. So « Change le titre » (“Change the title”) changes a single line, with no risk of breaking the animation. The pacing rules One idea per scene. If the text holds two ideas, that’s two scenes. Time to read: a scene lasts at least 1.5 s + 0.3 s per word shown. Nothing still for more than 2.5 s : a drifting background or a staggered entrance is enough. End on a still frame lasting at least half a second. Short text: a title of 40 characters at most, scene text of 80 at most, numbers written as figures."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"scenes","t":"Concepts","s":"Ready-made scenes and composed scenes","x":"A scene is one animated screen of the video, like a slide that moves. Each style offers nine ready-made scenes , one per role: Scene Role Suggested length titre Open the video or a chapter 7 s liste 2 to 4 points revealed one by one 12 s chiffre A key figure that counts up to its value 7 s comparaison Two columns: before / after, do / don’t 12 s etape A numbered step in a procedure 8 s alerte A point to watch out for, a mistake to avoid 8 s schema Three steps linked by arrows 8 s moyeu One idea in the centre, 3 to 5 parts around it, linked by arrows 10 s conclusion The key message and the logo 6 s Lengths vary a little from one style to another (the energetic style is shorter). When none of them fits, the animator composes the scene freely: a character (in French), a background, a moving camera. The difference matters when you change style: a ready-made scene is carried over as it is into the new style; a composed scene has to be rewritten. The animators An animator is a specialised Pulse Motion assistant that writes one scene. During the build there is one per scene, and they all work at the same time: a 10-scene video doesn’t take ten times longer than a one-scene video. Pulse Motion itself decides on particle effects and hands them to the animators concerned, to keep the video understated. The characters A character is an animated actor in the scene: it walks in, points to a figure, waves, changes expression, and can even speak the scene’s text with your voice. They all share the same skeleton, dressed as a stick figure, as a drawing made on request or as your own illustration; so they all strike the same poses and make the same moves. Everything is explained on the characters page (in French)."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"parcours","t":"Concepts","s":"The path: quick or guided","x":"At the start, you choose your path , that is, how many times Pulse Motion stops to ask for your approval. Path Your approvals Who it’s for Quick (default) Just one: the timeline, with the choice of style in the same question You want to see the video fast, and edit it afterwards. Guided Three: the timeline, then the style, then the final video You want to approve each step, or the video is important. Either way, nothing is animated before you approve the timeline , and everything can still be edited afterwards."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"apercu","t":"Concepts","s":"The preview","x":"The preview is the video itself, open in your browser, with a playback bar. It’s served by a small server on your computer (an address starting with http://localhost:… ): nothing goes out to the internet. It reloads on its own after every edit, at the same point. You can drive it from the keyboard: Space play/pause, ← → frame by frame, Shift + arrow to change scene, B to loop a scene. It hosts the voice studio (the Voix (Voice) button) and an MP4 button that exports directly in the browser. The video is deterministic : a given moment always shows exactly the same frame. That’s what makes it possible to check it frame by frame, and to export it without stutter, however powerful the computer is."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"controles","t":"Concepts","s":"The automatic checks","x":"After every build and every edit, Pulse Motion checks the video in two stages: first the timeline and the text, then the rendered video, in Chrome or Edge without a window, frame by frame. The safe cases are fixed on their own , and Pulse Motion tells you in one sentence what it did. The rest is fixed by Pulse Motion itself, in two rounds at most; whatever resists is reported to you. What is checked Fixed on its own? A scene too short to read its text Yes: lengthened, by 50% at most Dead time at the end of a scene (nothing moves for more than 2.5 s) Yes: scene shortened, without going below the reading time A video ending without a still frame Yes: last scene lengthened by one second at most French typography (spaces before ; : ! ?, « » quotation marks, ’ apostrophe, ellipses) Yes, in the timeline text A capital letter missing its accent (« Etape » → « Étape ») Yes Text spilling out of its box or off the frame Yes: size reduced down to 80%, never below readable Two overlapping elements, a hidden logo, text too close to the edge No: reported and fixed in the scene In vertical format: text under the social media buttons No: reported The real contrast of a text against what lies behind its letters No: reported More than 3 flashes per second over a quarter of the frame No: error , risk of an epileptic seizure A word from the brand vocabulary misspelt, a word written twice in a row No: reported Each automatic fix is backed up first: « annule » (“undo”) reverses it. If you asked for a specific length or spelling, Pulse Motion checks without fixing, to respect your request. Tip: the frame check needs Chrome or Edge. Without them, Pulse Motion tells you the render hasn’t been checked: the video is made, but overflows haven’t been measured."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"tempo","t":"Concepts","s":"Tempo: timing the picture to the music","x":"With composed music, a score, or a file whose tempo you know, the video takes on the music’s tempo , in beats per minute. At 120 beats per minute, there’s a beat every half-second. Animations can then start on the beat . It’s the music that fits the picture, not the other way round: composed music (or a score timed to the scenes) starts again at the beginning of each scene. Scene lengths stay free, and the check can fix a length on its own without putting the music out of step. Music from a file, on the other hand, doesn’t move: if you give its tempo, each scene must start on a beat (at 120 beats per minute, every half-second), and the check no longer fixes a length on its own, so as not to put the picture out of step with the music."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"transitions","t":"Concepts","s":"Transitions","x":"The transition is the passage from one scene to the next. It lasts half a second. Transition For fondu The default choice, everywhere. glissement Moving forward through a sequence (steps). balayage Changing section. rideau An opening from the centre (editorial style). zoom The scene comes closer (energetic style). coupe A hard cut, with no overlap (energetic and minimal styles). whip A fast, blurred whip pan: an energetic change. poussee The new scene pushes the old one out of frame: a logical sequence, a slideshow. traversee You go through the scene into the next one: diving into the subject. cercle The scene opens in a growing circle: a playful or retro tone. volets The scene appears in strips: a graphic tone. retournement The scene flips like a card: a question, then its answer. aucune Linking two scenes that answer each other (before / after). Two or three families at most in one video, and the same one for scenes of the same kind. Transitions, like text entrances and camera moves, also have a trade name and a number in the move dictionary: « un whip pan entre la scène 2 et la 3 » (“a whip pan between scenes 2 and 3”) or « #042 » are understood. For the list: « quels mouvements existent ? » (“which moves are there?”)."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"explicative","t":"Concepts","s":"The explainer video","x":"An explainer video helps people understand a mechanism: a circuit, a process, a calculation. The « Vidéo explicative » (Explainer video) template builds it in six parts: a hook, the problem and its causes, the mechanism in three steps, a close-up on one detail, a figure, the summary. Example: « Du devis au paiement : le trajet d’une facture » (“From quote to payment: the journey of an invoice”), on the Video ideas page. What is said is shown : an arrow draws itself from “Client” to “Server” when the voice says “sends”, a word is circled by hand as it’s spoken, a chart grows bar by bar over the years mentioned, a spreadsheet formula builds up step by step. The camera finds its own shots : an opening shot, a move in when there’s time to look, a final pull-back over the whole idea, never cutting off any text. « Laisse la caméra bouger toute seule » (“Let the camera move on its own”), or « garde la caméra fixe scène 2 » (“keep the camera still in scene 2”). Small, soft sounds go with each entrance (a card, an arrow, a list point, a bar counting up): « ajoute des petits sons » (“add little sounds”). The overall pace is set with one sentence: « plus rythmé pour les réseaux » (“punchier for social media”), « plus posé » (“calmer”). In an explainer video, the automatic camera and the small sounds are on by default; elsewhere, they stay off until you ask for them. The voice can be yours, or a synthetic voice made on your computer, with no account or connection once installed (about 300 MB, with your approval). It’s a single French female voice, decent but less natural than a real voice. Unlike the computer’s own voice, it can be delivered as it is, and its credits are added automatically. See /pulse-motion:voix ."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"export","t":"Concepts","s":"The two exports","x":"The same video gives you two deliverables : the animated page ( sortie/<titre>.html ), always up to date: a single file that contains everything (images, fonts, sound) and opens offline, ready to put on a website; the MP4 file , made on request by /pulse-motion:exporter , ready to publish or send. The MP4 can be made in two ways: High-fidelity export Export in the browser What you need ffmpeg, installed once Nothing beyond Chrome or Edge The frames The browser’s real frames, one by one Drawn in the browser; a few advanced effects come out less well The sound The page’s mix, level checked The same mix The time it takes About 2 s of processing per second of video in 1080p Depends on the computer"},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"historique","t":"Concepts","s":"The edit history","x":"Before every edit, Pulse Motion copies the state of the video (timeline, scenes, files, brand kit) into a hidden folder, .historique/ . The last 20 copies are kept. « annule » (“undo”) restores the most recent one; said again, it goes back in time, copy after copy."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"confidentialite","t":"Concepts","s":"Privacy","x":"When you record an app with /pulse-motion:filmer , what appears on screen may be personal: addresses, customer names, an IBAN. Pulse Motion follows four rules. The blur is in the frames themselves. It’s applied during recording, before the frame is written to disk: no sharp frame is kept, and nobody can “remove” the blur from the delivered page. Passwords, email addresses, phone numbers, IBANs, card and national insurance numbers, and address and date-of-birth fields are blurred automatically. Anything else (a name, a face, an amount), Pulse Motion points out to you when it looks at the contact sheet, and you decide. Your password never goes through Pulse Motion. You sign in yourself, in a visible window; Pulse Motion doesn’t see what you type, never types a password, and its browser’s password manager is turned off. The internet is only used for recording , and only after you’ve approved that site, one per recording. If the page tries to take you elsewhere, navigation is blocked. After that, the video never needs it again: the preview and the export read the recording kept in the folder. The clicks are real. Pulse Motion warns you before the first recording: an action taken in the app (save, send) isn’t undone afterwards. A demo account is the safest option. What is kept Where The recording (frames already blurred, click positions) and your approval for the site The video’s folder, under films/ . A take doesn’t go into the edit history: its blur is permanent. Your login to a site (its cookies, as in any browser) Your personal Pulse Motion folder, separately for each site, never in the video’s folder or in an exported library. « Oublie ma connexion à … » (“Forget my login to …”) deletes it. Your password Nowhere."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"lexique","t":"Concepts","s":"The personal glossary","x":"Pulse Motion talks to you without jargon. When it needs to explain one of the tool’s words (timeline, scene, brand kit, export…), it does so once , with an everyday image, and notes it in your personal glossary . Next time, it doesn’t explain it all over again. You can read and correct this glossary: it’s a text file, lexique.md , in your personal Pulse Motion folder. The glossary of this wiki (in French) lists these words."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"bibliotheque","t":"Concepts","s":"Your library","x":"What you keep for all your videos makes up your personal library : your saved brand kits, your personal styles, the effects, characters and music you’ve kept, and the preferences it remembers: « jamais de rebond » (“never any bounce”). Keep. « Garde cet effet de neige pour mes prochaines vidéos » (“Keep this snow effect for my next videos”), « garde ce personnage » (“keep this character”): it joins your library, along with what it needs (a character’s poses and moves, an effect’s image). When you’ve just drawn a character or invented a move, Pulse Motion offers to keep it. A name already used by something that comes with Pulse Motion can’t be used: it suggests another one. Find again. When Pulse Motion suggests an effect or a character, the ones in your library are in the list, alongside the ones that come with it. By copy. An item from your library is copied into the video that uses it. The video stays complete: you can copy it or send it on its own. And changing your library never changes a video that’s already made. Take it to another computer. « Emporte mes chartes sur mon autre ordinateur » (“Take my brand kits to my other computer”): your whole library, or whatever you choose, goes into a single ZIP file that your file explorer can open. You copy it yourself (USB stick, email); nothing goes over the internet. On the other computer: « importe ma bibliothèque » (“import my library”). Whatever is already there is never replaced without your approval. What doesn’t go with it: your logins to the sites you’ve recorded, the list of your videos and the error log stay on the computer where they were made."},{"u":"en/concepts.html","a":"donnees","t":"Concepts","s":"Where your files are","x":"Pulse Motion works without the cloud : nothing is sent to a video service. Everything is on your computer, in two places. Where What you’ll find there The video’s folder The timeline, the scenes, the video’s brand kit, your files ( assets/ ), app recordings ( films/ ), the outputs ( sortie/ ), the history. Copy this folder to back up or hand over the video. Your personal Pulse Motion folder .pulse-motion , in your user folder Your library : saved brand kits ( chartes/ ), personal styles ( styles/ ), kept effects ( effets/ ), characters ( personnages/ ) and music ( musiques/ ), remembered preferences ( preferences.json ). Also your glossary ( lexique.md ), the list of your videos, and your logins to the sites you’ve recorded (see Privacy ). Claude, for its part, reads the files in the folder to work with you, as it does for any project in Claude Code."},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"","t":"Quick start","s":"","x":"Quick start · 5 minutes Quick start One sentence, a few answers, and you’re watching an animated video of about one minute in your browser, ready to export as an MP4. Five minutes of your time, plus a few minutes of building during which you have nothing to do. Pulse Motion is made for French speakers: the plugin’s questions, messages and reports are in French, and Claude answers in French while it uses the plugin. This English guide helps you discover it; the requests shown as examples stay in French, with their translation alongside. Your browser can’t play this video: download it ."},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"pret","t":"Quick start","s":"Before you start","x":"You need Claude Code, Node.js, Chrome or Edge, and the Pulse Motion plugin. If any of the four is missing, the requirements page installs them step by step; then come back here. Open an empty folder in VS Code (the video will be stored there), then ask Claude to check your computer: /pulse-motion:outils What you should see: Pulse Motion shows a table of what’s ready. Node.js and the browser must be ticked; ❌ lines for ffmpeg, Python or transcription don’t stop you from starting. If anything is missing, it offers to install everything in a single question: « Tout installer (Recommandé) » (Install everything (Recommended)). One approval, and no need to restart Claude Code."},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"creer","t":"Quick start","s":"Create","x":"Describe your video in one sentence, after the command: /pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires “the cold chain, for my trainees” Pulse Motion asks two short rounds of questions (length, audience, music, voice and subtitles). To go fast, each time keep the answer marked (Recommandé) (Recommended); for the audience, choose the one your video is for; at « À ajouter » (To add), answer « Rien pour l’instant » (Nothing for now); for the voice, « Pas de voix » (No voice). Even quicker: when asked about the path, choose « Faites pour moi » (Do it for me), and Pulse Motion won’t ask you any more questions. It then shows you the timeline , the list of scenes with their text, and three possible styles. Voici le déroulé : 6 scènes, environ 1 minute. → Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible et rassurant (Recommandé) / … / Je veux changer quelque chose Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible et rassurant In English Claude: Here is the timeline: 6 scenes, about 1 minute. → I approve, with the calm, clear and reassuring style (Recommended) / … / I want to change something You: I approve, with the calm, clear and reassuring style What you should see: a first animated draft opens in your browser in under a minute. Pulse Motion then animates the scenes, which replace the draft one by one, and checks readability on its own: allow a few minutes, there’s nothing for you to do."},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"regarder","t":"Quick start","s":"Watch","x":"The preview has been open in your browser since the draft: it’s the video, with a playback bar at the bottom. Click ▶ or press the Space bar. Something you don’t like? Say it in one sentence, and the preview reloads straight away: /pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 “slow down scene 3”"},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"exporter","t":"Quick start","s":"Export","x":"/pulse-motion:exporter Pulse Motion makes the MP4 file on your computer, in a few minutes for a one-minute video. What you should see: the sortie/ folder contains the MP4, ready to publish or send, and the animated page, which opens offline."},{"u":"en/demarrage-rapide.html","a":"ensuite","t":"Quick start","s":"What next?","x":"The same path, explained step by step: tutorial 1, First video . Your logo, your colours and your voice: tutorial 2, Complete video (in French). Everything one sentence can change: /pulse-motion:retoucher . Not sure which command to use? Which command? (in French)"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"","t":"Examples","s":"","x":"Made with Pulse Motion Examples What Pulse Motion makes from a single sentence. For each video: the request typed into Claude Code, the timeline you approve before any animation, then the resulting video. Plus two edits shown before and after, and three ways to use it day to day."},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"mode-emploi","t":"Examples","s":"From sentence to video","x":"Each example follows the three stages of a real session: The request : one sentence, exactly as it was typed. Pulse Motion then asks a few questions; the examples took the recommended answers. The approved timeline : the scenes, what each one does, its texts and its length. It is the only approval asked for on the quick path. The video : published as Pulse Motion made it, with no voice, with the music composed for it. To start from one of them, the Ideas page gives sentences to copy for each kind of video. The cold chain Creating an invoice in Facturo From quote to payment: the journey of an invoice Plannette, your team's schedule The Tilleuls garden 3 tips for saving water Welcome to the Saules media library Potagine, the pitch Bike shop opening Vibe Connection"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-chaine-du-froid","t":"Examples","s":"The cold chain","x":"Template Short training video · 60 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires “the cold chain for my trainees” Made step by step in the “Première vidéo” tutorial, with the recommended answers to the questions; shown here without its voice. The step-by-step tutorial . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: state the topic « Hygiène alimentaire » · « La chaîne du froid » · « 1 minute pour comprendre l’essentiel » 8 s 2 Figure: the temperature to remember « La température à ne pas dépasser » · « °C » · « pour les produits frais au réfrigérateur » 9 s 3 List: the 4 links in the chain « Les 4 maillons de la chaîne » · « Le transport en camion réfrigéré » · « Le stockage en chambre froide » · « La vitrine du magasin » · « Votre réfrigérateur à la maison » 13 s 4 Comparison: what to do, what to avoid « Les bons réflexes » · « À faire » · « Ranger les courses au froid dès le retour » · « À éviter » · « Laisser un produit frais 2 h hors du froid » 13 s 5 Warning: the most common mistake « Ne jamais recongeler » · « Un produit décongelé se consomme vite : le recongeler favorise les bactéries. » 10 s 6 Conclusion: the message to remember « Le froid, c’est la sécurité » · « Merci d’avoir suivi cette formation » 9.5 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-tutoriel-logiciel","t":"Examples","s":"Creating an invoice in Facturo","x":"Template Software tutorial · 29.5 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel : comment créer une facture dans Facturo, l'appli d'essai “a tutorial: how to create an invoice in Facturo, the test app” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Screen: show the end result straight away: the saved invoice « Votre facture en 30 secondes » 3 s 2 Title: say why, in one sentence « Facturo » · « Créer une facture » · « Sans rien oublier » 5 s 3 Screen: steps 1 and 2: open a new invoice, type the customer; the camera follows the clicks « 1 » · « Cliquez sur Nouvelle facture » · « 2 » · « Tapez le nom du client » 8 s 4 Screen: steps 3 and 4: save, confirm; spotlight on the Enregistrer button, the key step « 3 » · « Cliquez sur Enregistrer » · « 4 » · « Confirmez dans la fenêtre » 7 s 5 List: recap the steps, one line each « En 4 étapes » · « Nouvelle facture » · « Le nom du client » · « Enregistrer » · « Confirmer » 5 s 6 Conclusion: what you can now do, and where to find help « Votre facture est prête » · « Besoin d’aide ? Menu Aide » 4 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-explication-facture","t":"Examples","s":"From quote to payment: the journey of an invoice","x":"Template Explainer video · 63.5 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative : du devis au paiement, le trajet d'une facture “an explainer video: from quote to payment, the journey of an invoice” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: the hook: what we are going to understand, as a question or a journey « Comptabilité » · « Du devis au paiement » · « Le trajet d’une facture, en trois étapes » 8 s 2 Hub: the problem in the centre with its causes around it; the key word circled « Pourquoi une facture est payée en retard » · « Paiement en retard » · « Montant erroné » · « Mention manquante » · « Mauvais destinataire » · « Relance oubliée » 12 s 3 Diagram: the mechanism in three steps, linked by arrows drawn one after the other « Le trajet en trois étapes » · « Devis signé » · « Facture envoyée » · « Paiement reçu » 14 s 4 Step: one step up close: a spreadsheet formula building up « 2 » · « Calculer le montant TTC » · « Le montant TTC ajoute la TVA au montant hors taxes » 12 s 5 Figure: a figure that shows the scale, as a bar chart « Délai moyen de paiement » · « jours » · « Une petite entreprise, de 2023 à 2025 » 12 s 6 Conclusion: the summary: the whole idea in one sentence « Une facture complète est payée à l’heure » · « Vérifiez ses mentions avant chaque envoi » 8 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-presentation-produit","t":"Examples","s":"Plannette, your team's schedule","x":"Template Product presentation · 42.5 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer la présentation de Plannette, une application qui organise les plannings d'une petite équipe “the presentation of Plannette, an app that organises a small team's schedules” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Warning: the problem the target audience faces « Vos plannings, un casse-tête ? » · « Des heures perdues chaque semaine en tableaux et en messages » 7 s 2 Title: the answer: the product's name « Application de planning » · « Plannette » · « Le planning de votre équipe, prêt en 5 minutes » 7 s 3 List: 3 concrete benefits « Ce que ça change » · « Le planning de la semaine en un clic » · « Chacun voit ses horaires sur son téléphone » · « Les échanges de créneaux se font sans vous » 11 s 4 Diagram: how it works, in 3 stages « Comment ça marche » · « Vous ajoutez l’équipe » · « Plannette propose » · « Vous validez » 8 s 5 Figure: a key figure that proves the promise (value shown: 3) « Temps gagné chaque semaine » · « h » · « en moyenne, pour une équipe de 10 personnes » 6 s 6 Conclusion: the call to action « Essayez Plannette gratuitement » · « 30 jours offerts, sans engagement » 6 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-histoire","t":"Examples","s":"The Tilleuls garden","x":"Template Story · 55.5 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire de notre jardin partagé, d'un terrain vague à un lieu de rencontre “the story of our community garden, from wasteland to meeting place” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: the starting point « Quartier des Tilleuls » · « Le jardin des Tilleuls » · « Au départ, un terrain vague entre deux immeubles » 8 s 2 Warning: the difficulty that blocks everything « Un lieu que tout le monde évitait » · « Des herbes folles, des déchets, et personne pour s’en occuper » 10 s 3 Figure: the turning point, in one figure (value shown: 300) « Une parcelle prêtée par la mairie » · « m² » · « le déclic : la mairie accepte de nous la confier » 9 s 4 Diagram: how we got out of it, in 3 stages « Ce que nous avons fait » · « Nettoyer ensemble » · « Planter des bacs » · « Ouvrir à tous » 11 s 5 Comparison: before and after « Avant, après » · « Avant » · « Un terrain vague que l’on contournait » · « Après » · « 40 jardiniers et une fête chaque été » 12 s 6 Conclusion: the moral of the story « Un jardin, ça rapproche » · « Venez planter avec nous le samedi matin » 8 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-publication-sociale","t":"Examples","s":"3 tips for saving water","x":"Template Social media post · 30 seconds, vertical (9:16). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour les réseaux : 3 astuces pour économiser l'eau à la maison “a social media video: 3 tips for saving water at home” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: the hook, readable in 2 seconds « Astuces maison » · « Moins d’eau, même confort » · « 3 astuces en 30 secondes » 5 s 2 Figure: a surprising figure (value shown: 150) « Eau utilisée par jour » · « L » · « par personne, souvent sans y penser » 6 s 3 List: the 3 ideas, short « 3 astuces » · « Une douche de 5 minutes » · « Robinet fermé au brossage » · « Un lave-linge toujours plein » 9 s 4 Step: the tip to try today « 1 » · « Le défi de la semaine » · « Chronométrez votre douche avec une chanson de 4 minutes » 6 s 5 Conclusion: the punchline and the invitation « Chaque goutte compte » · « Partagez à qui en a besoin » 6 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-accueil","t":"Examples","s":"Welcome to the Saules media library","x":"Template Welcoming a newcomer · 57 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue pour les nouveaux arrivants de notre médiathèque “a welcome video for newcomers to our media library” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: say welcome « Médiathèque des Saules » · « Bienvenue parmi nous » · « Tout ce qu’il faut savoir pour bien commencer » 8 s 2 List: what you will find there « Ce que vous trouverez ici » · « Livres, BD et magazines pour tous les âges » · « Films et musique à emprunter » · « Des postes informatiques en libre accès » · « Un coin calme pour lire ou travailler » 12 s 3 Step: the first step « 1 » · « Faites votre carte » · « À l’accueil, avec une pièce d’identité : elle est gratuite » 8 s 4 Step: the second step « 2 » · « Empruntez jusqu’à 10 documents » · « Pour 3 semaines, à rendre sur place ou dans la boîte de retour » 8 s 5 Step: the third step « 3 » · « Réservez en ligne » · « Votre compte permet de réserver et de prolonger vos prêts » 8 s 6 Diagram: where to find help, from the closest to the widest « Besoin d’aide ? » · « Les bibliothécaires » · « La banque d’accueil » · « Le site de la médiathèque » 9 s 7 Conclusion: a warm word to finish « Bonne découverte ! » · « L’équipe de la médiathèque des Saules » 7 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-pitch","t":"Examples","s":"Potagine, the pitch","x":"Template Pitch · 53.5 seconds, landscape (16:9). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de Potagine, une plateforme qui relie les maraîchers aux cantines scolaires, pour nos investisseurs “the pitch for Potagine, a platform connecting market gardeners with school canteens, for our investors” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Warning: the problem, real and measurable « Des cantines loin des producteurs » · « Les maraîchers voisins vendent peu aux écoles : trop de démarches » 8 s 2 Title: the solution: the project's name « Notre solution » · « Potagine » · « Les légumes du coin, commandés en 3 clics » 8 s 3 Figure: the market size in one figure (value shown: 450) « Repas servis chaque jour dans la région » · « k » · « autant d’assiettes à remplir de produits locaux » 9 s 4 Diagram: how the project makes money « Notre modèle » · « Les cantines s’abonnent » · « Commandes groupées » · « 5 % sur chaque vente » 10 s 5 List: the team and its strengths « L’équipe » · « Une ancienne maraîchère, à la production » · « Un chef de cuisine collective, aux ventes » · « Une développeuse, à la plateforme » 12 s 6 Conclusion: what we are asking for « Nous levons 400 000 € » · « Pour ouvrir 3 nouvelles régions en 2027 » 9 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-annonce","t":"Examples","s":"Bike shop opening","x":"Template Announcement · 29.5 seconds, vertical (9:16). 1. The request /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce de l'ouverture de notre nouvelle boutique de vélos, le 15 mars “the announcement of the opening of our new bike shop, on 15 March” Then the recommended answers to Pulse Motion’s questions, with no edit: the details are on the Ideas page . 2. The approved timeline Before animating anything, Pulse Motion proposes this timeline and waits for your approval. Scene What it does On screen (in French) Length 1 Title: the hook, readable in 2 seconds « Nouveau » · « Ça roule en ville ! » · « Bientôt chez vous » 4 s 2 Title: the news being announced « Rue des Lilas » · « Une boutique de vélos » · « Vente, location et réparation » 6 s 3 List: what it brings « Ce qui vous attend » · « Des vélos à essayer sur place » · « Un atelier de réparation rapide » · « Des conseils pour bien rouler » 9 s 4 Figure: the countdown to the big day (value shown: 10) « Ouverture dans » · « j » · « le 15 mars, dès 10 h, rue des Lilas » 6 s 5 Conclusion: the invitation « Venez nombreux ! » · « Un café offert le jour de l’ouverture » 6.5 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"exemple-vibe-connection","t":"Examples","s":"Vibe Connection","x":"A story, a mock silent retro film · 1 min 40 s, square (1:1). 1. The requests A 1 min 40 film, made with Pulse Motion from a script written in advance: one first request for the first draft, then around thirty requests to redo each sequence, create the characters' gestures, add the effects and sound effects, and export it. They are all in the tutorial. The first request, in full /pulse-motion:creer le film « Vibe Connection » décrit dans scenario.md (dans ce dossier) : lis-le en entier avant tout. C'est un faux film muet et rétro de 1 min 40 qui fait la promotion de pulse-motion, en 7 scènes composées librement, une par séquence, avec exactement les durées du scénario (20, 6, 17, 8, 23, 10 et 16 secondes) et ces noms de scènes : generique, installation, probleme, tournant, demo, preuve, pancartes. Genre storytelling, format carré 1:1 à 25 images par seconde, style tech-sombre, ton sombre, sans voix ni sous-titres, une musique composée d'ambiance inspirante (nappes et mélodie douces, sans sonorités chiptune) à 120 battements par minute, volume 0,45. Les textes à l'écran sont les cartons du scénario, mot pour mot ; aucun texte plus petit que 38 px. Pour ce premier jet : décors, cartons, caméra et objets de chaque séquence, avec les Tousfous du plugin (le dessin « touffu », hauteur 14 % de l'image en plan large) et seulement les poses et mouvements de la bibliothèque ; les gestes propres au film, les effets de particules et les bruitages viendront ensuite. “the film “Vibe Connection” described in scenario.md (in this folder): read it in full before anything else. It is a mock silent, retro film of 1 min 40 promoting pulse-motion, in 7 freely composed scenes, one per sequence, with exactly the durations in the script (20, 6, 17, 8, 23, 10 and 16 seconds) and these scene names: generique, installation, probleme, tournant, demo, preuve, pancartes. Storytelling genre, square 1:1 format at 25 frames per second, tech-sombre style, dark tone, no voice and no subtitles, inspiring ambient composed music (soft pads and melody, no chiptune sounds) at 120 beats per minute, volume 0.45. The on-screen texts are the script's title cards, word for word; no text smaller than 38 px. For this first draft: the sets, title cards, camera and objects of each sequence, with the plugin's Tousfous (the “touffu” drawing, height 14% of the frame in a wide shot) and only the poses and moves from the library; the film's own gestures, the particle effects and the sound effects will come later.” 2. The timeline Scene What it does Length 1 Opening credits: the logo drops, the Tousfous arrive, the title is revealed in the smoke 20 s 2 The set-up: the Tousfous fall through a trapdoor and walk straight ahead 6 s 3 The problem: the designer draws a bridge by hand over the precipice, and fails 17 s 4 The turning point: everything stops at the edge of the void, and the terminal appears 8 s 5 The demo: a single command, and the bridge builds itself 23 s 6 The comic punchline: the designer breaks free and has a coffee while the Tousfous play on the bridge 10 s 7 The call to action, carried by two clumsy Tousfous and their placards 16 s 3. The video"},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"retouches","t":"Examples","s":"Before and after: an edit in one sentence","x":"A video is edited with one sentence, using /pulse-motion:retoucher . Here are two edits on “The cold chain”: on the left, the scene before; on the right, the same scene after. Both are rendered by Pulse Motion, from the edit applied; nothing is drawn separately. The previous version stays in the history: « annule » (“undo”) brings it back."},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"retouche-titre-percutant","t":"Examples","s":"","x":"“Make the title punchier” /pulse-motion:retoucher rends le titre plus percutant “make the title punchier” The title of scene 1 now comes in with a pop-in ( apparition rebondie , #001 in the dictionary): it grows from nothing, overshoots its size slightly, then settles, instead of rising gently as the style would have it. Nothing else changes."},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"retouche-ralentir","t":"Examples","s":"","x":"“Slow down scene 3” /pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 “slow down scene 3” Pulse Motion sets the pace of scene 3 in the timeline (0.8): all its timings stretch, and it goes from 13 s to 16.25 s. The texts and the drawing do not change."},{"u":"en/exemples.html","a":"scenarios","t":"Examples","s":"Three ways to use it","x":"Scenarios, not testimonials. These three people are imagined to show the journey, based on the three audiences Pulse Motion is made for. The times are rough estimates, taken from the tutorials in this wiki; the build, during which you have nothing to do, is counted separately. Claire, food hygiene trainer What she does. Claire is preparing a session on the cold chain for her trainees. She wants a one-minute video to show at the start of the session, then to upload to her training platform. She types her request in one sentence, answers the questions with the recommended answers, and approves the six-scene timeline. She adds her colours and her logo, then records her voice in the preview; the subtitles line up with her words. The day before the session, she finds the links scene too fast: « ralentis la scène 3 » (“slow down scene 3”). She exports the video to show it in the room. The commands. /pulse-motion:creer , /pulse-motion:voix , /pulse-motion:retoucher , /pulse-motion:exporter (« présentation » (presentation) use). Time spent. Half an hour for the first video, as in the first tutorial ; one more hour for her brand kit, her voice and the high-fidelity export, as in the full tutorial (in French). Two minutes for the edit the day before. Malik, solo creator on social media What he does. Every week, Malik posts a cooking tip on Instagram and TikTok. He wants a 30-second vertical video that stops the scroll, without spending his whole evening on it. He copies a sentence from the Ideas page and changes the topic: Pulse Motion recognises a social media post, in vertical format. He doesn’t like the way the title comes in: he opens the moves page, picks one by watching it, and asks for it by name (« un pop-in sur le titre » (“a pop-in on the title”)). He exports for social media: the video keeps its texts clear of the apps’ buttons. The commands. /pulse-motion:creer , /pulse-motion:retoucher , /pulse-motion:exporter (« réseaux » (social media) use). Time spent. Five minutes for the request and the questions, as in the Quick start , a few minutes of editing, then the export. The following week, he starts again from the same sentence. Atelier Nord, a small agency What it does. The agency produces presentation videos for its clients, each in the client’s own colours. It wants two videos for two clients never to look alike. For each client, it applies their logo, colours and font, then saves this brand kit under the client’s name. For a client who wants a look of their own, it creates a personal style in one sentence (« un style papercraft aux couleurs pop-art » (“a papercraft style in pop-art colours”)), checked before use. For each new video, it applies the client’s saved brand kit, then approves the timeline with them. It exports the MP4 for the client’s website, and emails them a lightweight version for feedback. The commands. /pulse-motion:creer , /pulse-motion:style , /pulse-motion:retoucher (« enregistre ma charte sous le nom dupont » (“save my brand kit under the name dupont”), « applique la charte dupont » (“apply the dupont brand kit”)), /pulse-motion:exporter (« web » and « courriel » (email) uses). Time spent. About ten minutes per client for their brand kit, just once, as in the full tutorial (in French). After that, about half an hour per video, timeline and edits included."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"","t":"Video ideas","s":"","x":"Start from an idea Video ideas Nine kinds of video that Pulse Motion knows by heart. For each one: a finished example, sentences to copy as they are, and the structure it will follow. Copy a sentence, change the topic, and off you go."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"mode-emploi","t":"Video ideas","s":"How to use it","x":"Watch the example that looks like your project. Copy a sentence and replace the topic with your own. Give it to Claude Code: /pulse-motion:creer recognises the video template, tells you so in one sentence, then asks you its usual questions. Everything can still be changed. Want to see the result right there on your computer? In an empty folder, run the command below: the finished example is copied into it and opens in the preview, ready to be edited. /pulse-motion:creer montre-moi un exemple de pitch “show me an example of a pitch” Short training video Software tutorial Explainer video Product presentation Story Social media post Welcoming a newcomer Pitch Announcement"},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-formation-courte","t":"Video ideas","s":"Short training video","x":"Trainers, quality or safety managers, teachers: an instruction people need to remember. One minute to get a rule or a gesture across: why it matters, the essentials, what to do and what to avoid. 1 min or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer une formation d'une minute sur la chaîne du froid “a one-minute training video on the cold chain” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo courte pour expliquer le tri des déchets au bureau “a short video explaining waste sorting at the office” /pulse-motion:creer un rappel des gestes de sécurité avant d'utiliser une échelle “a reminder of the safety steps before using a ladder” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer une formation d'une minute pour bien laver ses fruits et légumes, pour le grand public (“a one-minute training video on washing fruit and vegetables properly, for the general public”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer une formation d'une minute sur le port des gants en cuisine, pour des cuisiniers (“a one-minute training video on wearing gloves in the kitchen, for cooks”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo d'une minute sur les règles du laboratoire de chimie, pour des élèves de seconde (“a one-minute video on the chemistry lab rules, for Year 11 pupils”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 State the topic and the promise (understand it in one minute) 8 s 2 Why it matters: the figure to remember 9 s 3 The 3 or 4 key points, one at a time 13 s 4 What to do, what to avoid 12 s 5 The mistake never to make 10 s 6 The message to remember 8 s Recommended styles: clair-pedagogique (recommended) · chaleureux · minimal . How this video was made This is the video from the First video tutorial, made step by step with /pulse-motion:creer ; shown here without its voice."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-tutoriel-logiciel","t":"Video ideas","s":"Software tutorial","x":"Trainers, support teams, managers of an internal tool: a procedure to show in a piece of software, step by step. Two minutes to show how something is done in an application: the result first, then each step filmed on screen, with the pointer, the clicks and a spotlight on what matters most. 2 min or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel : comment créer une facture dans Facturo, l'appli d'essai “a tutorial: how to create an invoice in Facturo, the test app” /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel pour montrer comment réserver une salle sur notre site intranet “a tutorial showing how to book a room on our intranet site” /pulse-motion:creer filme comment on ajoute un client dans notre logiciel de facturation “film how to add a customer in our invoicing software” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel pour prendre rendez-vous sur le site de la mairie, pour le grand public (“a tutorial on booking an appointment on the town hall website, for the general public”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel pour saisir une note de frais dans notre logiciel, pour les commerciaux (“a tutorial on entering an expense claim in our software, for the sales team”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel pour rendre un devoir sur l'espace numérique de travail, pour des élèves de quatrième (“a tutorial on handing in homework on the school's online learning platform, for Year 9 pupils”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 Show the end result straight away (screen recording) 3 s 2 Say why, in one sentence 8 s 3 Perform each step on screen: the pointer, the clicks, the typing (screen recording) 88 s 4 Recap the steps, one line each 13 s 5 What you can now do, and where to find help 8 s Recommended styles: clair-pedagogique (recommended) · minimal · tech-sombre . Variant: “30-second tip”, vertical (9:16), 30 s, 3 steps, filmed on a phone. How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer un tutoriel : comment créer une facture dans Facturo, l'appli d'essai “a tutorial: how to create an invoice in Facturo, the test app” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): What does it show? The app: I give its address and the steps Length 30 s Audience Industry professionals Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style clair-pedagogique Edits: none. The recorded steps, on the test app: Click “Nouvelle facture” In “Client”, type “Dupont SARL” Click “Enregistrer” Confirm in the dialog box During recording: First take redone: the test app followed the computer's dark mode and the typed name was hard to read. Second take in light appearance, kept; the first one was deleted. Privacy: the customer's email address is blurred automatically; the account name (Camille Durand) is fictitious and left as it is."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-explication","t":"Video ideas","s":"Explainer video","x":"Trainers, administrative departments, charities: explain how something works (a process, a procedure, a calculation). Explain a mechanism in one minute: a hook, the problem and its causes, the mechanism in three steps linked by arrows, a detail shown up close (a formula, a calculation), a figure as a chart, then the summary. The camera finds its close-ups on its own. 1 min 6 s or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative : du devis au paiement, le trajet d'une facture “an explainer video: from quote to payment, the journey of an invoice” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative sur le cycle de l'eau “an explainer video on the water cycle” /pulse-motion:creer explique comment fonctionne le tri sélectif dans notre commune “explain how recycling works in our town” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative pour le grand public : comment fonctionne une carte bancaire sans contact (“an explainer video for the general public: how a contactless bank card works”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative pour des comptables : le trajet d'une note de frais (“an explainer video for accountants: the journey of an expense claim”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative pour des collégiens : le mécanisme de la photosynthèse (“an explainer video for secondary school pupils: how photosynthesis works”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The hook: what we are going to understand, as a question or a journey 8 s 2 The problem in the centre with its causes around it; the key word circled 12 s 3 The mechanism in three steps, linked by arrows drawn one after the other 14 s 4 One step up close: a spreadsheet formula or a calculation building up 12 s 5 A figure that shows the scale, as a bar chart (the person's own data or a cited source) 12 s 6 The summary: the whole idea in one sentence 8 s Recommended styles: clair-pedagogique (recommended) · minimal · editorial . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo explicative : du devis au paiement, le trajet d'une facture “an explainer video: from quote to payment, the journey of an invoice” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 1 min Audience Industry professionals Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style clair-pedagogique Edits: none. Fixed by Claude after the check: “detail” scene rewritten: the formula overflowed its window and nothing new happened for 8 s (a third step of the formula, the rounding, added to the timeline) “probleme” scene rewritten: inside the automatic camera's container, the title was covered by the parts of the hub"},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-presentation-produit","t":"Video ideas","s":"Product presentation","x":"Freelancers, small businesses, marketing teams: present an offer on a website or in a meeting. Introduce a product or a service in 45 seconds: the problem, the answer, three benefits, a figure, the call to action. 45 s or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer la présentation de Plannette, une application qui organise les plannings d'une petite équipe “the presentation of Plannette, an app that organises a small team's schedules” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de 45 secondes pour présenter notre service de livraison de paniers de légumes “a 45-second video presenting our vegetable box delivery service” /pulse-motion:creer une présentation de notre logiciel de devis pour artisans “a presentation of our quoting software for tradespeople” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer la présentation d'une application pour partager les courses en colocation, pour le grand public (“the presentation of an app for sharing grocery shopping in a flatshare, for the general public”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer la présentation d'un outil de suivi de chantier, pour des conducteurs de travaux (“the presentation of a site-monitoring tool, for construction site managers”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer la présentation d'une application de révision par fiches, pour des lycéens (“the presentation of a flashcard revision app, for sixth-form students”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The problem the target audience faces 7 s 2 The answer: the product's name 7 s 3 3 concrete benefits 11 s 4 How it works, in 3 stages 8 s 5 A key figure that proves the promise 6 s 6 The call to action 6 s Recommended styles: minimal (recommended) · tech-sombre · energique . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer la présentation de Plannette, une application qui organise les plannings d'une petite équipe “the presentation of Plannette, an app that organises a small team's schedules” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 45 s Audience General public Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style minimal Edits: none."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-histoire","t":"Video ideas","s":"Story","x":"Charities, companies, project leaders: tell the story of an origin, a project or a success. Tell a journey in one minute: a situation, a difficulty, the turning point, the solution, before and after, the moral. 1 min or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire de notre jardin partagé, d'un terrain vague à un lieu de rencontre “the story of our community garden, from wasteland to meeting place” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo qui raconte comment notre atelier de réparation de vélos est né “a video telling how our bike repair workshop came about” /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire d'une boulangerie de village qui a changé sa façon de travailler “the story of a village bakery that changed the way it works” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire de la bibliothèque de notre quartier, pour les habitants (“the story of our neighbourhood library, for local residents”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire d'une équipe qui a réduit ses délais de moitié, pour des chefs de projet (“the story of a team that halved its lead times, for project managers”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire de l'invention de l'imprimerie, pour des élèves de cinquième (“the story of the invention of the printing press, for Year 8 pupils”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The starting point 8 s 2 The difficulty that blocks everything 10 s 3 The turning point, in one figure 9 s 4 How we got out of it, in 3 stages 11 s 5 Before and after 12 s 6 The moral of the story 8 s Recommended styles: editorial (recommended) · chaleureux · tech-sombre . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer l'histoire de notre jardin partagé, d'un terrain vague à un lieu de rencontre “the story of our community garden, from wasteland to meeting place” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 1 min Audience General public Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style editorial Edits: none."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-publication-sociale","t":"Video ideas","s":"Social media post","x":"Communicators, creators, shops: a post for Instagram, TikTok or LinkedIn. A 30-second vertical video that hooks in 2 seconds: a figure, three ideas, a tip, the punchline. 30 s or so, vertical (9:16). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour les réseaux : 3 astuces pour économiser l'eau à la maison “a social media video: 3 tips for saving water at home” /pulse-motion:creer une publication Instagram sur les bienfaits de la marche après le repas “an Instagram post on the benefits of walking after a meal” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo LinkedIn : 3 conseils pour des réunions plus courtes “a LinkedIn video: 3 tips for shorter meetings” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour les réseaux : 3 gestes pour mieux dormir (“a social media video: 3 habits for sleeping better”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo LinkedIn : 3 erreurs à éviter dans un devis, pour des artisans (“a LinkedIn video: 3 mistakes to avoid in a quote, for tradespeople”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour les réseaux : 3 méthodes pour réviser efficacement, pour des étudiants (“a social media video: 3 methods for revising effectively, for students”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The hook: the promise can be read within the first 2 seconds 5 s 2 A surprising figure 6 s 3 The 3 ideas, short 9 s 4 The tip to try today 6 s 5 The punchline and the invitation 6 s Recommended styles: energique (recommended) · minimal · editorial . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour les réseaux : 3 astuces pour économiser l'eau à la maison “a social media video: 3 tips for saving water at home” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 30 s Audience General public Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style energique Edits: none. Fixed by Claude after the check: I lengthened the hook to 5 s to leave time to read its 11 words."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-accueil","t":"Video ideas","s":"Welcoming a newcomer","x":"Human resources, team leaders, charities: welcome a new recruit, a volunteer, a member. Say welcome in one minute: what you will find there, the first three steps, where to find help. 1 min or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue pour les nouveaux arrivants de notre médiathèque “a welcome video for newcomers to our media library” /pulse-motion:creer l'accueil des nouveaux bénévoles de notre banque alimentaire “welcoming the new volunteers at our food bank” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de prise en main pour les nouveaux adhérents du club de sport “a getting-started video for the sports club's new members” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue pour les nouveaux habitants de la commune (“a welcome video for the town's new residents”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo d'accueil pour les nouveaux infirmiers du service de nuit (“an induction video for the new night-shift nurses”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue pour les nouveaux élèves de sixième (“a welcome video for the new Year 7 pupils”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 Say welcome 8 s 2 What you will find there 12 s 3 The first step 8 s 4 The second step 8 s 5 The third step 8 s 6 Where to find help, from the closest to the widest 9 s 7 A warm word to finish 7 s Recommended styles: chaleureux (recommended) · clair-pedagogique · minimal . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo de bienvenue pour les nouveaux arrivants de notre médiathèque “a welcome video for newcomers to our media library” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 1 min Audience General public Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style chaleureux Edits: none."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-pitch","t":"Video ideas","s":"Pitch","x":"Project leaders, start-ups: a competition, a jury, investors, a call for projects. Convince in one minute: the problem, the solution, the market in one figure, the business model, the team, the ask. 1 min or so, landscape (16:9). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de Potagine, une plateforme qui relie les maraîchers aux cantines scolaires, pour nos investisseurs “the pitch for Potagine, a platform connecting market gardeners with school canteens, for our investors” /pulse-motion:creer un pitch d'une minute pour présenter notre projet de recyclerie au jury du concours “a one-minute pitch presenting our second-hand reuse shop project to the competition jury” /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de notre application de covoiturage entre collègues “the pitch for our car-sharing app for colleagues” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de notre campagne de financement participatif pour une épicerie solidaire (“the pitch for our crowdfunding campaign for a community grocery shop”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer le pitch d'un logiciel de maintenance pour des directeurs d'usine (“the pitch for maintenance software, for factory managers”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de notre mini-entreprise pour le concours du lycée (“the pitch for our mini-company for the school competition”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The problem, real and measurable 8 s 2 The solution: the project's name 8 s 3 The market size in one figure 9 s 4 How the project makes money 10 s 5 The team and its strengths 12 s 6 What we are asking for 9 s Recommended styles: tech-sombre (recommended) · minimal · editorial . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer le pitch de Potagine, une plateforme qui relie les maraîchers aux cantines scolaires, pour nos investisseurs “the pitch for Potagine, a platform connecting market gardeners with school canteens, for our investors” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 1 min Audience Industry professionals Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style tech-sombre Edits: none."},{"u":"en/idees.html","a":"modele-annonce","t":"Video ideas","s":"Announcement","x":"Shops, charities, services: a launch, an opening, an event, a registration. Announce something new or an event in 30 seconds, vertically: the hook, the news, the benefit, the date, the invitation. 30 s or so, vertical (9:16). Sentences to copy /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce de l'ouverture de notre nouvelle boutique de vélos, le 15 mars “the announcement of the opening of our new bike shop, on 15 March” /pulse-motion:creer une annonce pour le lancement de notre nouvelle offre de cours du soir “an announcement for the launch of our new evening classes” /pulse-motion:creer une vidéo pour annoncer la fête de quartier du 21 juin “a video announcing the neighbourhood party on 21 June” For your audience For the general public: /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce du marché de Noël de la commune, le 14 décembre (“the announcement of the town's Christmas market, on 14 December”) For professionals: /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce d'un salon professionnel de la menuiserie, pour des artisans (“the announcement of a joinery trade show, for tradespeople”) For pupils or trainees: /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce des portes ouvertes du lycée, pour des collégiens et leurs parents (“the announcement of the school open day, for secondary school pupils and their parents”) The template’s scenes Scene Role Length 1 The hook: the promise can be read within the first 2 seconds 4 s 2 The news being announced 6 s 3 What it brings 9 s 4 The countdown to the big day 6 s 5 The invitation 6.5 s Recommended styles: energique (recommended) · editorial · chaleureux . How this video was made The opening request: /pulse-motion:creer l'annonce de l'ouverture de notre nouvelle boutique de vélos, le 15 mars “the announcement of the opening of our new bike shop, on 15 March” The answers to the questions (the recommended answers, the audience that suits the topic, and no voice): Length 30 s Audience General public Path Quick To add Nothing for now Music Music composed for the video Voice No voice Subtitles No voice, no subtitles Style energique Edits: none. Fixed by Claude after the check: I lengthened the hook to 4 s to leave time to read its 8 words. The examples are made by Pulse Motion from the first sentence of each video template, with the recommended answers, and no voice. The topics, places and figures are fictional."},{"u":"en/index.html","a":"","t":"Pulse Motion Wiki – motion design videos with Claude Code, no editing software","s":"","x":"The motion design plugin for Claude Code Your idea as an animated video, in minutes. You describe the video in your own words. Pulse Motion writes the timeline, asks you to approve it, animates each scene, checks readability on its own, then opens the video in your browser. An edit takes one sentence. Everything is made on your computer, with no cloud and no editing software. Pulse Motion is made for French speakers: the plugin’s questions, messages and reports are in French, and Claude answers in French while it uses the plugin. This English guide helps you discover it; the requests shown as examples stay in French, with their translation alongside. Start in 5 minutes Make my first video What to install Illustration: the frame of a video screen draws itself, with a title and a circle inside. Below it, a three-track timeline lights up, its keyframes appear and the playhead settles in place. Then a pulse wave sweeps across the screen: the video comes to life."},{"u":"en/index.html","a":"trois-etapes","t":"Pulse Motion Wiki – motion design videos with Claude Code, no editing software","s":"Create, watch, export","x":"The result One sentence is enough to get started. Here is a video made with Pulse Motion on the topic « la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires » (“the cold chain, for my trainees”), the one from the first tutorial. Your browser can’t play this video: download it . 1. Create One sentence and a few questions /pulse-motion:creer followed by your idea. You approve the timeline and the style. 2. Watch The video in your browser The preview opens on its own. An edit takes one sentence. 3. Export An MP4 ready to publish /pulse-motion:exporter makes the file on your computer. Start in 5 minutes"},{"u":"en/index.html","a":"pourquoi","t":"Pulse Motion Wiki – motion design videos with Claude Code, no editing software","s":"Publish more often, with videos that feel like you","x":"Why Pulse Motion An animated video holds attention far better than text. Yet few people publish them, nobody edits them on their own, and a consistent series of videos seems out of reach. Today there are three ways to go about it, and each one gets stuck somewhere. An online template tool Fast. But the videos all look alike, and you can’t control the pace. A freelance video editor Real expertise. But it’s expensive, it takes time, and every edit costs a round trip. Professional animation software Anything is possible. But it takes months to learn, and most people give up after a few evenings of tutorials. What Pulse Motion changes Your words are enough. You describe the video as you would to a colleague; Pulse Motion writes the timeline, you approve it, and it animates. It knows the craft. Reading pace, typography, contrast, sound at the right level: checked every time, and fixed on its own when it’s safe. A video that feels like you. A wide range of styles, your logo, your colours and fonts, your voice: no two videos look the same. The edits are yours. « Ralentis la scène 3 » (“Slow down scene 3”): you see the result straight away, whether it’s the night before your session or five minutes before you publish. Everything is made at home. On your computer, with no cloud and no editing software; the video belongs to you. See for yourself: videos made with Pulse Motion , each with the sentence that started it, two edits shown before and after, and three ways to use them."},{"u":"en/index.html","a":"parcours","t":"Pulse Motion Wiki – motion design videos with Claude Code, no editing software","s":"Six steps, from idea to MP4 file","x":"The path You type a command, then answer questions in your own words. Pulse Motion asks for only one approval: the timeline. On the guided path, it also asks for your approval of the style and of the final video. See all the commands Where to start Four ways in Install the tools first, then pick your pace: a first video in half an hour, a complete video in your colours and with your voice, or a ready-made idea to adapt. 1. Install Requirements Claude Code, Node.js, Chrome or Edge, then Pulse Motion itself. Every step can be checked. 2. Try First video A one-minute training video on the cold chain, in about 30 minutes, with /pulse-motion:creer . 3. Master Complete video Your logo and colours, your voice, subtitles, edits, then the high-fidelity MP4 export. (in French) Or else Start from an idea Pitch, announcement, welcome, tutorial… Eight finished video examples, each with the sentence to copy to make your own. What it can do A polished video, with nothing to learn Pulse Motion takes care of the craft: reading pace, French typography, contrast, sound at the right level. You stay in charge of what matters: the message. Twenty styles, your colours Clear and educational, warm, editorial, energetic, minimal, dark tech, silent info, technical blueprint, launch, whiteboard, blackboard, notebook, data story, isometric infographic, isometric album, bold crayon, paper cut-out, riso, pictograms, 1950s instruction manual. Your logo, colours and fonts apply to all of them. Sound included Music composed on your computer, your voice recorded in the preview, sound effects and subtitles timed to the words. (in French) Checks that fix things Text that overflows, a scene too short to read, dead time, typography: the safe cases are fixed on their own, and Pulse Motion tells you so. One edit, one sentence « Ralentis la scène 3 » (“slow down scene 3”), « mets mes couleurs » (“use my colours”), « annule » (“undo”): the preview reloads straight away, at the same point. The name of every move Pop-in, mask reveal, whip pan, zoom-through… 128 moves from the trade, each one shown in motion, with the sentence to ask for it. Particle effects Sparks on a key figure, confetti for the conclusion, snow or smoke for atmosphere: thirty-four effects, used with restraint, to watch in motion. Animated characters A character drawn on request, or your own mascot: it walks in, points to a figure, smiles, waves, and speaks with your voice. (in French) Two deliverables An MP4 file to publish, and an animated page that opens offline, ready to put on your website."},{"u":"en/index.html","a":"installer","t":"Pulse Motion Wiki – motion design videos with Claude Code, no editing software","s":"Pulse Motion in two commands","x":"Install and update Once Claude Code and Node.js are installed (see the requirements ), type these commands in Claude Code, one at a time. Install /plugin marketplace add jdelauney/pulse-motion /plugin install pulse-motion@pulse-motion Close and restart Claude Code: the /pulse-motion:… commands appear. Update From time to time, in a terminal: claude plugin marketplace update pulse-motion claude plugin update pulse-motion@pulse-motion Close and restart Claude Code: the new version loads at startup. Tip: to stop thinking about it, type /plugin in Claude Code, open Marketplaces , choose pulse-motion , then Enable auto-update . The details are in the requirements ."},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"","t":"Moves","s":"","x":"The move dictionary Moves The name of each move, so you can ask Claude for it: « un pop-in sur le titre » (“a pop-in on the title”), « un whip pan entre la scène 2 et la 3 » (“a whip pan between scenes 2 and 3”), or simply its number: « #001 ». Every preview is rendered by Pulse Motion itself."},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"mode-emploi","t":"Moves","s":"How to use it","x":"Watch : the previews play when they are on screen. Open a move’s card and copy the sentence to ask for it. Give it to /pulse-motion:retoucher on a video in progress, or slip it into your request to /pulse-motion:creer . On your computer, you can also choose by watching: say « montre-moi les mouvements » (“show me the moves”), tick the ones you like in the page that opens, then click « Envoyer à Claude » (Send to Claude). Search Pause all"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-entrees","t":"Moves","s":"Entrances and exits","x":"#001 Pop-in Apparition rebondie Grows from nothing, overshoots its size slightly, then settles Details To ask for it: Fais entrer le logo en pop-in, en 0,45 s, avec un léger rebond. “Bring the logo in with a pop-in, in 0.45 s, with a slight bounce.” When: An icon, a badge, a number that should catch the eye without being abrupt. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-pop\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 001 of Motion Prompt #002 Fade up Fondu montant Rises a few line heights as it appears Details To ask for it: Fais monter le sous-titre en fondu, sur 0,6 s, depuis 4 % de la hauteur. “Bring the subtitle in with a fade up, over 0.6 s, from 4% of the height.” When: The most neutral entrance, for almost any text. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-monte\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> #003 Slide in Glissé latéral Arrives from the side as it appears Details To ask for it: Fais glisser l'image depuis la gauche, en 0,6 s, sur 10 % de la hauteur. “Make the image slide in from the left, in 0.6 s, over 10% of the height.” When: An image or a block that adds to what is already there. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauche\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> #004 Scale in Zoom d'entrée Starts slightly smaller and grows to its full size as it appears Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître la carte en zoom, en 0,6 s, depuis 80 % de sa taille. “Make the card appear with a scale in, in 0.6 s, from 80% of its size.” When: A card, a photo, a box. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-zoom-entree\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> #005 Blur in De flou à net Appears blurred, then comes into focus Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître le titre de flou à net, en 0,6 s. “Make the title appear with a blur in, from blurred to sharp, in 0.6 s.” When: A soft mood, a memory, a calm reveal. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-net\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> #006 Mask reveal Révélé par masque The text rises from behind an invisible edge that stays still Details To ask for it: Révèle le titre par masque, en 0,7 s, ligne par ligne. “Bring the title in with a mask reveal, in 0.7 s, line by line.” When: An elegant, editorial title that “comes out” of the page. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-masque\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 005 of Motion Prompt #007 Drop in Chute avec rebond Falls from above and bounces when it hits the ground Details To ask for it: Fais tomber le chiffre d'en haut, en 0,9 s, avec des rebonds. “Make the number drop in from above, in 0.9 s, with bounces.” When: A number, a strong word, a playful tone. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-chute\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 123 of Motion Prompt #008 Anticipation Anticipation Pulls back a little before heading to its place Details To ask for it: Fais entrer la flèche avec une anticipation : elle recule d'un tiers puis part, en 0,8 s. “Bring the arrow in with anticipation: it pulls back by a third, then sets off, in 0.8 s.” When: Give an entrance momentum, cartoon-style. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-anticipe\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 121 of Motion Prompt #009 Squash & stretch Écrasement-étirement Squashes when it hits the ground, then regains its shape, like a ball Details To ask for it: Fais tomber le bouton avec un écrasement à l'arrivée, en 0,7 s. “Drop the button in with a squash & stretch on landing, in 0.7 s.” When: An object that should look supple, lively, elastic. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-ecrase\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 011 of Motion Prompt #010 Stagger Entrée en cascade The elements come in one after another, with a small offset Details To ask for it: Fais entrer les trois points de la liste en cascade, à 0,2 s d'écart. “Bring the three bullet points in with a stagger, 0.2 s apart.” When: A list, a grid of icons, several cards. Recipe: <h2 id=\"c1\" class=\"pm-m-monte\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></h2> <p id=\"c2\" class=\"pm-m-monte\" data-texte=\"texte\" data-start=\"#c1+0.2\"></p> Inspired by card no. 002 of Motion Prompt #011 Line draw Tracé de ligne A line draws itself, as if traced in pencil Details To ask for it: Trace la flèche du schéma en 1 s, de gauche à droite. “Draw the diagram's arrow with a line draw in 1 s, from left to right.” When: A diagram, an arrow, a drawn underline, a signature. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 20\" style=\"width: 60cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"1\"><path d=\"M2 10 H 98\" pathLength=\"100\" stroke=\"var(--c-accent)\" s… Inspired by card no. 004 of Motion Prompt #012 Fade out Sortie en fondu Moves down slightly as it fades away Details To ask for it: Fais sortir le premier titre en fondu à 2 s, en 0,5 s, avant que le second arrive. “Fade out the first title at 2 s, over 0.5 s, before the second one arrives.” When: Make room for the next element in the same scene. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-m-monte\" data-start=\"0.2\"><h2 class=\"pm-m-sortie\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"2\"></h2></div> #013 Stop-motion style Image par image Moves in jerks, like frame-by-frame animation Details To ask for it: Fais monter le titre par à-coups, en 6 images sur 0,6 s. “Make the title rise in a stop-motion style, in 6 frames over 0.6 s.” When: A handcrafted, cut-paper, home-made tone. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-saccade\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 128 of Motion Prompt #014 Burst Éclat Sparks burst out when something appears Details To ask for it: Fais jaillir des étincelles autour du chiffre quand il apparaît, pendant 1 s. “Make a burst of sparks shoot out around the number when it appears, for 1 s.” When: Highlight the arrival of a key figure or a result. Recipe: <h2 id=\"chiffre\" class=\"pm-m-pop\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.5\"></h2> <div data-effet=\"etincelles\" data-ancre=\"#chiffre\" data-start=\"#chiffre\" data-dur=\"1… Inspired by card no. 003 of Motion Prompt #112 Hand-drawn stroke Tracé à main levée The stroke wobbles slightly and goes back over itself, as if drawn in felt-tip pen Details To ask for it: Dessine la flèche du schéma à main levée, en 1 s. “Draw the diagram's arrow freehand with a hand-drawn stroke, in 1 s.” When: A diagram, a whiteboard explanation, a handcrafted or teaching tone. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 20\" style=\"width: 60cqh; margin-top: 40cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"1\"> <path data-main-levee d=\"M4 10 H 96\" pathLen… #133 Soft wipe Révélé en fondu balayé The element appears behind a soft edge that sweeps across it Details To ask for it: Révèle le texte de gauche à droite en 1,2 s, bord doux. “Reveal the text from left to right in 1.2 s, with a soft edge.” When: A softer entrance than a hard wipe, for a sentence or an image. Recipe: <p class=\"pm-m-masque-doux\" data-texte=\"texte\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-dur=\"1.2\" style=\"margin: 0; font: 600 5cqh/1.3 var(--f-texte)\"></p> #135 QR code build-up QR qui se compose The QR code builds up row by row, then stays still Details To ask for it: Ajoute un QR code vers notre site, qui se compose en 1 s, et laisse-le 5 s. “Add a QR code to our website that builds up in 1 s, and leave it for 5 s.” When: At the end of the video, for a sign-up link or a resource to download. Recipe: <svg class=\"pm-qr-compose\" data-qr=\"lien\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"width: 40cqh; height: 40cqh\"></svg>"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-texte","t":"Moves","s":"Text","x":"#015 Typewriter Machine à écrire The text writes itself letter by letter Details To ask for it: Écris la phrase en machine à écrire, une lettre toutes les 0,04 s. “Write the sentence with a typewriter effect, one letter every 0.04 s.” When: Code, a quotation, a technical or retro mood. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-lettres=\"frappe\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 006 of Motion Prompt #016 Wave text Vague de lettres The letters jump one after another, like a wave Details To ask for it: Fais onduler les lettres du titre en vague, à 0,02 s d'écart. “Make the title's letters ripple with a wave text effect, 0.02 s apart.” When: A cheerful title, an announcement, a word to bring to life. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-lettres=\"onde\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 130 of Motion Prompt #017 Word by word Mots qui montent The words rise one after another Details To ask for it: Fais monter la phrase mot par mot, à 0,12 s d'écart. “Bring the sentence up word by word, 0.12 s apart.” When: A sentence to read at the pace of the voice. Recipe: <p data-texte=\"texte\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-mots=\"monte\"></p> #018 Blur letters Lettres de flou à net Each letter appears blurred, then comes into focus Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître le titre lettre par lettre, de flou à net, à 0,05 s d'écart. “Make the title appear letter by letter with blur letters, from blurred to sharp, 0.05 s apart.” When: A soft, elegant title. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-lettres=\"flou\"></h2> #019 Falling letters Lettres qui tombent Each letter falls from above and bounces Details To ask for it: Fais tomber les lettres du titre une à une, à 0,05 s d'écart. “Make the title's letters fall one by one as falling letters, 0.05 s apart.” When: An energetic, playful title. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-lettres=\"chute\"></h2> #020 Text scramble Texte brouillé The letters scroll at random, then settle on the right text Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître le mot en texte brouillé, une lettre fixée toutes les 0,04 s. “Make the word appear as a text scramble, one letter locked in every 0.04 s.” When: Code, a password, a digital mood. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-lettres=\"brouille\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 129 of Motion Prompt #021 Count-up Compteur A number rolls quickly up to its value Details To ask for it: Fais compter le chiffre de 0 à 1 250 en 1,5 s. “Make the number count up from 0 to 1,250 in 1.5 s.” When: A key figure, a statistic, a result. Recipe: <p class=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"1.5\" data-tween=\"nombre 0→1250 cubic-out\" aria-label=\"1 250\"></p> Inspired by card no. 007 of Motion Prompt #022 Kinetic typography Typographie cinétique The words drop in rhythm while the frame tightens Details To ask for it: Fais tomber les mots du titre un par un, toutes les 0,25 s, avec un zoom lent de 15 %. “Make the title's words drop one by one in kinetic typography, every 0.25 s, with a slow 15% zoom.” When: A punchy sentence, a hook for social media. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→1.15 0+3\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"><h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-mots=\"chute\" data-pas=\"0.25\"></h2></d… Inspired by card no. 008 of Motion Prompt #023 Gradient text Texte en dégradé The text is filled with a gradient of the brand kit colours Details To ask for it: Mets le titre en dégradé à 90 degrés, de la couleur principale à la couleur d'accent. “Give the title a gradient text at 90 degrees, from the main colour to the accent colour.” When: A brand title, a hook. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-degrade pm-m-monte\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> #024 Highlight Surlignage A flat block of colour settles behind a word, like a highlighter pen Details To ask for it: Surligne le mot-clé à 1 s, en 0,6 s. “Highlight the keyword at 1 s, over 0.6 s.” When: Make one word stand out in a sentence. Recipe: <p><span class=\"pm-m-surligne\" data-texte=\"texte\" data-start=\"1\"></span></p> #025 Underline draw Soulignement tracé A line draws itself under the text Details To ask for it: Souligne le titre d'un trait qui se trace en 0,6 s. “Underline the title with an underline draw that traces in 0.6 s.” When: A section title, a conclusion. Recipe: <h2><span class=\"pm-m-souligne\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.5\"></span></h2> #085 Echo trail Échos Three pale afterimages follow the incoming text, then fold back into it Details To ask for it: Fais glisser le titre avec des échos derrière lui, repliés en 0,8 s. “Slide the title in with an echo trail behind it, folding back in 0.8 s.” When: A title that comes in fast, a word that should leave a trace: the eye follows the path travelled. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauche\" data-start=\"0.3\"> <h2 class=\"pm-m-echo\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> </div> #125 Code morph Code qui se transforme A formula or code builds up step by step: the shared pieces slide to their new place Details To ask for it: Montre la formule =SOMME(B2:B13), puis transforme-la en =MOYENNE(B2:B13) en 0,8 s quand je dis « moyenne ». “Show the formula =SUM(B2:B13), then turn it into =AVERAGE(B2:B13) with a code morph in 0.8 s when I say “average”.” When: A spreadsheet formula being built, code that evolves, a command being completed. Recipe: <figure data-code=\"formule\" data-start=\"0.2\"></figure> #129 Text on path Texte sur un tracé The text follows a curve and slides along its path Details To ask for it: Fais glisser le slogan le long d'un arc en 3 s. “Slide the tagline along an arc in 3 s.” When: A tagline, a journey, a road: the text takes the shape of what it tells. Recipe: <svg class=\"pm-sur-trace\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 400\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 10%; top: 18%; width: 80%\"> <path id=\"arc-1\" d=\"M50 350 Q500 0 950 350\" fill=\"… #130 Variable font weight animation Graisse qui respire The word slowly thickens, as if gaining confidence Details To ask for it: Fais passer le titre de maigre à gras en 1,6 s. “Take the title from light to bold in 1.6 s.” When: Stress a word without moving it or changing its colour. Recipe: <h1 class=\"pm-variable\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-dur=\"1.6\" data-tween=\"--pm-wght 300→800 sine-inout\" style=\"margin: 0; font-family: var(--f-titr… #131 Staggered weight reveal Lettres qui s'épaississent The letters go one after another from a thin stroke to a full one Details To ask for it: Fais s'épaissir les lettres du titre une à une, 0,04 s entre deux. “Thicken the letters of the title one by one, 0.04 s apart.” When: A title that asserts itself letter by letter, calmer than a bounce. Recipe: <h1 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-lettres=\"graisse\" style=\"margin: 0; font-family: var(--f-titres); font-weight: 800\"></h1> #132 Image-filled text Texte fenêtre The letters are windows onto an image sliding behind them Details To ask for it: Remplis le titre de la photo de la cuisine, qui glisse en 3 s. “Fill the title with the kitchen photo, sliding in 3 s.” When: A big title word that already carries the setting of the topic. Recipe: <h1 class=\"pm-texte-image\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-dur=\"3\" data-tween=\"--pm-texte-image-x 0%→100% sine-inout\" style=\"margin: 0; font: 900 18cqh… #140 Extruded text Texte en relief The letters gain thickness, as if cut out of a block Details To ask for it: Donne du relief au titre, une épaisseur de 0,08 em. “Give the title some depth, 0.08 em thick.” When: A poster title, a key figure, a game or retro mood. Recipe: <h1 class=\"pm-relief\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"margin: 0; font: 900 14cqh/1 var(--f-titres); --pm-relief: 0.08em\"></h1>"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-finition","t":"Moves","s":"Motion polish","x":"#026 Easing Accélération douce The motion speeds up then slows down, instead of moving at a constant speed Details To ask for it: Fais glisser la carte avec une courbe douce (sine-inout), en 0,8 s. “Slide the card with gentle easing (sine-inout), in 0.8 s.” When: Any motion: a constant speed looks mechanical. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"0.8\" data-tween=\"--x -20cqh→0cqh sine-inout\" style=\"transform: translateX(var(--x))\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 009 of Motion Prompt #027 Overshoot Dépassement Goes slightly past its place, then comes back Details To ask for it: Fais arriver le bouton avec un léger dépassement (back-out), en 0,6 s. “Bring the button in with a slight overshoot (back-out), in 0.6 s.” When: An interface element, a dynamic tone. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"0.6\" data-tween=\"--x -20cqh→0cqh back-out\" style=\"transform: translateX(var(--x))\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 010 of Motion Prompt #028 Bounce Rebond Arrives and bounces several times before stopping Details To ask for it: Fais arriver l'icône avec des rebonds (bounce-out), en 0,9 s. “Bring the icon in with a bounce (bounce-out), in 0.9 s.” When: A playful tone, a falling object. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"0.9\" data-tween=\"--y -20cqh→0cqh bounce-out\" style=\"transform: translateY(var(--y))\"></h2> #029 Elastic Élastique Oscillates around its place before settling, like a spring Details To ask for it: Fais arriver la pastille avec un effet élastique (elastic-out), en 1 s. “Bring the badge in with an elastic effect (elastic-out), in 1 s.” When: After a sudden stop, parts that keep moving. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"1\" data-tween=\"--s 0→1 elastic-out\" style=\"transform: scale(var(--s))\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 122 of Motion Prompt #030 Motion path Trajectoire courbe Moves in an arc instead of a straight line Details To ask for it: Fais voler l'avion en arc vers la droite, sur 60 % de la hauteur, en 2 s. “Fly the plane in an arc to the right along a motion path, over 60% of the height, in 2 s.” When: An object that flies, jumps or is thrown. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"2\" data-tween=\"--x 0cqh→60cqh sine-inout; --y 0cqh→-20cqh sine-arch\" style=\"transform: translate(var(--x), var… Inspired by card no. 126 of Motion Prompt #031 Shape morph Forme qui se transforme A shape smoothly turns into another Details To ask for it: Transforme l'octogone en étoile à 4 branches en 0,8 s. “Turn the octagon into a 4-pointed star with a shape morph in 0.8 s.” When: Move from one idea to another, an icon that changes state. Recipe: <style>@scope { @keyframes morphe { from { d: path('M50 10 L78 22 L90 50 L78 78 L50 90 L22 78 L10 50 L22 22 Z'); } to { d: path('M50 5 L60 40 L95 50 L60 60 L50 … Inspired by card no. 012 of Motion Prompt #084 Follow-through Suivi retardé A second element repeats the first one's motion, slightly later and slightly weaker Details To ask for it: Fais suivre le sous-titre derrière le titre, 0,09 s après lui et moitié moins loin. “Make the subtitle follow behind the title with follow-through, 0.09 s after it and half as far.” When: A title and its subtitle, a card and its shadow, an object and what it drags along: the whole thing feels supple. Recipe: <h2 id=\"meneur\" class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauche\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> <p class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauche\" data-texte=\"texte\" data-start=\"#meneur+0.09\" … #113 Line boil Ébullition des traits Hand-drawn lines redraw themselves a few times per second: the drawing quivers, alive Details To ask for it: Fais frémir les traits du schéma, 8 fois par seconde. “Make the diagram's lines quiver with line boil, 8 times per second.” When: A hand-drawn cartoon, a drawn scene that stays on screen for a long time. Recipe: <style>@scope { :scope { --pm-trait: main; --pm-ebullition: 8; } }</style> <p id=\"a\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 30cqh; top: 45cqh; font-size: 5cqh\" data-t…"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-valeur","t":"Moves","s":"Emphasis","x":"#032 Pulse Battement Grows and shrinks back, like a beating heart Details To ask for it: Fais battre le bouton 3 fois, de 8 %, toutes les 0,8 s. “Make the button pulse 3 times, by 8%, every 0.8 s.” When: Draw the eye to a call to action. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-pulse\" data-start=\"0.5\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 125 of Motion Prompt #033 Wiggle Tremblotement Small quick jolts, like an object fidgeting Details To ask for it: Fais trembloter la cloche 3 fois, de 3 degrés. “Make the bell wiggle 3 times, by 3 degrees.” When: An alert, a notification, an error. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-tremblote\" data-start=\"0.5\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 124 of Motion Prompt #034 Ping-pong loop Aller-retour Slides to one side then comes back, several times Details To ask for it: Fais faire 2 allers-retours à la flèche, de 6 %, en 1,2 s chacun. “Make the arrow do 2 back-and-forth ping-pong loops, by 6%, in 1.2 s each.” When: Show a direction, invite people to swipe or scroll. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" class=\"pm-m-va-vient\" data-start=\"0.5\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 127 of Motion Prompt #035 Light sweep Reflet lumineux A band of light crosses the element Details To ask for it: Fais passer un reflet sur le titre à 1 s, en 0,9 s. “Run a light sweep across the title at 1 s, over 0.9 s.” When: A logo, a product, a premium button. Recipe: <h2><span class=\"pm-m-reflet\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"1\"></span></h2> Inspired by card no. 022 of Motion Prompt #036 Impact shake Secousse d'impact The whole frame shakes when something lands Details To ask for it: Fais trembler l'image quand le titre touche le sol, à 0,63 s, pendant 0,3 s. “Shake the picture with an impact shake when the title hits the ground, at 0.63 s, for 0.3 s.” When: An impact, a number dropping, a strong reveal. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"secousse 1.2 0.63+0.3\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div><h2 class=\"pm-m-chute\" data-texte=\"titre… Inspired by card no. 024 of Motion Prompt #037 Punch in Zoom sur un détail The frame quickly tightens on a precise point Details To ask for it: Zoome vite sur le chiffre à 2 s : 1,4 fois, en 0,3 s, centré sur 40 % de la largeur. “Punch in quickly on the number at 2 s: 1.4 times, in 0.3 s, centred at 40% of the width.” When: Emphasise a detail, a reaction, a number. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→1.4 2+0.3 back-out; x 50→40 2+0.3 back-out\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"><h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2></d… #038 Particles Pluie de confettis A cloud of small pieces flies up and falls back down Details To ask for it: Fais tomber des confettis sur la conclusion pendant 3 s. “Make confetti particles fall on the conclusion for 3 s.” When: A success, a celebration, a conclusion. Recipe: <h2 class=\"pm-m-pop\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\"></h2> <div data-effet=\"confettis\" data-start=\"0.5\" data-dur=\"3\"></div> Inspired by card no. 015 of Motion Prompt #081 Press Appui The button sinks in ever so slightly under the finger, then comes back Details To ask for it: Fais un appui sur le bouton à 1 s, en 0,2 s. “Add a press on the button at 1 s, over 0.2 s.” When: A clicked button, a pressed key, a card chosen in an interface shown on screen. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-m-appui\" data-start=\"1\" style=\"padding: 2.5cqh 6cqh; border-radius: 2cqh; background: var(--c-primaire); color: var(--c-fond)\"><p data-texte=\"tex… #082 Chart draw-on Graphique qui se trace A chart's bars rise one after another from their base, and the number counts along with them Details To ask for it: Fais monter les 4 barres du graphique l'une après l'autre, 60 ms d'écart, puis compte jusqu'à 90. “Make the chart's 4 bars rise one after another with a chart draw-on, 60 ms apart, then count up to 90.” When: A chart, a histogram, a change in figures: the final frame shows the data. Recipe: <div data-start=\"0.3\" data-dur=\"1.5\" style=\"display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 3cqh; height: 36cqh; border-bottom: 0.5cqh solid var(--c-texte)\"> <div cl… #083 Beat punch Pulsation sur le temps The element beats on every beat of the music: a small hit that drops back straight away Details To ask for it: Fais battre le logo sur les temps de la musique, de 1,2 % ; plus fort sur chaque mesure. “Make the logo beat punch on the music's beats, by 1.2%; harder on each bar.” When: A logo, a title or a key word that lives with the music, in a rhythmic video. Recipe: <div data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\"> <h2 class=\"pm-v-cogne\" data-texte=\"titre\"></h2> <p class=\"pm-v-cogne\" data-texte=\"texte\" style=\"--pm-dur: calc(4 * var(--p… #115 Hand underline Soulignement à la main A slightly curved line draws itself under the element, on the spoken word Details To ask for it: Souligne le titre d'un trait dessiné en 0,6 s. “Underline the title with a hand underline drawn in 0.6 s.” When: Emphasise a word, a conclusion, without hiding it. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"souligne\" data-cible=\"#mot\"… #116 Circle Entourer An ellipse draws itself around the element, as if with a pen Details To ask for it: Entoure le prix en 0,8 s quand je dis « cher ». “Circle the price in 0.8 s when I say “expensive”.” When: The number or word that must not be missed. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"entoure\" data-cible=\"#mot\" … #117 Box Encadrer A frame draws itself around the element Details To ask for it: Encadre la règle à retenir en 0,6 s. “Box the rule to remember in 0.6 s.” When: A rule, a definition, what needs to be remembered. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"encadre\" data-cible=\"#mot\" … #118 Highlight sweep Surligner à la main A translucent flat colour spreads under the text from left to right, on the spoken word Details To ask for it: Surligne la phrase clé en 0,6 s. “Highlight the key sentence with a highlight sweep in 0.6 s.” When: A key word in a sentence, on a light or dark background. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"surligne\" data-cible=\"#mot\"… #119 Strike Barrer A slightly slanted line strikes through the element Details To ask for it: Barre l'ancien prix en 0,6 s. “Strike through the old price in 0.6 s.” When: A misconception, an old figure, what we no longer do. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"barre\" data-cible=\"#mot\" da… #120 Cross Croix Two lines draw themselves one after the other in a cross over the element Details To ask for it: Fais une croix sur la mauvaise réponse en 0,6 s. “Put a cross over the wrong answer in 0.6 s.” When: A mistake, a bad practice, a rejected option. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"croix\" data-cible=\"#mot\" da… #121 Bracket Accolade A curly bracket draws itself to the left of the element Details To ask for it: Mets une accolade devant la liste en 0,6 s. “Put a bracket in front of the list in 0.6 s.” When: Group elements, show that they belong together. Recipe: <p id=\"mot\" style=\"font-size: 6cqh; text-align: center; margin-top: 40cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <i data-annoter=\"accolade\" data-cible=\"#mot\"… #134 Shape mask reveal Image dans une forme An image is revealed through a growing shape Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître la photo dans un cercle qui grandit en 1 s. “Reveal the photo in a circle that grows in 1 s.” When: A portrait, a product, a place: the image comes in through a shape from the brand kit. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-masque-forme\" data-start=\"0.2\" data-dur=\"1\" data-tween=\"--pm-forme-taille 0%→100% back-out\" style=\"width: 40cqh; height: 40cqh; background: linea… #137 Card flip Carte qui se retourne A card turns over and shows its back: the question, then the answer Details To ask for it: Retourne la carte à 1 s, en 0,8 s, pour montrer la réponse. “Flip the card at 1 s, in 0.8 s, to show the answer.” When: Question and answer, before and after, word and definition. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-espace-3d\" style=\"width: 50cqh; height: 32cqh\"> <div class=\"pm-m-retourne\" data-start=\"1\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%\"> <div class=\"pm-3d-fa… #138 Rotating cube Cube qui tourne A cube turns a quarter turn on each beat and shows one face after another Details To ask for it: Fais tourner un cube de 4 faces, un quart de tour par seconde. “Turn a 4-sided cube, a quarter turn per second.” When: Four ideas, four steps or four words following one another in the same place. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-espace-3d\" style=\"width: 30cqh; height: 30cqh\"> <div class=\"pm-m-cube\" data-start=\"0.5\"> <div style=\"display: grid; place-items: center; backgrou… #139 Tilted screen mockup Écran incliné A screenshot settles at an angle, like a screen on a desk Details To ask for it: Pose la capture inclinée de 22° en 1,2 s. “Lay the screenshot down tilted by 22° in 1.2 s.” When: Present a document or an interface with depth, without real 3D. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-3d\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"--pm-3d-pose: rotateX(22deg) rotateZ(-8deg); width: 90cqh; height: 52cqh; border-radius: 1.5cqh; background: var(--c-…"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-transitions","t":"Moves","s":"Scene transitions","x":"#039 Cross dissolve Fondu enchaîné The next scene fades in on top Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène 3 en fondu enchaîné. “Move to scene 3 with a cross dissolve.” When: The default, discreet transition. Recipe: \"transition\": \"fondu\" Inspired by card no. 044 of Motion Prompt #040 Slide transition Glissement The next scene slides in from the right Details To ask for it: Passe de l'étape 1 à l'étape 2 en glissement. “Go from step 1 to step 2 with a slide transition.” When: Move forward through a sequence (steps). Recipe: \"transition\": \"glissement\" #041 Push transition Poussée The next scene pushes the previous one out of the frame Details To ask for it: Fais pousser la scène 2 par la scène 3. “Make scene 3 push scene 2 out with a push transition.” When: A logical sequence, a slideshow. Recipe: \"transition\": \"poussee\" Inspired by card no. 137 of Motion Prompt #042 Whip pan Panoramique filé A very fast, blurred movement carries you to the next scene Details To ask for it: Fais un whip pan entre la scène 2 et la 3. “Do a whip pan between scene 2 and scene 3.” When: An energetic change, a fast pace. Recipe: \"transition\": \"whip\" Inspired by card no. 043 of Motion Prompt #043 Wipe Balayage A moving edge uncovers the next scene Details To ask for it: Change de partie à la scène 4 avec un balayage. “Switch section at scene 4 with a wipe.” When: Change section. Recipe: \"transition\": \"balayage\" Inspired by card no. 045 of Motion Prompt #044 Barn door Rideau The next scene opens from the centre Details To ask for it: Ouvre la scène 6, la conclusion, en rideau. “Open scene 6, the conclusion, with a barn door.” When: An opening, an elegant reveal. Recipe: \"transition\": \"rideau\" #045 Iris / mask transition Ouverture en cercle The next scene appears inside a growing circle Details To ask for it: Ouvre la scène 2 dans un cercle qui grandit. “Open scene 2 inside a growing circle with an iris / mask transition.” When: A playful, retro tone, a focus. Recipe: \"transition\": \"cercle\" Inspired by card no. 017 of Motion Prompt #046 Blinds transition Volets The next scene appears in widening strips Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène 4 avec des volets. “Move to scene 4 with a blinds transition.” When: A graphic tone, a presentation. Recipe: \"transition\": \"volets\" Inspired by card no. 138 of Motion Prompt #047 Zoom transition Zoom The next scene comes closer as it grows Details To ask for it: Fais approcher la scène 5 en zoom. “Bring scene 5 closer with a zoom transition.” When: Go into a detail. Recipe: \"transition\": \"zoom\" #048 Zoom through Zoom traversant We travel through the growing scene to arrive in the next one Details To ask for it: Traverse la scène 1 en zoom pour arriver dans la 2. “Zoom through scene 1 to arrive in scene 2.” When: Dive into the subject, change scale. Recipe: \"transition\": \"traversee\" Inspired by card no. 020 of Motion Prompt #049 Flip Retournement The next scene turns over like a card being flipped Details To ask for it: Retourne la scène 3 comme une carte pour montrer la réponse. “Flip scene 3 like a card to show the answer.” When: A question then its answer, a before/after. Recipe: \"transition\": \"retournement\" Inspired by card no. 021 of Motion Prompt #050 Jump cut Coupe franche A hard switch from one scene to the next, with no effect Details To ask for it: Coupe net entre la scène 2 et la 3. “Do a clean jump cut between scene 2 and scene 3.” When: A fast pace, a strong contrast. Recipe: \"transition\": \"coupe\" Inspired by card no. 041 of Motion Prompt #051 Organic dissolve Dissolution organique The scene dissolves into grains, with a coloured edge Details To ask for it: Fais une dissolution organique de 0,8 s vers la scène suivante. “Do a 0.8 s organic dissolve into the next scene.” When: A soft, lively transition. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"dissolution\",\"duree\":0.8} #052 Ripple Ondulation The picture ripples like water during the transition Details To ask for it: Passe au souvenir avec une ondulation de 1 s. “Move to the memory with a 1 s ripple.” When: A memory, a dream, a change of era. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"ondulation\",\"duree\":1} #053 Pixelate Pixellisation The picture breaks into large squares, then changes Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène suivante en pixellisant, en 0,8 s. “Move to the next scene with a pixelate, in 0.8 s.” When: Digital world, video games. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"pixels\",\"duree\":0.8} Inspired by card no. 141 of Motion Prompt #054 Glitch Glitch The picture scrambles like a broken screen Details To ask for it: Fais un glitch de 0,5 s entre les deux scènes. “Do a 0.5 s glitch between the two scenes.” When: A break, a bug, a tech mood. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"glitch\",\"duree\":0.5} Inspired by card no. 023 of Motion Prompt #055 Ink wipe Balayage à l'encre A living edge, like ink, covers the picture Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène suivante avec un balayage à l'encre de 1 s. “Move to the next scene with a 1 s ink wipe.” When: An artistic, fluid tone. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"balayage-organique\",\"duree\":1} Inspired by card no. 019 of Motion Prompt #107 Light leak Fuite de lumière A flash of warm light, in the brand colour, carries one scene into the next Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène suivante par une fuite de lumière de 1 s. “Move to the next scene with a 1 s light leak.” When: A warm tone, a memory, an opening. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"fuite-lumiere\",\"duree\":1} #108 Burn Brûlure The picture burns away in patches: a glowing front, ash and sparks uncover the next scene Details To ask for it: Brûle la scène vers la suivante en 1,2 s. “Burn the scene into the next one in 1.2 s.” When: A strong break: the old disappears, we turn the page. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"brulure\",\"duree\":1.2} #109 Gravitational lens Lentille The picture bends into a ring around a dark core, then the next one unfolds Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène suivante par une lentille de 1 s. “Move to the next scene with a 1 s gravitational lens.” When: Change scale, dive into a subject, an idea that pulls you in. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"lentille\",\"duree\":1} #110 Iris wipe Iris coloré A shape opens from the centre (circle, diamond or star), edged with a ring in the brand colour Details To ask for it: Ouvre la scène suivante par un iris en étoile de 0,9 s. “Open the next scene with a 0.9 s star-shaped iris wipe.” When: A focus, an opening, a nod to cinema; the “circle” transition (#045) is enough for a simple round shape with no colour. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"iris\",\"duree\":0.9,\"reglages\":{\"forme\":2}} #111 Domain warp Déformation liquide The picture twists like a liquid with coloured veins, then untwists onto the next scene Details To ask for it: Passe à la scène suivante par une déformation liquide de 1,2 s. “Move to the next scene with a 1.2 s liquid domain warp.” When: A transformation, an idea taking new shape, a changing world. Recipe: \"transition\": {\"shader\":\"deformation\",\"duree\":1.2}"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-camera","t":"Moves","s":"Camera","x":"#056 Dolly in Zoom avant lent The camera slowly moves closer to the subject Details To ask for it: Fais un zoom avant lent de 10 % pendant toute la scène. “Do a slow 10% dolly in throughout the scene.” When: Build tension, keep a picture alive. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→1.1 0+4\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 027 of Motion Prompt #057 Pan Panoramique The camera turns from left to right to follow the scene Details To ask for it: Fais un panoramique de gauche à droite sur 4 s. “Do a pan from left to right over 4 s.” When: Reveal a setting wider than the frame. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.3→1.3 0+4; x 35→65 0+4\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"><… Inspired by card no. 025 of Motion Prompt #058 Tilt Bascule verticale The camera tilts from top to bottom Details To ask for it: Descends la caméra du ciel vers le sol, en 3 s. “Tilt the camera down from the sky to the ground, in 3 s.” When: Reveal something at the top or the bottom. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.3→1.3 0+3; y 35→65 0+3\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"><… Inspired by card no. 026 of Motion Prompt #059 Crane up Montée en grue The camera rises as it pulls back to reveal the whole picture Details To ask for it: Fais monter la caméra en reculant sur 4 s. “Do a crane up, pulling back over 4 s.” When: An opening, a conclusion that rises above it all. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.4→1 0+4; y 70→50 0+4\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"></h… Inspired by card no. 031 of Motion Prompt #060 Dutch angle Plan incliné The frame tilts diagonally Details To ask for it: Penche le cadre de 6 degrés pendant la scène d'alerte. “Give the frame a 6-degree Dutch angle during the alert scene.” When: Unease, tension, an offbeat tone. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"rotation 0→6 0+1\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"></h2> Inspired by card no. 036 of Motion Prompt #061 Rack focus Bascule de mise au point Focus shifts from the foreground to the background Details To ask for it: Passe la netteté du premier plan au fond à 2 s, en 0,6 s. “Rack focus from the foreground to the background at 2 s, over 0.6 s.” When: Guide the eye from one element to another. Recipe: <div class=\"decor\" data-decor data-start=\"2\" data-dur=\"0.6\" data-tween=\"--flou 1cqh→0cqh\" style=\"filter: blur(var(--flou))\"></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-s… Inspired by card no. 033 of Motion Prompt #062 Handheld Caméra à l'épaule A slight float, like a handheld camera Details To ask for it: Ajoute un léger effet caméra à l'épaule pendant 6 s. “Add a slight handheld camera effect for 6 s.” When: A documentary, lively, intimate tone. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.05→1.05 0+6\"><div class=\"pm-m-epaule\" data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"6\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"><div class=\"decor\" data-de… Inspired by card no. 034 of Motion Prompt #063 Parallax Parallaxe Near layers move faster than distant ones Details To ask for it: Fais un travelling avec parallaxe : fond à 20 %, collines à 60 %. “Do a tracking shot with parallax: background at 20%, hills at 60%.” When: Give a setting depth. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.2→1.2 0+4; x 40→60 0+4\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor data-profondeur=\"0.2\"></div><div class=\"decor\" data-decor data-profondeur=\"0.6\"><… Inspired by card no. 016 of Motion Prompt #064 Dolly zoom Effet vertigo The subject keeps its size while the background distorts Details To ask for it: Fais un effet vertigo : le fond grossit de 40 % en 2 s, le titre ne bouge pas. “Do a dolly zoom (vertigo effect): the background grows by 40% in 2 s, the title stays still.” When: A shock, a moment of realisation. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→1.4 0.5+2 sine-inout\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"></h… Inspired by card no. 032 of Motion Prompt #086 Log zoom Zoom continu The camera dives into the picture at a steady pace: each doubling of the zoom takes the same time Details To ask for it: Fais un zoom continu de 1 à 6 en 4 s, qui plonge régulièrement. “Do a continuous log zoom from 1 to 6 in 4 s, diving in steadily.” When: A big zoom (a map, a detail of a picture, a dive into a diagram): an ordinary zoom looks slow, then rushes. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→6 0+4 log\"><div class=\"decor\" data-decor></div></div> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.3\" style=\"position: relative\"></h2> #126 Auto-director camera Caméra automatique The camera finds its own shots: opening on the picture, moving in when there is time to look, a final pull-back, never cutting off any text Details To ask for it: Laisse la caméra bouger toute seule dans cette scène de 8 s. “Let the auto-director camera move on its own in this 8 s scene.” When: A fully composed explanatory scene (diagram, hub, steps) that builds up along with the voice. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"auto\"> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 14cqh; top: 18cqh; margin: 0\"></h2> <p data-texte=\"texte\" data… #127 Focus close-up Gros plan sur un élément The automatic camera moves in on a chosen element for a few seconds, without cutting off its neighbours Details To ask for it: Fais un gros plan de 3 s sur le prix quand je dis « cher ». “Do a 3 s focus close-up on the price when I say “expensive”.” When: Emphasise a number, a word, a line of code, just as the voice mentions it. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"auto\"> <h2 id=\"titre\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 20cqh; top: 30cqh; margin: 0\"></h2> <p data-texte=\"… #128 Pull-back reveal Recul final To finish, the camera pulls back and shows the whole idea at once Details To ask for it: Termine la scène par un recul de 1,4 s qui montre tout le schéma. “End the scene with a 1.4 s pull-back reveal that shows the whole diagram.” When: After a close-up, to show how the pieces fit together. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1.6→1 3+1.4; x 30→50 3+1.4; y 40→50 3+1.4\"> <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 20cqh; top: 30cqh; m… #136 Ken Burns effect Ken Burns The photo slowly slides or moves closer within its frame Details To ask for it: Fais défiler mes photos doucement, un zoom de 1,12 par photo. “Pan gently across my photos, with a 1.12 zoom on each.” When: Still photos (places, people, archives) that need to come alive without distracting. Recipe: <div data-ken-burns=\"auto\" data-start=\"0\" style=\"position: absolute; inset: 8cqh 20cqh; border-radius: 2cqh\"> <svg viewBox=\"0 0 160 90\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMi…"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-temps","t":"Moves","s":"Time","x":"#065 Slow motion Ralenti The action plays out more slowly Details To ask for it: Passe la chute au ralenti, de 1 s à 3 s, à moitié de la vitesse. “Put the fall in slow motion, from 1 s to 3 s, at half speed.” When: Savour a gesture, an impact. Recipe: <div data-vitesse=\"1+2 x0.5\"><h2 class=\"pm-m-chute\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.8\"></h2></div> Inspired by card no. 037 of Motion Prompt #066 Speed ramp Variation de vitesse The action speeds up then slows down within the same shot Details To ask for it: Accélère l'entrée ×2 pendant 1 s, puis ralentis à moitié pendant 1 s. “Speed ramp the entrance: ×2 for 1 s, then slow down to half speed for 1 s.” When: A dynamic pace, an emphasis. Recipe: <div data-vitesse=\"0.5+1 x2; 1.5+1 x0.5\"><h2 class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauche\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.5\" style=\"--pm-dur: 2s\"></h2></div> Inspired by card no. 038 of Motion Prompt #067 Freeze frame Arrêt sur image The action stops for a moment, then resumes Details To ask for it: Fige l'image à 1,2 s pendant 1,5 s, en la passant en gris. “Freeze frame at 1.2 s for 1.5 s, turning the picture grey.” When: A key moment, a “this is where…”. Recipe: <div data-gel=\"1.2+1.5\" data-camera=\"saturation 1→0.4 1.2+0.3; saturation 0.4→1 2.7+0.3\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"><h2 class=\"pm-m-glisse-gauch… Inspired by card no. 040 of Motion Prompt #068 Fast forward Accéléré The action plays much faster Details To ask for it: Passe la préparation en accéléré ×4 pendant 2 s. “Fast forward the preparation ×4 for 2 s.” When: Summarise a long step. Recipe: <div data-vitesse=\"0.5+2 x4\"><h2 class=\"pm-m-va-vient\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.5\" style=\"--pm-fois: 6\"></h2></div>"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-vie","t":"Moves","s":"Living scenery","x":"#069 Sway Balancement Sways gently around its base, like a plant in a breath of air Details To ask for it: Fais balancer la plante de 2 degrés, lentement, en 4 secondes. “Make the plant sway by 2 degrees, slowly, over 4 seconds.” When: A plant, grass, a hanging sign, a lantern: scenery that lives without drawing the eye. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 60 100\" style=\"height: 50cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <g class=\"pm-v-balance\" data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\"> <path d=\"M30 100 Q28 62 31 22\" stro… #070 Flicker Flamme A flame dancing in two waves: it leans and stretches from its base Details To ask for it: Une flamme qui danse sur la bougie, deux vagues, de 3 degrés. “A flame flickering on the candle, two waves, by 3 degrees.” When: A candle, a torch, a drawn campfire; for a fire with volume, the particle effects. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 40 90\" style=\"height: 45cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <rect x=\"14\" y=\"54\" width=\"12\" height=\"36\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"var(--c-primaire)\"/> <g class=\"pm-v-f… #071 Steam Volutes Wisps rise in swirls, grow and fade away Details To ask for it: Fais monter trois volutes de vapeur au-dessus de la tasse, toutes les 2,4 s. “Make three wisps of steam rise above the cup, every 2.4 s.” When: A coffee, a soup, a machine heating up. Recipe: <div data-decor data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"position: relative; width: 26cqh; height: 36cqh\"> <svg class=\"pm-v-vapeur\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 30\" style=\"pos… #072 Bob Flottement Bobs gently up and down, tilting slightly, as if resting on water Details To ask for it: Fais flotter la bouée de 0,8 % de la hauteur, en 3,4 s. “Make the buoy bob by 0.8% of the height, over 3.4 s.” When: An object on water, a balloon, a bubble, a weightless icon. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-v-flotte\" data-decor data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"width: 24cqh; height: 24cqh; border-radius: 50%; border: 4cqh solid var(--c-accent); … #073 Glow Lueur A halo grows and shrinks back very gently, like a breathing light Details To ask for it: Fais respirer le halo de la lampe de 4 %, en 3 s. “Make the lamp's halo glow and breathe by 4%, over 3 s.” When: A lamp, a light bulb, a button to highlight without making it pulse. Recipe: <div data-decor data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"position: relative; display: grid; place-items: center\"> <div class=\"pm-v-lueur\" style=\"width: 40cqh; hei… #074 Twinkle Scintillement A star stays still, then tightens and shines for a moment Details To ask for it: Fais scintiller les cinq étoiles, chacune à son moment, toutes les 2,8 s. “Make the five stars twinkle, each at its own moment, every 2.8 s.” When: A night sky, a bright idea, small scattered sparkles. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 120 60\" style=\"height: 40cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <g data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" fill=\"var(--c-accent)\"> <path class=\"pm-v-scintille\" d=\"M… #075 Gear train Engrenage Gears turn together, at a constant speed, in opposite directions Details To ask for it: Fais tourner les deux engrenages : un tour en 6 s pour la grande roue de 16 dents. “Turn the two gears in a gear train: one turn in 6 s for the large 16-tooth wheel.” When: A machine, a process, a method that “turns”. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 60\" style=\"height: 40cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <g data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" fill=\"var(--c-primaire)\" stroke=\"var(--c-primaire)\"> <g c… #076 Buzz Vibreur A short burst of jolts, then calm: a vibrating phone Details To ask for it: Fais vibrer le téléphone : 4 secousses de 3 degrés en 0,45 s, toutes les 1,6 s. “Make the phone buzz: 4 jolts of 3 degrees in 0.45 s, every 1.6 s.” When: A notification, a call, an alarm clock. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-v-vibre\" data-decor data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"width: 16cqh; height: 28cqh; border-radius: 2.5cqh; border: 1.2cqh solid var(--c-prima… #077 Idle jiggle Attente A waiting object jiggles: it flattens on one side, then the other Details To ask for it: Fais attendre le colis en se dandinant de 2 %, en deux temps de 270 ms. “Make the parcel wait with an idle jiggle of 2%, in two 270 ms beats.” When: A simple object or mascot waiting its turn, an icon biding its time. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-v-attend\" data-decor data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"width: 26cqh; height: 20cqh; border-radius: 3cqh; background: var(--c-accent)\"></div> #078 Cloud drift Dérive A cloud moves slowly, at a constant speed, throughout the scene Details To ask for it: Fais dériver les nuages vers la droite, à 4 % de la hauteur par seconde, le plus lointain à 2. “Make the clouds drift to the right, at 4% of the height per second, the farthest at 2.” When: A sky, a landscape, a background that should live without any sudden movement. Recipe: <div data-decor style=\"display: grid; gap: 6cqh; justify-items: start; width: 70cqh\"> <div class=\"pm-v-nuage\" data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"--pm-vitess… #079 Ripples Ondes Rings appear at the centre, widen and fade, one after another Details To ask for it: Fais partir trois ondes depuis le point, toutes les 2,6 s. “Send three ripples out from the dot, every 2.6 s.” When: A signal, a drop in water, a point to draw attention to. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" style=\"height: 50cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <g data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--c-accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"> <… #080 Grow Pousse A plant grows: the stem draws itself in two spurts, then the leaves and petals open Details To ask for it: Fais pousser la fleur en 1,6 s, puis balancer de 2 degrés. “Make the flower grow in 1.6 s, then sway by 2 degrees.” When: Growth, a project that grows, an idea blossoming. Recipe: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 60 100\" style=\"height: 50cqh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <g class=\"pm-v-balance\" data-start=\"1.6\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"--pm-ampleur: 2\"> <g data-s…"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-compositions","t":"Moves","s":"Compositions","x":"#087 Logo marquee Défilement de logos A row of logos scrolls with no apparent end, at a constant speed, with no visible seam Details To ask for it: Fais défiler les logos de nos clients en bandeau, un tour en 20 s. “Scroll our clients' logos in a logo marquee, one loop in 20 s.” When: A project's clients, partners or funders, in a strip under a title. Recipe: <div data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"scene\" style=\"width: 100%; overflow: hidden\"> <div class=\"pm-v-defile\"> <span style=\"display: block; width: 14cqh; height: 8cqh; b… #088 Before-after wipe Avant/après balayé A line sweeps across the picture from left to right and reveals the “after” over the “before” Details To ask for it: Montre l'avant/après : la ligne balaie l'image en 1,2 s. “Show the before/after with a before-after wipe: the line sweeps across the picture in 1.2 s.” When: A transformation: a renovated room, a corrected document, a spreadsheet before and after the training. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-m-avant-apres\" data-start=\"0.5\" data-dur=\"1.2\" data-tween=\"--pm-balai 0→100 cubic-inout\" style=\"width: 80cqh; height: 44cqh; border-radius: 2cqh\"… #089 Collapse to pill Effondrement en pastille The block clears its content, shrinks into a pill and disappears, and the logo pops up in its place Details To ask for it: Fais s'effondrer la carte en pastille en 0,7 s, puis fais surgir le logo. “Collapse the card to a pill in 0.7 s, then make the logo pop up.” When: A video ending: the last message shrinks and makes way for the logo. Recipe: <div style=\"display: grid; place-items: center\"> <div id=\"bloc\" class=\"pm-m-pastille\" data-start=\"1.2\" data-dur=\"0.7\" style=\"grid-area: 1 / 1; padding: 4cqh 8cq… #090 Flood circle Cercle d'inondation A circle of colour starts from a point and covers the whole picture in a flash Details To ask for it: Fais partir un cercle d'inondation depuis le bouton, à 80 % et 70 % de l'image, en 0,32 s. “Send a flood circle out from the button, at 80% and 70% of the picture, in 0.32 s.” When: A brisk move to the next scene, from where you have just clicked or looked. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></h2> <div class=\"pm-m-inonde\" data-decor data-start=\"1.5\" data-dur=\"0.32\" style=\"--pm-x: 80; --pm-y: 70\"></div> #091 Selection wheel Roue de sélection A list scrolls in a one-line window and stops dead on the choice Details To ask for it: Fais tourner la roue jusqu'au 5e choix en 1,4 s, sans rebond. “Spin the selection wheel to the 5th choice in 1.4 s, with no bounce.” When: A choice among several (a day, an offer, a job), an answer that lands. Recipe: <div data-start=\"0.4\" data-dur=\"1.4\" style=\"height: 8cqh; overflow: hidden; border-block: 0.4cqh solid var(--c-accent)\"> <div class=\"pm-m-roue\"> <div style=\"hei… #092 Tile wall Mur de vignettes A wall of thumbnails seen at an angle appears in a wave from one corner, then the camera moves from one to the next Details To ask for it: Fais apparaître le mur de 12 vignettes en onde, 60 ms entre deux, puis un gros plan sur l'une. “Make the tile wall of 12 thumbnails appear in a wave, 60 ms apart, then do a close-up on one of them.” When: Past work, products, team photos: lots of pictures at once, then one in detail. Recipe: <div data-camera=\"zoom 1→1.6 1.6+1.2 quart-inout; x 50→62 1.6+1.2 quart-inout; y 50→42 1.6+1.2 quart-inout\" style=\"display: grid; place-items: center; position:… #093 Helix wall Hélice Thumbnails wrapped in a helix turn around a vertical axis; those facing away are hidden Details To ask for it: Fais tourner l'hélice de 12 vignettes d'un tiers de tour en 6 s. “Turn the helix wall of 12 thumbnails by a third of a turn in 6 s.” When: A collection (products, faces, past work) to show with depth, as an opening or a closing. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-m-helice\" data-start=\"0\" data-dur=\"6\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 60cqh\"> <div class=\"pm-m-helice-vignette\" style=\"--i: -5; border-radius: 2cqh; … #114 Connector arrow Flèche qui relie An arrow draws itself from one element to another, stopping at the edge of each, with its label Details To ask for it: Trace une flèche de Client à Serveur en 0,7 s quand je dis « serveur ». “Draw a connector arrow from Client to Server in 0.7 s when I say “server”.” When: An exchange, a cause and its effect, a circuit, a diagram that builds up along with the voice. Recipe: <p id=\"client\" style=\"position: absolute; left: 25cqh; top: 45cqh; font-size: 5cqh\" data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.2\"></p> <p id=\"serveur\" style=\"position: ab… #122 Bar chart grow Barres qui poussent A bar chart where each bar grows from zero, its value counting along with it Details To ask for it: Fais pousser les barres 2023, 2024 et 2025 une par une quand la voix dit chaque année, en 0,9 s chacune. “Make the 2023, 2024 and 2025 bars grow one by one as the voice says each year, in 0.9 s each.” When: Quantities to compare, a before/after, a year-on-year progression. Recipe: <figure data-graphique=\"ventes\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"width: 100cqh; height: 56cqh\"></figure> #123 Line chart draw Courbe qui se trace A curve draws itself point by point, and a gradient area appears beneath it Details To ask for it: Trace la courbe des ventes de janvier à juin en 1,6 s, en passant par chaque mois quand la voix le dit. “Draw the sales curve from January to June in 1.6 s, passing through each month as the voice says it.” When: A change over time, a trend, a season. Recipe: <figure data-graphique=\"mois\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"width: 110cqh; height: 56cqh\"></figure> #124 Donut chart fill Anneau qui se remplit A ring (or a disc) fills up slice by slice, each label arriving with its slice Details To ask for it: Remplis l'anneau des trois parts en 1,2 s, avec « Images 55 % » quand sa part est complète. “Fill the donut chart's three slices in 1.2 s, with “Images 55%” once its slice is complete.” When: The parts of a whole, five at most: budget, breakdown, origin. Recipe: <figure data-graphique=\"parts\" data-start=\"0.2\" style=\"width: 100cqh; height: 60cqh\"></figure>"},{"u":"en/mouvements.html","a":"categorie-logiciel","t":"Moves","s":"Software demos","x":"#094 Cursor click Clic du pointeur The pointer moves to the button in an arc, pauses and clicks: the button sinks in, a circle tightens, you hear the click Details To ask for it: Fais cliquer le pointeur sur le bouton Envoyer à 1,6 s. “Make the pointer click the Send button at 1.6 s.” When: Show where to click in a piece of software, on a screenshot or a mock-up of the screen. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #095 Hover Survol The pointer comes to rest on an element without clicking Details To ask for it: Fais passer le pointeur sur la liste à 1,2 s. “Make the pointer hover over the list at 1.2 s.” When: Show a tooltip that appears on hover, or point to a spot on the screen before talking about it. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #096 Double click Double-clic Two clicks close together, 0.15 s apart, each with its press, its circle and its sound Details To ask for it: Fais un double-clic sur la liste à 1,6 s. “Double-click on the list at 1.6 s.” When: Open a file, enter a folder, select a word in the software being shown. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #097 Human typing Saisie au clavier The text is typed in small irregular bursts, like a real hand, with the text cursor blinking Details To ask for it: Tape le nom du client dans le champ à 0,5 s, comme au clavier. “Type the client's name into the field at 0.5 s, as if on a keyboard.” When: A field being filled in in the software being shown, a search, a typed command. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0.5\" data-lettres=\"saisie\"></h2> #098 Spotlight Projecteur The whole screen darkens except the area being talked about Details To ask for it: Mets un projecteur sur le bouton Envoyer de 1 s à 3 s. “Put a spotlight on the Send button from 1 s to 3 s.” When: A precise area of a busy screen: a total, a button, a column. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #099 Focus ring Anneau de focus A ring in the accent colour surrounds the element and pulses gently Details To ask for it: Entoure le champ d'un anneau qui pulse de 1 s à 3 s. “Surround the field with a pulsing focus ring from 1 s to 3 s.” When: Point out a field or a button without darkening the rest. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #100 Dim around Assombrir autour The rest of the scene fades to leave only the screen Details To ask for it: Fais pâlir tout autour de l'écran de 1 s à 3 s. “Dim everything around the screen from 1 s to 3 s.” When: A screen surrounded by text or scenery, at the moment it needs to be looked at on its own. Recipe: <h2 data-texte=\"titre\" data-start=\"0\"></h2> <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; ins… #101 Zoom to target Zoom sur l'étape The screen moves closer to the element being talked about, then comes back Details To ask for it: Zoome sur le bouton Envoyer de 1 s à 3,4 s. “Zoom to the Send button from 1 s to 3.4 s.” When: A detail too small to read on the full screen: a field, a button, a number. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: grid; gap: 6%; align-content… #102 Follow the cursor Zooms qui suivent les clics On each click, the screen moves closer to the target, slides to the next one, then pulls back at the end Details To ask for it: Fais suivre les clics par la caméra sur les deux clics à 1,2 s et 2,4 s. “Make the camera follow the cursor on the two clicks at 1.2 s and 2.4 s.” When: A path of several clicks on a large screen, so that every gesture can be read. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" data-film-camera=\"suivre\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; display: gr… #103 Notification Notification A generic notification slides in from the corner of the picture Details To ask for it: Fais arriver une notification en haut à droite à 1 s. “Bring in a notification at the top right at 1 s.” When: A message received, an app alert, a reminder shown in a tutorial. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-bloc pm-bloc-notification pm-m-glisse-droite\" data-start=\"1\" style=\"--pm-ampleur: 14\"> <span class=\"pm-bloc-pastille\"><svg class=\"pm-bloc-icone\" … #104 Toast Message bref A short message with a tick rises at the bottom of the picture Details To ask for it: Fais monter le message « Facture enregistrée » en bas à 1 s. “Bring up the “Invoice saved” toast at the bottom at 1 s.” When: Confirm that an action has succeeded (saved, sent), or warn of an error. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-bloc pm-bloc-toast pm-m-monte\" data-start=\"1\" style=\"--pm-ampleur: 3\"> <svg class=\"pm-bloc-icone\" data-icone=\"circle-check\"></svg> <p class=\"pm-b… #105 Copy button Copier, puis Copié A command in a dark pill; the Copy button turns into Copied with a tick Details To ask for it: Montre la commande et fais passer Copier à Copié à 2 s. “Show the command and switch the copy button from Copy to Copied at 2 s.” When: A command or code to copy; with the terminal variant, a series of typed commands and their responses. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-bloc pm-bloc-commande pm-m-pop\" data-start=\"0.3\"> <code class=\"pm-bloc-code\" data-texte=\"texte\"></code> <span class=\"pm-bloc-copier\" data-start=\"… #106 3D reveal Révélation 3D A screenshot tilted backwards straightens up and lies flat Details To ask for it: Fais se redresser la capture de l'écran en 1,2 s à 0,2 s. “Make the screenshot straighten up with a 3D reveal, in 1.2 s, at 0.2 s.” When: Present a screen or a software product at the start of a demo. Recipe: <div class=\"pm-3d\" data-start=\"0.2\"> <div class=\"pm-ecran\" data-adresse=\"app.exemple.fr\" style=\"--pm-ecran-l: 92cqh\"> <div style=\"position: absolute; inset: 0; … Names, meanings and sentence ideas inspired by Motion Prompt by Shohei Ohtaki (MIT licence), translated and adapted to the Pulse Motion engine. Every move is produced by the Pulse Motion engine."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"","t":"Requirements","s":"","x":"Before you start Requirements Everything to install before your first video with Pulse Motion. Never installed developer software before? This page is made for you: every step is explained click by click and ends with a simple check."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"introduction","t":"Requirements","s":"What you’ll need","x":"Pulse Motion works inside Claude Code , Anthropic’s assistant. It makes your videos on your computer : no online service, no extra account, no video sent anywhere. You won’t have to write any code: you’ll download, click “Next”, and copy a few commands that this page gives you. Tool What it’s for Required? VS Code and the Claude Code extension Where you chat with Claude, next to your video’s files. Recommended (the terminal or Claude Desktop work too) A Claude subscription Claude Code needs a Pro, Max, Team or Enterprise plan, or a Console account. Yes Node.js 20 or later Runs Pulse Motion: assembly, checks, preview, export. Nothing else to install alongside it. Yes Chrome or Edge Shows the video preview, checks the rendered video and makes the MP4. Yes ffmpeg Makes the MP4 as a high-fidelity export , with clean sound, and imports voice recordings in any format. Optional: Pulse Motion offers it at your first export Python and transcription Times the subtitles and animations to the actual words of your voice, and splits a recording of the whole video. Optional: Pulse Motion offers it when you add a voice Tip: already have Claude Code and Node.js for another plugin, Pulse for example? Go straight to the Chrome or Edge step, then to installing the plugin ."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"vscode","t":"Requirements","s":"Install VS Code","x":"VS Code (Visual Studio Code) is a free editor from Microsoft: it shows your video’s files (the timeline, the scenes), and it’s where you’ll chat with Claude. Windows Open code.visualstudio.com/download and click the big Windows button (Windows 10 or 11). Open the downloaded file ( VSCodeUserSetup-x64-….exe , in your Downloads folder). Then, in the installer: licence: tick I accept the agreement , then Next ; install folder and Start menu folder: keep everything, Next ; Additional Tasks : keep Add to PATH ticked. You can also tick the two “Add ‘Open with Code’ action” boxes: right-clicking a folder will then open it in VS Code; Install , then Finish . Where to find it afterwards: Start menu, “Visual Studio Code”. macOS Open code.visualstudio.com/download and click the big Mac button (the “Universal” version, for every Mac). Open the downloaded file, then drag Visual Studio Code into the Applications folder. Open it from Applications. The first time, macOS asks you to confirm: click Open . Where to find it afterwards: Applications folder, “Visual Studio Code”. VS Code’s menus are in English by default, and this page gives the English name of every menu. What you should see: VS Code opens on a welcome page (“Welcome”). On Windows, a blue “Windows protected your PC” window appears Check that the file really comes from code.visualstudio.com, then click More info and Run anyway . I’d rather have VS Code in French Open the list of extensions ( Ctrl + Shift + X , or Cmd + Shift + X on a Mac), search for “French Language Pack” published by Microsoft, install it, then restart VS Code."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"node","t":"Requirements","s":"Install Node.js","x":"Node.js runs Pulse Motion: it assembles the video, checks it, serves the preview and makes the export. Pulse Motion needs no other component: nothing else to download. Pulse Motion needs Node.js 20 or later . Take the version marked LTS (“long-term support”, the most stable). If Claude Code and the plugin are already there but Node.js isn’t, Pulse Motion offers to install it for you, with your approval (on Windows, and on macOS with Homebrew); it then finds it without a restart. Windows Open nodejs.org/fr/download . At the top, check that the selected version is marked LTS . Further down, click the Windows Installer (.msi) button. Open the downloaded file. Then, in the installer: first screen: Next ; licence: tick the acceptance box, then Next ; install folder and list of features: keep everything, Next ; “Tools for Native Modules” screen: leave the box unticked , then Next . Pulse Motion doesn’t use these tools; Install , then Yes when Windows asks for permission, and finally Finish . macOS Open nodejs.org/fr/download . At the top, check that the selected version is marked LTS . Further down, click the macOS Installer (.pkg) button. Open the downloaded file, then click Continue on each screen, Agree to the licence, and Install . Your Mac asks for your login password: type it in. Finish with Close . Node.js has no window: it works behind the scenes. Close VS Code completely, reopen it, open the terminal (menu Terminal → New Terminal ), then check: node --version What you should see: a number starting with v20 or higher, for example v24.21.0 . The version shown is older than 20 An older version of Node.js is already installed. Install the LTS version over it from nodejs.org, then close and reopen VS Code. Pulse Motion also reminds you at the start of every session for as long as the version is too old. Later on, Pulse Motion says « node introuvable » (“node not found”) even though it’s installed Claude Code reads the list of installed software when it starts. Close VS Code completely and reopen it (or Claude Desktop, or the terminal, depending on how you work)."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"claude","t":"Requirements","s":"The Claude subscription and the Claude Code extension","x":"Claude Code is Anthropic’s assistant that works with you on the files in a folder. The extension adds it to VS Code, in a chat panel. Take out a subscription Claude Code needs a Claude Pro, Max, Team or Enterprise account, or a Console account (pay as you go). The free plan isn’t enough. If you’re starting out on your own, the Pro plan is the simplest. Open claude.com/pricing . Choose a plan and click its button. Create your account (or sign in), then pay for the subscription. Make a note of the email address you used: it’s the one you’ll sign in with in VS Code. Install the extension In VS Code, press Ctrl + Shift + X (Windows) or Cmd + Shift + X (macOS): the list of extensions opens on the left. In the search box, type Claude Code . Choose the one published by Anthropic , then click Install . Open the panel and sign in Click the spark-shaped icon in the vertical bar on the left, or ✻ Claude Code at the bottom right of the window. The first time, a sign-in screen appears: click Sign in , sign in in your browser with your subscription account, and accept. Go back to VS Code: the Claude panel shows a text box. In the text box, type / : the list of available commands opens. This is how you’ll run Pulse Motion’s commands, such as /pulse-motion:creer . What you should see: when you type / , a list of commands appears above the text box. Source: using Claude Code in VS Code . The spark-shaped icon doesn’t appear Restart VS Code. Or open the command palette ( Ctrl + Shift + P , or Cmd + Shift + P on a Mac), type Developer: Reload Window and confirm. The panel suggests upgrading to a paid plan The account you used doesn’t have a compatible subscription. Check the email address you signed in with."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"terminal","t":"Requirements","s":"Install Claude Code for the terminal","x":"The copy of Claude Code that comes with the extension only serves its panel. The plugin update commands, on the other hand, are typed in the terminal. So install Claude Code for the terminal too: just once, and then it keeps itself up to date. In the VS Code terminal (menu Terminal → New Terminal ): Windows The line must start with PS (PowerShell). Paste this command, then press Enter : irm https://claude.ai/install.ps1 | iex macOS Paste this command, then press Enter : curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash Close VS Code completely, reopen it, open the terminal, then check: claude --version What you should see: a version number followed by (Claude Code) . Source: the official Claude Code documentation . “claude is not recognized” or “command not found: claude” Close VS Code completely and reopen it. If the message is still there, follow the “Troubleshooting” part of the terminal guide . On Windows: “irm is not recognized” The terminal is the Command Prompt (CMD), not PowerShell. In the VS Code terminal, open the small arrow next to the + and choose PowerShell , then run the command again."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"navigateur","t":"Requirements","s":"Chrome or Edge","x":"Pulse Motion uses a browser from the Chrome family for three things: showing the preview of the video, checking the rendered video (overflowing text, contrast, flashes) and making the MP4 . It runs them without a window, behind the scenes. Windows: Microsoft Edge is already installed. Nothing to do. macOS: install Google Chrome from google.com/chrome , or Microsoft Edge from microsoft.com/edge . Safari and Firefox aren’t enough. What you should see: Chrome or Edge opens on your computer. Pulse Motion says « Chrome ou Edge introuvable » (“Chrome or Edge not found”) even though one of them is installed The browser is installed in an unusual place. Put its path in the PM_NAVIGATEUR environment variable, then restart VS Code. You can ask Claude to guide you: « aide-moi à régler PM_NAVIGATEUR » (“help me set PM_NAVIGATEUR”). I open the preview in Firefox or Safari The video plays, but voice recording and the MP4 button need Chrome or Edge. Copy the preview address (it starts with http://localhost: ) into Chrome or Edge."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"plugin","t":"Requirements","s":"Install Pulse Motion and keep it up to date","x":"Pulse Motion is a plugin (an add-on for Claude Code), stored in a catalogue called pulse-motion . First you add the catalogue, then the plugin. Install, in VS Code In the Claude Code panel, type /plugins , then press Enter : the Manage plugins window opens. Click the Marketplaces tab. In the box for adding a catalogue, type jdelauney/pulse-motion and confirm. Go back to the Plugins tab. Search for pulse-motion , then click Install . Choose Install for you : Pulse Motion will be available in all your folders. Close VS Code completely and reopen it. Or: install in Claude Code In the Claude Code panel, or in Claude Code started from a terminal (the claude command), type one line at a time: /plugin marketplace add jdelauney/pulse-motion /plugin install pulse-motion@pulse-motion Choose Install for you (user scope) if asked, then restart Claude Code. Check In the Claude Code panel, type /pulse-motion: (without pressing Enter ). What you should see: the list of Pulse Motion commands appears, from /pulse-motion:creer to /pulse-motion:voix (twelve in all, all described here ). Update Pulse Motion’s fixes only reach you after an update. In the VS Code terminal: claude plugin marketplace update pulse-motion claude plugin update pulse-motion@pulse-motion Then close VS Code completely and reopen it: the new version loads at startup. Stop thinking about it: automatic updates In the VS Code terminal, type claude , then press Enter . Type /plugin , then press Enter . Open the Marketplaces tab (the Tab key moves from one tab to the next). Choose pulse-motion , then Enable auto-update . Quit with exit . Sources: the Pulse Motion repository and installing and managing plugins . The /pulse-motion: commands don’t appear Close VS Code completely and reopen it. If they’re still missing, type /plugins : pulse-motion must be listed in the Plugins tab, with its switch turned on. A plugin that couldn’t load shows the reason on its line. Adding the catalogue fails Check your internet connection and the exact spelling: jdelauney/pulse-motion . Then try again."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"ffmpeg","t":"Requirements","s":"Optional: ffmpeg, for the high-fidelity export","x":"ffmpeg is a small piece of free software that makes the video in high quality, with clean sound. Without it, Pulse Motion still exports, directly in the browser. With it, the export is high-fidelity : the browser’s real frames, and sound measured at the right level. The simplest way: do nothing for now. At your first /pulse-motion:exporter , Pulse Motion checks whether ffmpeg is there and offers to install it, with your approval. If you’d rather install it yourself: Windows In the VS Code terminal: winget install --id Gyan.FFmpeg -e --accept-source-agreements --accept-package-agreements macOS With Homebrew installed, in the terminal: brew install ffmpeg Close VS Code completely and reopen it, then check: ffmpeg -version What you should see: a line starting with ffmpeg version . On Linux, Pulse Motion gives me a command to type The installation needs your password: Pulse Motion can’t run it for you. Type the command it gives you in a terminal, then tell it you’re done."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"transcription","t":"Requirements","s":"Optional: voice transcription","x":"When you add your voice, transcription finds the exact moment of each word. The subtitles and animations are then timed to your actual words. It’s essential for splitting a recording of the whole video into scenes. Without it, everything still works: the words are timed as well as possible. It runs on your computer, with Python and a speech recognition model (about 800 MB, or 250 MB for the lighter version). The simplest way, once again: wait for Pulse Motion to offer it during /pulse-motion:creer . With your approval, it installs Python if needed, then transcription, in a few minutes. One sentence, one approval, no restart: ask « installe ce qui manque » (“install whatever is missing”), or accept « Préparer l’ordinateur pendant que je travaille » (Prepare the computer while I work) during /pulse-motion:creer . Pulse Motion shows you the list (what each tool is for, its size, how long it takes), you answer « Tout installer » (Install everything), and it installs one tool after another, in the background. A freshly installed Python or Node.js is found in its usual place, without restarting Claude Code; it only asks you to restart as a last resort, if a tool still can’t be found. Installing transcription failed Pulse Motion tells you in one sentence and carries on without it: the video is made, and the words are timed as well as possible. You can try again later by asking « installe la transcription » (“install the transcription”)."},{"u":"en/prerequis.html","a":"verification","t":"Requirements","s":"Final check","x":"Create an empty folder, for example chaine-du-froid , wherever you keep your documents. One video = one folder. In VS Code, menu File → Open Folder… , choose that folder. If VS Code asks, trust its authors: it’s your folder. Open the Claude Code panel and type /pulse-motion: . What you should see: Pulse Motion’s commands appear. You’re ready for the First video tutorial."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/index.html","a":"","t":"Tutorials","s":"","x":"Learn by doing The tutorials Two training videos, a film with characters, then a screen-recorded software tutorial, from the first message to the MP4 file. You type a command, Pulse Motion asks you questions, and you answer in your own words. Before you start, check the requirements . Tutorial 1 · About 30 minutes First video “The cold chain”, a one-minute training video on the quick path: the timeline, the style, the preview, one edit, then the MP4. With composed music and no voice. Tutorial 2 · About 1 h 30 min Full video (in French) “The 5 kitchen hygiene habits”, on the guided path: your logo and your colours, your voice recorded in the studio, subtitles, sound effects, edits, then the high-fidelity export. Tutorial 3 · Advanced · About 5 hours Recreating “Vibe Connection” (in French) A mock silent film, 1 min 40 s long, in square format for LinkedIn: animated characters acting out a comedy, seven composed sequences, a camera, a freeze frame, particle effects, music timed to the picture and original sound effects. Tutorial 4 · About 40 minutes Filming a piece of software (in French) “Creating an invoice in Facturo”, a 30-second tutorial filmed on the demo app: describe the journey, sign in, blur, pick your take, know what stays on your computer, and import a recording of your screen. Which one to choose? In order Tutorial 1 walks you through the whole journey, quickly. Tutorial 2 goes through each step in depth and adds everything that makes a video truly yours. Tutorial 3 assumes you have done the first two: it builds a whole film, with characters, sequence by sequence. Tutorial 4, shorter, comes after the first one: it films an application. Tutorial 1: first video Tutorial 2: full video Tutorial 3: Vibe Connection Path Quick: a single approval, on the timeline Guided: three approvals (timeline, style, final video) Guided, from a written script, then each sequence reworked Brand kit The style’s colours Your logo, your colours, your font, your glossary A full brand kit given as colour codes, a font in three weights Scenes The style’s ready-made scenes The style’s ready-made scenes Seven composed scenes: camera, parallax, freeze frame, fast motion, animated letters, particle effects (smoke, explosion, confetti, fireworks) Characters None None The Tousfous: a single drawing for every role, four poses made for the film, groups, objects held in hand, hands that join and glances, up to seven characters per scene Sound Composed music Music, your voice, subtitles, sound effects Composed music timed to the picture, around thirty sound effects including 21 original sounds made for the film, no voice Edits Just one Text, pace, music, effect, and « annule » (“undo”) One per sequence and per beat, checked on screen Export MP4, in the browser or high-fidelity High-fidelity MP4 with ffmpeg, and the subtitle files Square high-fidelity MP4, 1080 × 1080 at 25 frames per second, for LinkedIn"},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"","t":"First video","s":"","x":"Tutorial 1 · About 30 minutes First video Claire is a food hygiene trainer. She wants a short video to open her next session: “The cold chain”, in one minute. You are going to create it with her, watch it in your browser, edit it with a single sentence, then export it as an MP4. This tutorial follows the quick path : you only approve the timeline and the style, and Pulse Motion does everything else. To keep things simple, the video will have composed music but no voice. You will add your voice, your logo and your colours in tutorial 2 (in French). The answers shown here are examples: give your own, or pick another topic. Pulse Motion talks to you in French in the conversations below (that is what you will see); an English translation follows each one. Your browser cannot play this video: download it ."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"avant","t":"First video","s":"Before you start","x":"Goal: an empty folder, open in VS Code, with the Claude Code panel ready and Pulse Motion installed. Check that everything is installed with the Requirements page: Claude Code, Node.js 20 or later, Chrome or Edge, and the pulse-motion plugin. Create an empty folder called chaine-du-froid , wherever you keep your documents. One video = one folder : each new video gets its own. In VS Code, open the File menu → Open Folder… and choose chaine-du-froid . If VS Code asks whether you trust the authors, answer Yes, I trust the authors : it is your folder. Open the Claude Code panel: click ✻ Claude Code , bottom right, or the spark-shaped icon in the left-hand bar. Expected result: when you type /pulse-motion in the panel’s input box, the /pulse-motion:creer command appears in the list. The /pulse-motion:… commands do not appear The plugin is not installed, or VS Code was not restarted after the installation. Follow the Install Pulse Motion step, then close and reopen VS Code."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"creer","t":"First video","s":"Describe the video","x":"Goal: give the topic and answer the first round of questions. /pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires “the cold chain, for my trainees” Pulse Motion first checks that everything is ready (Node.js, empty folder), then asks four questions. Each question has one answer marked (Recommandé) (Recommended) at the top: if in doubt, pick it. /pulse-motion:creer la chaîne du froid pour mes stagiaires pulse-motion etat . Durée ? → 30 s / 1 min (Recommandé pour une formation courte) / 3 min / 5 min Public ? → Grand public / Professionnels de la restauration / Stagiaires en formation Parcours ? → Rapide (Recommandé) : je valide seulement le déroulé / Accompagné : je valide aussi le style et la vidéo finale À ajouter ? → Mon logo et mes couleurs / Rien pour l'instant 1 min · Stagiaires en formation · Rapide · Rien pour l'instant In English You: /pulse-motion:creer the cold chain, for my trainees Claude: Length? → 30 s / 1 min (Recommended for a short training session) / 3 min / 5 min. Audience? → General public / Catering professionals / Trainees. Path? → Quick (Recommended): I only approve the timeline / Guided: I also approve the style and the final video. Anything to add? → My logo and my colours / Nothing for now You: 1 min · Trainees · Quick · Nothing for now Tip: the topic is enough for Pulse Motion to guess the video’s genre (here, a training video) and its format (16:9, landscape). For a vertical video meant for social media, say so in the topic: « … pour Instagram » (“… for Instagram”). Expected result: Pulse Motion moves on to the sound round."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"son","t":"First video","s":"Choose the sound","x":"Goal: music composed for the video, with no voice for this first try. Musique ? → Une musique composée pour la vidéo (Recommandé), lumineuse, posée, qui avance / J'ai un fichier / Pas de musique Voix ? → Je l'enregistre (Recommandé) / J'ai un fichier / Pas de voix Sous-titres ? → Oui (Recommandé) / Non Une musique composée · Pas de voix · Non Sans voix, il n'y aura pas de sous-titres : ils reprennent le texte dit. In English Claude: Music? → Music composed for the video (Recommended), bright, calm, moving forward / I have a file / No music. Voice? → I’ll record it (Recommended) / I have a file / No voice. Subtitles? → Yes (Recommended) / No You: Composed music · No voice · No Claude: Without a voice, there will be no subtitles: they repeat the spoken text. The music is composed on your computer , for this video: it starts gently on the first scene, builds before the last one, and ends on a chord. Pulse Motion describes its mood in three words; you can change it later with a single sentence (« plus calme » (“calmer”), « une autre variante » (“another variation”)). Everything is explained on the Sound page (in French)."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"deroule","t":"First video","s":"Read the timeline and choose the style","x":"Goal: approve the list of scenes, and choose the look of the video from three styles. Pulse Motion writes the timeline : the list of scenes in order, like the running order of a TV show. One idea per scene, with enough time to read each text. It shows it to you as a table: No. Scene On-screen text Length 1 Title Food hygiene · The cold chain · 1 minute to grasp the essentials 8 s 2 Figure The temperature not to exceed: 4 °C, for fresh products in the fridge 9 s 3 List The 4 links in the chain: lorry, cold room, display counter, fridge 13 s 4 Comparison Good habits: do, avoid 13 s 5 Alert Never refreeze 10 s 6 Conclusion Cold means safety 9.5 s Total length: about 1 minute. This is the timeline of the example shipped with the plugin (its on-screen texts are in French; they are translated here). Meanwhile, a page opens in your browser: the style comparison . Your first two scenes are drawn there in the three styles recommended for a training video. Have a look, then come back to VS Code. Voici le déroulé : 6 scènes, environ 1 minute. → Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible et rassurant (Recommandé) / Je valide, avec le style accueillant, doux et humain / Je valide, avec le style net, sûr et essentiel / Je veux changer quelque chose Je valide, avec le style posé, lisible et rassurant In English Claude: Here is the timeline: 6 scenes, about 1 minute. → I approve, with the calm, readable and reassuring style (Recommended) / I approve, with the welcoming, soft and human style / I approve, with the clean, confident and essential style / I want to change something You: I approve, with the calm, readable and reassuring style Tip: this is the moment to correct a text or the order of the scenes: choose « Je veux changer quelque chose » (I want to change something) and say it in your own words (« remplace “vitrine” par “rayon frais” » (“replace ‘display counter’ with ‘chilled aisle’”)). Pulse Motion makes the correction and shows you the timeline again. Once the scenes are animated, everything can still be changed, but a correction here costs less. Expected result: the folder now contains deroule.json , and Pulse Motion announces that it is building the video."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"fabrication","t":"First video","s":"Let it build","x":"Goal: wait while the scenes are written and checked. You have nothing to do. Pulse Motion hands each scene to an animator , a specialised assistant, and all six work at the same time. Each one starts from a ready-made scene of the style (title, figure, list…) and adapts it to your texts. Pulse Motion also decides on the effects: here, for example, sparkles on the 4 °C figure. Then come the checks . They make sure each text has time to be read, nothing overflows, nothing stays still for too long, and French typography rules are followed. Safe cases are fixed automatically , and Pulse Motion tells you so: pulse-motion assembler . pulse-motion controler . J'ai allongé la scène 3 d'une seconde pour laisser le temps de lire, et remplacé les guillemets droits par des guillemets français dans la scène 4. In English Claude: I made scene 3 one second longer to leave time for reading, and replaced the straight quotes with French quotation marks in scene 4. Expected result: the folder contains scenes/ (six files) and sortie/la-chaine-du-froid.html , the animated video. Pulse Motion says the render could not be checked Neither Chrome nor Edge was found. The video is built, but the on-screen checks (overflow, contrast) could not be run. See Chrome or Edge ."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"apercu","t":"First video","s":"Watch the video","x":"Goal: play the video in the preview and learn to find your way around it. The preview opens by itself in your browser. It is the video itself, with a playback bar at the bottom. Click ▶ (or press the space bar): the music starts with the picture. To… Do this Play or pause ▶ or the Space bar Go to a precise moment Drag the slider; the small markers show where each scene starts Step forward or back one frame → or ← Jump to the next or previous scene Shift + → or Shift + ← Replay a scene in a loop B ( B again to stop) The label on the bar shows the current scene: « Scène 3/6 · maillons » (Scene 3/6 · links). Note the number of any scene you want to edit. Expected result: the video plays right to the end, with music, and finishes on a still frame. I can’t hear the music Browsers wait for you to do something before they play any sound: click ▶. Also check the volume of your computer and of the tab. I closed the preview tab Just ask « rouvre l'aperçu » (“reopen the preview”) in Claude Code, or run /pulse-motion:reprendre ."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"retouche","t":"First video","s":"A first edit","x":"Goal: change something with a single sentence, then go back. Let’s say the list of the four links scrolls by too fast for your trainees: /pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 “slow down scene 3” /pulse-motion:retoucher ralentis la scène 3 pulse-motion historique sauvegarder . ✅ Fait : la scène 3, « Les 4 maillons de la chaîne », se déroule plus lentement. L'aperçu s'est rechargé. In English You: /pulse-motion:retoucher slow down scene 3 Claude: ✅ Done: scene 3, “The 4 links in the chain”, now plays more slowly. The preview has reloaded. Look at the preview: it has reloaded by itself, at the same position. If you don’t like the result: /pulse-motion:retoucher annule “undo” Tip: more sentence ideas are on the /pulse-motion:retoucher page: « change le titre » (“change the title”), « plus calme » (“calmer”), « ajoute des confettis à la fin » (“add confetti at the end”)… Expected result: scene 3 lasts longer in the preview; after « annule » (“undo”), it goes back to its previous pace."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"export","t":"First video","s":"Export as MP4","x":"Goal: get the MP4 file to share with your trainees. /pulse-motion:exporter Pulse Motion checks the video one last time, then looks for ffmpeg . If it is not installed, it offers to install it: it is a small piece of free software that builds the video in high quality, with clean sound. Accept (recommended), or choose to export in the browser, without installing anything. /pulse-motion:exporter → Installer ffmpeg (Recommandé) / Exporter sans, dans le navigateur Installer ffmpeg pulse-motion exporter . ✅ Fait : la vidéo « La chaîne du froid » est exportée en MP4 (1 min 03, avec le son, export fidèle). In English You: /pulse-motion:exporter Claude: → Install ffmpeg (Recommended) / Export without it, in the browser You: Install ffmpeg Claude: ✅ Done: the video “The cold chain” has been exported as an MP4 (1 min 03 s, with sound, high-fidelity export). The high-fidelity export takes about two seconds of processing per second of video: one minute of video, two minutes of waiting. Expected result: the sortie/ folder contains la-chaine-du-froid.mp4 , next to the animated page la-chaine-du-froid.html . Double-click the MP4: it plays in your computer’s video player. The ffmpeg installation failed Pulse Motion then exports in the browser, without installing anything. The MP4 is there; you can install ffmpeg later by following the requirements ."},{"u":"en/tutoriels/premiere-video.html","a":"ensuite","t":"First video","s":"What next?","x":"You have been through the whole journey: describe, approve, watch, edit, export. You now have two files: the MP4 , to send, publish, or upload to your training platform; the animated page , which opens in any browser, even offline, and updates with every edit. To pick this video up again another day, reopen the chaine-du-froid folder in VS Code. Pulse Motion reminds you of the video in progress; type /pulse-motion:reprendre . Tutorial 2: in your colours, with your voice (in French) →"}];
